window.__DOCS_EN__ = {"GOV-00":{"title":"Library Charter and Scope","content":"# Library Charter and Scope\n\n## 1. Purpose\nThis library provides **verifiable teaching cases, working templates and regulatory/standards references** for quality management of apparel, home textiles, and materials and trims sold in the US market. It serves three uses:\n1. Training quality teams and onboarding new staff;\n2. Quickly finding similar failure modes during pre-production risk reviews;\n3. Interview preparation for quality roles (subject to the [GOV-08 Interview Use Policy](doc:GOV-08)).\n\n## 2. What it is not\n- **Not legal advice.** Regulation entries are a working reference; formal determinations rely on the official text, customer manuals and legal counsel.\n- **Not a record of real events.** Unless `provenance` is `anonymized-real` with archived evidence, every case is an illustrative case and its numbers are teaching data of a realistic magnitude.\n- **Not a customer standard.** \"Customer requirements\" in cases are examples; actual acceptance follows the contract.\n\n## 3. Design principles\n| Principle | In practice |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Single source of truth** | Standards, regulations, metric definitions and taxonomy are maintained once in `registry/`; a case's numbers are maintained once in `case.json`, and the narrative and cards reference them |\n| **Executable rules** | Governance rules are written as validator rules wherever possible (`npm run validate`); human review handles only what a machine cannot judge |\n| **Three kinds of requirements kept apart** | Legal requirements, customer/retailer requirements and industry practice are written separately and never blurred into a vague \"US standard limit\" |\n| **Loss and disposition are mandatory** | Every quality incident explains the disposition of inventory and work in process and the basis of the loss; no \"perfect rescue\" stories |\n| **Evidence first** | Regulation and standard entries carry their source and verification date; unverified entries are marked `verifiedAt: null` rather than guessed |\n| **Traceable change** | Content carries version numbers, review status and a changelog |\n\n## 4. Scope\n- **Market**: United States (federal plus major state laws). Other markets require a new `jurisdiction` before inclusion.\n- **Categories**: apparel, home textiles, fabrics and trims, supply-chain systems.\n- **Stages**: pre-production/development, in production, shipment/arrival, post-market (defined in `registry/taxonomy.json`).\n- **Language**: this is the English edition. Every file is written in English; the validator rejects Chinese characters and full-width punctuation in any file. It was translated from the Chinese edition 2.0.2 (cases at 2.1.0); see the README for how the two editions relate.\n\n## 5. Governance documents\n| No. | Document | Question it answers |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| GOV-00 | This charter | Purpose, boundaries, principles |\n| GOV-01 | [Roles and Review Workflow](doc:GOV-01) | Who writes, who reviews, who approves; how status moves |\n| GOV-02 | [Case Writing Standard](doc:GOV-02) | Case structure, writing rules, banned wording |\n| GOV-03 | [Metrics and Data Definitions](doc:GOV-03) | How return rate, FTR, COPQ, Cpk and others are calculated |\n| GOV-04 | [Source and Evidence Policy](doc:GOV-04) | Source hierarchy for regulations and standards, verification and expiry |\n| GOV-05 | [Provenance and Confidentiality](doc:GOV-05) | Illustrative vs composite vs real cases, and de-identification |\n| GOV-06 | [Review Checklist](doc:GOV-06) | Technical, compliance, numbers and consistency checks |\n| GOV-07 | [Change Control and Versioning](doc:GOV-07) | Version numbers, regulatory change triggers, retirement |\n| GOV-08 | [Interview Use Policy](doc:GOV-08) | How to use cases legitimately for interview preparation |\n| GOV-09 | [Taxonomy and Registries](doc:GOV-09) | Maintenance rules for the taxonomy, standards, regulations and metrics registries |\n| GOV-10 | [Independent Audit](doc:GOV-10) | Role, authority, severity levels and handling of findings for the audit agent |\n\n## 6. Success criteria\n- Zero validator errors; warnings decline quarter over quarter;\n- Every entry in the regulations registry has an owner, and `verifiedAt` is no older than 180 days;\n- The share of cases moving from `draft` to `approved` rises each quarter;\n- The share of training and review users who report the material is \"directly usable at work\".\n"},"GOV-01":{"title":"Roles and Review Workflow","content":"# Roles and Review Workflow\n\n## 1. Roles\n\n| Role | Responsibilities | Qualification |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Library Owner** | Accountable for overall library quality; approves governance changes; chairs the quarterly health review | Quality manager or above |\n| **Author** | Writes cases and templates per [GOV-02](doc:GOV-02); runs the validator until there are zero errors | Any quality staff member |\n| **Technical Reviewer** | Reviews mechanisms, methods, plausibility of numbers and internal consistency | 5+ years in the relevant category/process; may not review their own case |\n| **Compliance Reviewer** | Reviews regulatory citations and claims (fiber content, origin, flammability, down, environmental) and whether legal and customer requirements are confused | Compliance or legal background |\n| **Registry Steward** | Maintains the standards and regulations registries; verifies on schedule; handles regulatory change | Compliance lead or their designee |\n\n## 2. RACI\n\n| Activity | Author | Technical Reviewer | Compliance Reviewer | Registry Steward | Library Owner |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| Writing a new case | R | C | C | I | A |\n| Technical review | I | R | I | I | A |\n| Compliance review (cases involving regulations or claims) | I | I | R | C | A |\n| Standards/regulations registry updates | I | C | C | R | A |\n| Governance document changes | C | C | C | C | R/A |\n| Quarterly health review | I | C | C | C | R/A |\n\n## 3. Case status flow\n\n```mermaid\nstateDiagram-v2\n    [*] --> draft\n    draft --> technical_review: author submits (zero validator errors)\n    technical_review --> draft: returned for changes\n    technical_review --> compliance_review: involves regulations/claims\n    technical_review --> approved: no regulations/claims\n    compliance_review --> draft: returned for changes\n    compliance_review --> approved\n    approved --> draft: major change\n    approved --> retired: outdated or superseded\n```\n\n- Status lives in `review.status` in `case.json`; reviewers and dates go in the `review` field, and review comments are filed in the case folder using `templates/review-record.md` (`review-YYYYMMDD.md`).\n- **Hard conditions for approved**: zero validator errors; the reviewer is not the author; cases citing regulations have a compliance reviewer; every cited regulation entry has a `verifiedAt` within 180 days. The validator checks the machine-checkable parts of the first three.\n- Changes after `approved`: typos and formatting may be fixed directly; any change to a number, conclusion or regulatory citation sends the case back to `draft`.\n\n## 4. Turnaround\n| Item | Deadline |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Technical review | 10 business days after submission |\n| Compliance review | 10 business days after submission |\n| Regulatory change (new rule, amendment, rescission) | Registry updated within 5 business days; affected cases re-reviewed within 30 days |\n| Validator warnings | Cleared, or kept with a stated reason, at the quarterly review |\n\n## 5. Disputes\nWhen the technical reviewer and the author disagree, the Library Owner appoints a second reviewer. On compliance questions the compliance reviewer's opinion prevails; escalate to legal when needed.\n"},"GOV-02":{"title":"Case Writing Standard","content":"# Case Writing Standard\n\n## 1. File structure\nEach case is a folder: `cases/<prefix>/<ID>_<slug>/`\n\n| File | Content | Maintained |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `case.json` | Structured facts: classification, order, metrics, loss ledger, disposition, standard/regulation citations, review status, provenance | By hand; the **single source** of numbers |\n| `case.md` | Narrative: background, process, mechanism, actions, STAR | By hand; numbers must match `case.json` |\n| `review-YYYYMMDD.md` | Review record (optional; required once approved) | Per `templates/review-record.md` |\n\n- ID rules: `APP-` apparel, `HT-` home textiles, `RM-` materials and trims, `SYS-` systems; three-digit sequence numbers, **never reused**. A retired case keeps its ID and its status becomes `retired`.\n- Case titles carry no adjectives such as \"classic\", \"major\" or \"outstanding\" and never name a real brand.\n\n## 2. Required content (by case type)\nThe validator checks the narrative for the section keywords below, per `requiredMarkers` in `registry/taxonomy.json`. A keyword counts when it appears in a heading or in bold text.\n\n| Type | Required sections | Required structured fields |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `incident` quality incident | Background, Root Cause, Disposition, Verification, STAR | `metrics`, `ledger`, `disposition` |\n| `gate` pre-production gate | Background, Root Cause, Verification, Gate, STAR | `metrics` |\n| `ci` continuous improvement | Background, Root Cause, Verification, STAR | `metrics` |\n| `system` system building | Background, STAR | `metrics` |\n\nTemplates are in `templates/case-incident/` and `templates/case-short/`.\n\n## 3. Writing rules\n\n### 3.1 State the source of every requirement\nEvery acceptance value must say which kind it is: **federal/state regulation** (cite the registry ID), **customer/retailer requirement**, **industry practice** or **internal target**. Vague phrases such as \"US standard limit\" or \"US standard requirement\" are prohibited.\n\n### 3.2 Method citations\n- Only methods present in `registry/standards.json` may be cited; entries with `status: withdrawn` or `kind: terminology` cannot be used as acceptance methods (they may be mentioned in a historical note).\n- After the method name, state the conditions: number of launderings, specimen direction and location, light source, counting rule.\n\n### 3.3 Numbers\n- Every before/after value in `case.json.metrics` must appear in the narrative with the same value (checked by the validator). Large amounts may be written as `1.55M`, `1.55 million` or `1,550,000`; round thousands may be written as `25K`.\n- State the basis of money amounts: `FOB`, `actual cost`, `actual spend` or `net loss` (the allowed values of `ledger.basis`). Ledger items must add up to the total (1% tolerance).\n- Give numerator and denominator for rates; for small samples write the counts (e.g. 5/12), not a percentage the sample cannot support.\n- Distinguish \"total return rate\" from \"quality return rate\" per [GOV-03](doc:GOV-03).\n- Cpk is used only for continuous metrics with `cpkAllowed: true` in `registry/metrics.json`, stating the number of subgroups and the MSA result.\n\n### 3.4 Disposition and loss (mandatory for incidents)\n- State the decision for every portion of goods (in stock, in transit, in production, fabric and trims): release, rework, reprocess, redeploy, downgrade, return, reject, recycle, scrap, recondition under supervision, redesign;\n- State **why rework is not possible** where it is not (e.g. crosslinking damage or oxidative degradation is irreversible), to avoid \"we changed the process, so the existing goods were fine\";\n- State who bears the loss and on what basis (contract, liability split).\n\n### 3.5 Timeline\nGive approximate times for key milestones (e.g. \"released about 7 weeks later\", \"re-weaving took about 10 weeks\"). Times must fit the process lead times; never write \"all issues resolved within two weeks\".\n\n### 3.6 Wording\nBanned wording in `registry/editorial-rules.json` is blocked by the validator, e.g. \"zero complaints\", \"100% pass\", \"completely eliminated\", \"world-class\". Write results as \"over × weeks of observation, the quality return rate was x%\".\n\n### 3.7 STAR\nEvery case ends with STAR talking points. They use only numbers already in the case and add none; the Result states both the cost (losses, delays) and the improvement, not only the positive outcome.\n\n### 3.8 Language\nThe English edition is written entirely in English (US spelling and units, with metric units where the method specifies them). Chinese characters and full-width punctuation are rejected by the validator in every file, including JSON, scripts and templates.\n\n## 4. Reference syntax in narratives\n| Syntax | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `[text](case:APP-001)` | Link to a case |\n| `[text](doc:GOV-03)` | Link to a governance document, template, SOP or reference |\n| `0.9` | Replaced at build time with the value from that case's `case.json` (used in interview and reference documents to avoid hand-copied numbers) |\n\nThe validator checks that every reference resolves.\n"},"GOV-03":{"title":"Metrics and Data Definitions","content":"# Metrics and Data Definitions\n\nThe single source of metric definitions is `registry/metrics.json`; this document explains the rules and common misuses. Register a new metric in the registry before using it in a case.\n\n## 1. Return metrics\n| Metric | Numerator | Denominator | Common misuse |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Total return rate | All returned units | Units sold | Apparel e-commerce is usually 15–30%; calling a figure like 0.3% a total return rate is not credible |\n| Quality return rate | Returns attributed to quality defects after **physical verification** | Units sold | Substituting customer-service free text or consumer check boxes for physical verification |\n| Vendor-attributable quality return rate | Returns attributed to that vendor's manufacturing defects | Units shipped by that vendor | Charging size, product-page or fulfillment reasons to the factory |\n| Size/color/fit (and similar) return rates | Returns with the matching reason code | Units sold | Writing \"x% of total returns\" and then comparing it as if it were a return rate |\n\n**Rule**: quality KPIs always use the quality return rate; the total return rate is context only, with the effect of size, preference and policy explained.\n\n## 2. FTR\n- **Audited basis**: first final-inspection results from brand QA, a third party or an unannounced audit, counted by lot. Used for performance and scorecards.\n- **Self-reported basis**: the supplier DQA's own results, for comparison only; the gap between the two is used to manage DQA, not as a performance result.\n\n## 3. Cost of quality\n- **COPQ (before recovery)** = internal failure cost + external failure cost.\n- **Net loss** = COPQ − supplier compensation actually recovered.\n- Recovery is a cost transfer, not a reduction in total supply-chain cost: report \"fewer failures\" and \"better recovery\" separately and deduct the new prevention/appraisal spend; also track FOB to catch suppliers pricing claims back into the product.\n- **Claim recovery rate** = recovered / supplier-liable claims confirmed and due; also report the amounts in dispute and their aging.\n\n## 4. Loss ledger (case.json → ledger)\n- `basis` is required and must be one of `FOB`, `actual cost`, `actual spend` or `net loss`.\n- Only losses that actually occurred are counted: products sold and not returned are not a loss; \"goods value\" is not \"money saved\".\n- The sum of `items` = `totalUsd` (1% tolerance).\n\n## 5. Process capability\n- Cpk is used only for continuous data from a stable process with an acceptable measurement system (%GRR generally < 10%; 10–30% must be explained) and ≥ 25 subgroups.\n- When the mean is outside the specification, Cpk is negative and must be reported as such.\n- Ordinal data (gray scales, pilling, smoothness) gets no Cpk; report the pass rate and grade distribution, and use Cohen's kappa for agreement.\n- With few destructive-test samples, release on \"every lot passes + minimum value\" first, and assess capability once enough data has accumulated.\n\n## 6. Small samples and rates\n- With a sample size < 30, give numerator and denominator (e.g. 1/16).\n- When before/after samples differ (e.g. 12 people vs 16 people), say so, and never imply statistical significance.\n\n## 7. Observation period\n- Post-launch improvements are observed for at least one full selling cycle (usually 8–12 weeks), stating whether promotions, channels and category mix are comparable.\n"},"GOV-04":{"title":"Source and Evidence Policy","content":"# Source and Evidence Policy\n\n## 1. Source hierarchy (highest to lowest)\n| Tier | Source | Use |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | Primary legal text: eCFR, Federal Register, state statutes | Final authority on regulatory provisions |\n| 2 | Official agency guidance and FAQs: CPSC, FTC, CBP, OEHHA | Scope and enforcement interpretation |\n| 3 | Standards-body catalogs and standard texts: AATCC, ASTM, ISO, NFPA, IDFB | Method names, status (active/withdrawn), method essentials |\n| 4 | Technical bulletins from accredited labs, industry association material | Practical interpretation; must be cross-checked against tiers 1–3 |\n| 5 | Media, blogs, supplier marketing | Leads only; never the sole basis |\n\n## 2. Registry entry requirements\n- `sources` has at least one link from tiers 1–3;\n- `verifiedAt`: the date the verifier confirmed the content against the source; use `null` if unverified, **never guess**;\n- Regulation entries unverified for more than 180 days trigger a validator warning; a regulation entry cited by a case must be verified before review;\n- Rescinded regulations (e.g. the former 16 CFR 253 down guides) keep their entry with `status: rescinded` and may only appear in a case's `historicalRegulations`.\n\n## 3. Facts in cases\n| Type of fact | Evidence requirement |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Regulatory/standard facts | Cite the registry ID; when the narrative disagrees with the registry, the registry prevails and the narrative is corrected |\n| Material/process mechanisms | Consistent with textbook or technical-literature understanding; contested mechanisms are labeled \"hypothesis\" with a verification test |\n| Numbers (illustrative cases) | Magnitudes must match common industry levels (e.g. a residential cut-pile carpet tuft bind floor of about 3 lbf, a total apparel e-commerce return rate of 15–30%); reviewers can veto clearly implausible magnitudes |\n| Numbers (real cases) | Must be backed by archived internal evidence (test reports, financial records), with the location recorded in the review record and kept out of the public library |\n\n## 4. Citation format\n- Registries: `sources` holds URLs;\n- Narratives: the regulation or standard number is enough (e.g. \"16 CFR 1632\", \"ASTM D7142\"); the registry carries the source link.\n\n## 5. When uncertain\nWrite \"verify against the current edition\" or \"per the customer manual\" rather than giving a value that looks precise but has not been verified.\n"},"GOV-05":{"title":"Provenance and Confidentiality","content":"# Provenance and Confidentiality\n\n## 1. Case provenance (case.json → provenance)\n| Value | Meaning | Requirement |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `illustrative` | Illustrative case: scenario and numbers written for teaching | Realistic magnitudes, internally consistent; the site shows an \"Illustrative case\" notice automatically |\n| `composite` | Composite case: several real experiences de-identified, merged and rewritten | Must not allow a specific company, order or person to be inferred; numbers adjusted |\n| `anonymized-real` | Anonymized real case | Requires written authorization from the information owner; evidence archived outside the library; the review record states the authorization and evidence location |\n\nAll current cases are `illustrative`.\n\n## 2. De-identification rules\n- No real retailer, brand, factory, lab or logistics company appears as the subject of an event (the validator raises an error for names on the `brandWatchlist`); use generic terms such as \"a large US retail chain\" or \"a third-party lab\".\n- No identifiable PO numbers, style numbers, date combinations, personal names, or city + factory-size combinations.\n- Equipment and materials may be described by generic specification (e.g. \"65/9 light ball-point needle\"); where a brand model is needed write \"a branded SES-type\", never as the subject of an event.\n- Real contract terms, prices and claim amounts must not enter the public library verbatim.\n\n## 3. External publication\n- Before public release (website, social media, course material), confirm that every case is `illustrative` or authorized;\n- Published material must keep the notice \"Illustrative case; does not correspond to a real event\".\n\n## 4. Personal information\nReturns and customer-service cases use aggregate data only and contain no consumer personal information; where an example is needed, use fictitious data.\n"},"GOV-06":{"title":"Review Checklist","content":"# Review Checklist\n\nBefore reviewing, run `npm run validate` and confirm zero errors; it is also recommended to run an independent audit with `/audit-cases <ID>` (see [GOV-10](doc:GOV-10)) and resolve its Critical and Major findings. Items marked 🤖 are checked automatically by the validator and only need confirmation; the rest require human judgment.\n\n## A. Technical review\n- [ ] The failure mechanism is physically/chemically sound; no errors such as \"reflectance changes with the light source\"\n- [ ] The root cause is supported by a **verification test** (comparison test, stratified retest), not reasoning alone\n- [ ] The method is correct and its conditions are stated (number of launderings, direction, light source, counting rule) 🤖 method exists and is not withdrawn\n- [ ] The actions address the root cause, and side effects were assessed (e.g. retest shrinkage after lowering the heat-set temperature; retest slippage after adding softener)\n- [ ] The timeline fits process lead times (re-spinning, re-weaving or re-finishing take weeks)\n- [ ] The disposition of goods already produced is sound: irreversible damage is not written up as \"passed after rework\" 🤖 incidents must have a disposition\n- [ ] Number magnitudes are credible (checked against the industry references in [GOV-04](doc:GOV-04))\n\n## B. Compliance review\n- [ ] Legal requirements, customer requirements and industry practice are clearly separated 🤖 blocks wording such as \"US standard limit\"\n- [ ] The cited regulations apply to the product (children's/adult, size range, product type, state of sale)\n- [ ] Cited regulation entries are verified and current 🤖 warning if unverified or older than 180 days\n- [ ] Claims (fiber content, thread count, origin, down, hypoallergenic, flame resistance, recycled, waterproof) are substantiated, and claims are changed when goods are downgraded\n- [ ] Children's products: third-party testing, CPC, retesting after material changes, tracking labels\n- [ ] Imports: UFLPA traceability, CPSC certificate eFiling\n- [ ] No real company is named as the subject of an event 🤖 brand watchlist\n\n## C. Numbers and definitions\n- [ ] Metrics use registry definitions 🤖 metric ID exists\n- [ ] Before/after metric values match the narrative 🤖\n- [ ] The loss ledger adds up and states its basis 🤖\n- [ ] Return rates distinguish total returns from quality reasons 🤖 distinct metric IDs\n- [ ] Cpk is used only for allowed metrics, with the number of subgroups and MSA stated 🤖 Cpk banned for ordinal metrics\n- [ ] Small samples give numerator and denominator\n\n## D. Consistency\n- [ ] Each fact appears in only one version across cases, templates, SOPs and interview material; other files reference it with `{{ID.path}}` or a link 🤖 references resolve\n- [ ] STAR adds no new numbers and states the cost\n- [ ] Title, classification and stage match the content 🤖 enum values valid\n- [ ] The text is entirely in English 🤖 no Chinese characters or full-width punctuation\n\n## Review conclusion\nRecord per `templates/review-record.md`: pass / conditional pass (list the required changes) / return.\n"},"GOV-07":{"title":"Change Control and Versioning","content":"# Change Control and Versioning\n\n## 1. Version numbers\n- **Library version** (`version` in `package.json` and `CHANGELOG.md`):\n  - Major: structure or schema change (e.g. the v1 → v2 folder and data-model restructuring);\n  - Minor: new cases, bulk registry updates, governance revisions;\n  - Patch: wording fixes, verification of a single registry entry.\n- **Case version** (`case.json → version`): a change to numbers, conclusions or regulatory citations bumps the minor version; wording fixes bump the patch.\n\n## 2. Change types and requirements\n| Change | Process triggered | Record |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| New case | Full GOV-01 process | CHANGELOG |\n| Changing numbers/conclusions of an approved case | Status returns to `draft` and the case is re-reviewed | CHANGELOG + review record |\n| Registry entry update | Changed by the Registry Steward after verification; `verifiedAt` updated | CHANGELOG |\n| Regulatory change (new rule/amendment/rescission) | Registry updated within 5 business days; the build report lists the cases citing the entry; re-review within 30 days | CHANGELOG + list of affected cases |\n| Standard withdrawn or replaced | Registry set to `withdrawn`; the validator errors wherever it is cited, and cases must be changed | CHANGELOG |\n| Governance document change | Approved by the Library Owner | CHANGELOG |\n\n## 3. Retirement\n- When a case becomes outdated (regulation changed, process obsolete) or is superseded by a better case, its status becomes `retired`; files and ID are kept and the site hides it by default;\n- The reason for retirement goes in `review.notes`.\n\n## 4. Generated files\nFiles under `dist/` are generated by `npm run build` and must not be edited by hand; rebuild after changing source files.\n\n## 5. History\nAll v1 content was migrated to the v2 structure; the v1 files and one-off migration scripts were deleted in 2.0.1. From then on, history is traced through version control (git is recommended) and the CHANGELOG.\n\n## 6. Language editions\n- This English edition was translated from the Chinese edition 2.0.2 (cases at 2.1.0). IDs, numbers, registry keys and validation rules are identical in both editions.\n- The two editions are maintained as separate libraries. A change to facts, numbers, conclusions or regulatory citations in one edition should be mirrored in the other within the same review cycle, and each CHANGELOG entry names the corresponding version of the other edition.\n- Audit reports are not translated: each edition keeps its own `audits/` history. The English edition's audit history starts empty.\n"},"GOV-08":{"title":"Interview Use Policy","content":"# Interview Use Policy\n\n## 1. Basic principle\nIllustrative cases are **practice material** for learning structure, mechanisms and disposition logic. They **must not be told as your own experience**. Presenting a fictional experience as your own is résumé/interview fraud; it is easily exposed under follow-up questions about details (factory, timing, colleagues, evidence), and it breaches professional ethics.\n\n## 2. Proper use\n1. **Learn the structure**: use the STAR structure of the cases to organize your own real experience;\n2. **Learn the mechanisms**: use the root causes and verification methods in the cases to check whether your own story holds up under questioning;\n3. **Learn disposition and cost**: interviewers most often ask \"what happened to the original lot?\" and \"how much was lost, and who paid?\"; the cases show how to answer;\n4. **Discussion questions**: in an interview you can say \"I have studied a similar scenario; my approach would be…\", making clear it is analysis, not first-hand experience.\n\n## 3. When telling your own experience\n- Use real numbers you can substantiate, or say \"about\" or \"on the order of\";\n- Distinguish what you did yourself from what the team did;\n- Naming the cost and what did not go well is more credible than a \"perfect rescue\";\n- Do not disclose a former employer's confidential information (customer names, prices, claim amounts); de-identify as described in [GOV-05](doc:GOV-05).\n\n## 4. Interview material in this library\n- [STAR index](doc:INT-STAR-INDEX): entry point to every case's STAR talking points;\n- [Common questions](doc:INT-QA-BANK): answer frameworks and regulatory essentials;\n- [Cross-functional conflict scenarios](doc:INT-CONFLICT): scenario practice.\n"},"GOV-09":{"title":"Taxonomy and Registries","content":"# Taxonomy and Registries\n\n## 1. Registries at a glance\n| File | Content | Maintained by | How the validator uses it |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `registry/taxonomy.json` | Domains, stages, case types, failure modes, disciplines, tools, disposition decisions, provenance, review statuses | Library Owner | Case fields must take their values from these enums |\n| `registry/standards.json` | Test methods and standards: organization, code, status, kind, verification date | Registry Steward | Errors when a withdrawn or terminology standard is cited as a method |\n| `registry/regulations.json` | Regulations: jurisdiction, agency, citation, key requirements, status, sources, verification date | Registry Steward (compliance) | Rescinded regulations may only be cited historically; unverified/expired entries trigger warnings |\n| `registry/metrics.json` | Metric definitions: unit, type, direction, whether Cpk is allowed | Library Owner | Metric IDs must exist; Cpk is banned for ordinal metrics |\n| `registry/editorial-rules.json` | Banned wording, brand watchlist (names and context patterns), number-check levels, language policy | Library Owner | Narrative scan; scan of every file for Chinese characters |\n\n## 2. Classification dimensions\nEach case carries all of the following tags, used for search and statistics:\n- **Domain** `domain`: apparel / home textiles / materials and trims / systems and supply chain;\n- **Stage** `stage`: where the problem was detected (pre-production/development, in production, shipment/arrival, post-market), not where the root cause occurred;\n- **Type** `caseType`: quality incident, pre-production gate, continuous improvement, system building;\n- **Failure modes** `failureModes` (multiple allowed);\n- **Disciplines** `disciplines` (multiple allowed);\n- **Tools** `tools` (multiple allowed).\n\n## 3. Adding enum values\n1. First confirm no existing value covers it;\n2. Add it to the taxonomy with a clear English label and definition;\n3. Record it in the CHANGELOG;\n4. One concept may not have two IDs (e.g. `seam-burst` and `seam-failure`).\n\n## 4. Adding and verifying registry entries\n- A new standard/regulation needs at least: code, title, kind/status and a source link; `verifiedAt` is filled in only after checking against the source;\n- At each quarterly review, export the \"unverified / about to expire\" list (shown directly on the site's Governance Health page), and the steward works through it entry by entry.\n\n## 5. Brand watchlist in English\nSome retailer names are also ordinary English words (\"Target\", \"Gap\"). These are listed under `brandWatchlist.patterns` and matched only in a retail context (e.g. \"Target stores\", \"Gap Inc.\"), so that headings such as \"Target\" in a metrics table are not flagged. Plain names in `brandWatchlist.names` are matched as whole words.\n"},"GOV-10":{"title":"Independent Audit","content":"# Independent Audit\n\n## 1. Positioning\nThe independent audit and the review in [GOV-01](doc:GOV-01) are two different gates:\n\n| | Review (GOV-01) | Independent audit (this document) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Purpose | Decide whether a case can be approved | Find what is unreasonable or does not hold up |\n| Performed by | The designated technical/compliance reviewers | The audit agent `case-auditor`, or a senior person not involved in writing or reviewing the case |\n| Output | Review record and conclusion | Audit report (findings only; no file changes, no rewriting) |\n| When | Before each case is approved | Pre-review before approval; quarterly sampling; full audit before external publication; targeted audit after regulatory change |\n\nThe auditor only finds problems; the author makes the changes and the case is re-reviewed under GOV-01.\n\n## 2. Audit agent\n- Definition file: `.claude/agents/case-auditor.md`\n- Role: senior, strict, calm, objective; runs the validator first and reports only what a machine cannot catch; every finding has a location, quoted text, basis, severity and confidence; challenges its own findings before reporting to reduce false positives.\n- Permissions: read-only (read files, search, run `npm run validate` / `npm test`, check official sources online); must not modify files or run `npm run build`.\n- Invocation: in Claude Code run `/audit-cases <scope>`, where the scope is a case ID (e.g. `APP-001`), a prefix (e.g. `HT`) or `all`. The report is saved to `audits/AUDIT-YYYYMMDD-<scope>.md`.\n\n## 3. Severity\n| Severity | Definition | Deadline |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Critical | A regulatory fact or applicability is wrong; or an error large enough to overturn the core conclusion | Case returns to `draft` immediately and may not be used externally until fixed |\n| Major | Wrong mechanism, contradictory or implausible numbers, unsound disposition, missing key verification | Fixed within 30 days; may not be approved until fixed |\n| Minor | Unclear basis, units or conditions; minor inconsistency | Handled at the next revision |\n| Observation | Would increase credibility if added | At the author's discretion |\n\n## 4. Handling findings\n1. The author gives a result for every finding: fixed / disagree (with reasons) / need more information;\n2. When the author and the auditor disagree, the Library Owner appoints a second senior person to decide;\n3. When the same kind of problem recurs across cases, the Library Owner assesses whether to turn it into a validator rule or a GOV-02 rule so it is caught automatically in future;\n4. Audit reports and their responses stay in `audits/` and are never deleted.\n\n## 5. Requirements for the audit itself\n- Every finding must be checkable: location, quoted text and basis;\n- Uncertain items are marked \"needs verification\" with the verification method; impressions are never written as conclusions;\n- Style issues are not reported to pad the count; if there is nothing to report, say \"no reportable issues found\";\n- The audit agent's conclusions can also be wrong. Critical and Major findings are spot-checked by a person before they are accepted.\n"},"PB-8D":{"title":"8D Problem-Solving and Reporting Guide for Textiles, Home Textiles and Apparel","content":"# 8D Problem-Solving and Reporting Guide for Textiles, Home Textiles and Apparel\n\n> **Standard 8D problem-solving report guide tailored for US apparel and home textiles**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Where 8D fits in US-market quality management\n\nIn US retail and brand business, the following events should trigger an 8D:\n1. **Third-party lab test failure**: regulatory items (lead, flammability, drawstrings) or key performance items (shrinkage, pilling, strength).\n2. **Bulk final inspection rejection**: lot-wide shade variation, seam failure, broken needles, wrong packaging barcodes, etc.\n3. **DC receiving rejection, port detention or a failed CPSC sample**.\n4. **Consumer complaints or an abnormal rise in physically verified quality returns**.\n\n**What success looks like for an 8D**: contain the loss, find a verifiable root cause, and prevent recurrence. **It is not \"rescuing the whole order\"**: goods that already carry physical damage usually cannot be repaired, and the disposition decision is itself a core part of the 8D.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Standard 8D process\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    D1[D1: Form a cross-functional team] --> D2[D2: Problem description 5W2H]\n    D2 --> D3[D3: Interim containment ICA + disposition of affected inventory]\n    D3 --> D4[D4: Root cause analysis 5-Why / fishbone / verification tests]\n    D4 --> D5[D5: Permanent corrective actions PCA, verified by test]\n    D5 --> D6[D6: Implementation and effectiveness verification]\n    D6 --> D7[D7: Prevent recurrence and standardize]\n    D7 --> D8[D8: Summary and closure]\n```\n\n**Timing (reference)**: D1–D3 within 24–72 h; D4 root cause confirmed by test within 7–10 days; D6 effectiveness is judged on \"data from consecutive production lots after the action\", which usually takes 4–8 weeks. An 8D should not be closed before the effectiveness data is in.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Sample 8D report\n\n### Report header\n> Note: this sample is a standalone teaching scenario (high-count percale bedding). Its mechanism is similar to [HT-001](case:HT-001) in the case library (400TC sateen bedding), but it is not the same event; orders, numbers and SOP numbers are not shared.\n\n- **Report no.**: `8D-2026-TXT-008`\n- **Product / style**: high-count percale bedding set #PC-8802 (duvet cover, flat sheet, fitted sheet, pillowcases)\n- **Customer**: US Luxury Home Specialty Retailer\n- **Supplier**: an integrated weaving, dyeing and sewing mill in Jiangsu (example)\n- **Order**: PO #984210 / 25,000 sets / FOB $50 per set, goods value $1,250,000\n- **Opened / root cause confirmed / closed**: 2026-08-08 / 2026-08-17 / 2026-10-20 (effectiveness verified on 3 new production lots before closure)\n\n---\n\n### D1: Team\n- **Lead**: Senior QA Manager (customer communication, cross-functional decisions)\n- **Members**: fabric process and dyeing/finishing engineer, factory technical manager, Sourcing Manager, compliance specialist, finance (COPQ and claim accounting), third-party lab technical support\n\n---\n\n### D2: Problem description (5W2H)\n- **What**: seam slippage openings at duvet cover and pillowcase seams; insufficient fabric tear strength, with holes at stressed hem folds after 3 launderings.\n- **Where**: sampling by the customer's designated third-party lab: ASTM D1424 tear strength; load to reach a 1/4 in (6 mm) opening under the customer's finished-seam method (referencing ASTM D1683).\n- **When**: 2026-08-08, first bulk test report.\n- **Who / scope**: the first lot of 10,000 sets was already received into the US DC; the second lot of 15,000 sets was packed at the factory awaiting shipment. Both came from the same finishing process, 7 finishing lots in total.\n- **How much**: tear strength warp 1.8 / weft 1.5 lbf (customer requirement ≥ 3.0 lbf); seam 1/4 in opening load 6.2 lbf (requirement ≥ 15 lbf); AATCC 124 durable press appearance grade 4.0 (customer requirement ≥ 4.0).\n- **Severity**: functional failure (S=8), no safety-regulation issue.\n\n---\n\n### D3: Interim containment and inventory disposition\n1. **System hold**: the US DC placed a WMS hold on the 10,000 sets, blocking transfers to stores and the e-commerce warehouse.\n2. **Factory quarantine**: the 15,000 sets were red-tagged and quarantined; cutting was suspended on later POs using the same fabric (#984211/984212).\n3. **Stratified retest by lot**: retained samples from all 7 finishing lots were tested for tear and seam; tear ranged from 1.5 to 2.3 lbf and **every lot failed**, showing the problem was not an isolated lot.\n4. **Effectiveness check**: WMS and factory counts were reconciled, confirming all 25,000 sets were under control and none had reached consumers.\n5. **Disposition decision** (signed jointly by the customer, Sourcing, Finance and QA):\n   - **Why rework is not possible**: unpicking and widening the seam allowance could fix seam slippage, but the low tear strength is fiber damage caused by the finishing process and **cannot be restored by rework**.\n   - **10,000 sets at the US DC**: the customer rejected them and deducted payment per the contract; the goods had brand identification removed and were recycled under third-party supervision, with a recycling/destruction certificate, so nothing entered the secondary market under the brand name.\n   - **15,000 sets at the factory**: brand removed and sold as seconds to a liquidation buyer (about $6 per set recovered), relabeled for fiber content and origin per FTC requirements; 15,000 sets were remade with fabric from the new process, and the customer accepted a 5-week delivery extension.\n   - **Loss accounting (filed at D8)**: deduction $500,000 + disposal and logistics $32,000 + remake cost about $525,000 − seconds recovery $90,000 ≈ **$967,000**. Under the liability agreement, the factory bears it because it changed the finishing process without approval.\n\n---\n\n### D4: Root cause analysis\n\n#### 1. Fishbone (6M)\n- **Man**: was the finishing department following the approved process card?\n- **Machine**: actual fabric-surface temperature in the curing range, line speed, oven temperature uniformity.\n- **Material**: yarn strength and variation, greige construction.\n- **Method**: resin concentration, catalyst, curing temperature and time, softener type.\n- **Measurement**: specimen direction, conditioning (ASTM D1776), inter-lab comparison.\n- **Milieu (environment)**: temperature and humidity in finishing, greige storage.\n\n#### 2. Verification test (not reasoning alone)\n- The same greige was finished once with the sampling process card and once with the actual bulk parameters: tear strength loss was 58% with bulk parameters and 30% with sampling parameters, matching the bulk results.\n- Fabric-surface temperature logs showed bulk curing at 170 °C, 12 °C above the approved 158 °C.\n\n#### 3. 5-Why\n- **Why 1**: why did tear strength and seam slippage fail? Excessive resin crosslinking degraded the cellulose, so yarns could not move and bunch in the tear triangle; at the same time the softener made yarn surfaces too slick, and seam SPI and seam allowance were insufficient.\n- **Why 2**: why was crosslinking excessive? To reach DP 4.0, resin was dosed at 80 g/L and cured at 170 °C.\n- **Why 3**: why was bulk hotter than sampling? Bulk moved to a high-speed continuous curing range, and temperature and speed were raised to meet the delivery date.\n- **Why 4**: why was the change not caught? The parameter change did not go through an ECN, and full physical testing was not done before cutting.\n- **Why 5 (system root cause)**: durable press finishing was not defined as a special process; there was no \"locked process card + fabric release green card\" mechanism.\n\n---\n\n### D5: Permanent corrective actions (implemented only after test verification)\n1. **Process and formulation**: low-formaldehyde modified resin 80 → 45 g/L; curing at 158 °C, written into the machine recipe with an interlock; polyethylene softener added to improve yarn mobility (silicone dosage controlled to avoid worsening seam slippage).\n2. **Customer specification change**: tests showed that with this weave and yarn count, DP 4.0 and tear ≥ 3.0 lbf cannot both be met. After review by the customer's technical department, the specification was changed in writing to DP ≥ 3.5. This was a customer-approved specification change, not a unilateral QA concession.\n3. **Sewing**: seam allowance widened from 1/4 in to 1/2 in; duvet cover perimeters and pillowcases changed to overlock plus double-needle topstitching, with SPI raised from 10 to 12.\n4. **Test results** (300 m of trial fabric, 60 sample sets, third-party lab): tear warp 4.2 / weft 3.8 lbf; 1/4 in opening load 24 lbf; DP grade 3.5–3.8; formaldehyde within the customer RSL.\n\n---\n\n### D6: Implementation and effectiveness verification\n- The new process was applied to the 15,000 remade sets and the first 3 production lots of later POs (7 finishing lots in total): every finishing lot was tested for tear and seam strength and all passed. Minimum tear values were warp 3.9 / weft 3.5 lbf.\n- **Capability assessment**: with data from only 7 finishing lots, **a credible Cpk cannot yet be calculated**; release is on \"every lot passes + mean/minimum value\", and process capability will be assessed after 25 or more finishing lots.\n- After replenishment reached stores, 8 weeks of tracking: after physical verification, no returns related to tears or seam openings occurred on this style.\n\n---\n\n### D7: Prevent recurrence and standardize\n1. Update the \"Durable Press Finishing Process Control SOP for High-Count, High-Density Home Textiles\" (SOP-QA-RM-042): resin concentration, curing temperature and line speed are special-process parameters; any change goes through an ECN and full physical retesting.\n2. Fabric release green card: key fabrics such as durable press and coated fabrics are tested for tear, seam strength, DP and formaldehyde on **every finishing lot** (lot-level sample testing, not \"inspecting every piece\") before cutting is released.\n3. Add the failure mode \"strength loss from durable press finishing\" to the PFMEA; check similar styles across the range.\n\n---\n\n### D8: Summary and closure\n- Closed on 2026-10-20 after effectiveness verification was complete.\n- The COPQ for this event was about $967,000, borne by the responsible factory per the agreement; the customer relationship was retained and later POs resumed.\n- Lessons learned were added to the US home-textile quality case library; the finishing department head and QA were trained on ECNs and special processes.\n- **Approved by**: QA Director, 2026-10-20\n"},"PB-QCP":{"title":"End-to-End Quality Control Plan (QCP) for Apparel and Home Textiles","content":"# End-to-End Quality Control Plan (QCP) for Apparel and Home Textiles\n\n> **End-to-end quality control plan (QCP) standard template: from yarn/greige to finished-goods AQL**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview of the quality control plan (QCP)\n\nThe **quality control plan (QCP)** is the core operating document that turns product design specifications (tech pack), physical and chemical standards (AATCC/ASTM/CPSC) and process risks (PFMEA) into **specific monitoring actions on the shop floor**.\nThis template covers closed-loop control across every operation, from **incoming material inspection (IQC) → in-process patrol inspection in cutting and sewing (IPQC) → final shipment inspection (FQC/AQL)**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. QCP architecture and operation control matrix\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    M1[1. Fabric and trims IQC<br>4-point system / lab test] --> M2[2. Cutting and cut-part prep<br>relaxing / first-piece check / stripe and plaid matching]\n    M2 --> M3[3. Sewing room IPQC<br>line first piece / patrol / pull tests]\n    M3 --> M4[4. Finishing / washing / pressing<br>wash hand-feel / pH neutralization / measurements]\n    M4 --> M5[5. Needle detection and safety<br>nine-point check at customer sensitivity]\n    M5 --> M6[6. Final shipment FQC / AQL<br>ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 Level II sampling]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Standard end-to-end control plan table\n\n### Document metadata\n- **Product category**: high-spec US-market woven/knit apparel and home-textile bedding\n- **Applicable standards**: ASTM / AATCC / 16 CFR / ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 (Level II, Major 2.5, Minor 4.0, Critical 0)\n\n| Op. no. | Process step | Key characteristic (CTQ / parameter) | Specification and requirement | Method / tool | Frequency | Owner | Reaction plan |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **01** | **Fabric receiving inspection (IQC)** | 1. Visual defects<br>2. Width and weight<br>3. Side-to-center / end-to-end shade variation | 1. ASTM D5430 4-point system, on the common basis in the [Raw Material SOP](doc:SOP-FABRIC): regular lots average ≤ 28 and single rolls ≤ 40 points/100 sq yd (premium 20/28), stricter where the customer is stricter<br>2. Weight ±3–5% (per customer); width no less than the usable cutting width<br>3. Side-to-center/end-to-end shade by AATCC EP9 visual assessment (gray scale grade ≥ 4) or instrumental ΔE | Fabric inspection machine / light booth (customer-specified illuminant) / electronic balance | At least 10% of rolls per lot and no fewer than the customer-specified number; tightened for new suppliers or high-risk fabrics | IQC fabric inspector | Points over the limit: quarantine and grade the whole lot; shade over the limit: ask the dye house for a shade band or return for reprocessing. |\n| **02** | **Fabric lab testing** | 1. Dimensional change after washing and leg twist<br>2. Colorfastness (laundering/crocking/perspiration)<br>3. Tear and burst strength<br>4. Formaldehyde/pH/banned azo dyes | 1. Shrinkage per customer (commonly −3% to +1% for wovens, −5% to +2% for knits); twist per customer<br>2. Laundering color change ≥ 4, staining ≥ 3–3.5, wet crocking per customer (commonly ≥ 2.5–3)<br>3. Strength meets the buyer's matrix<br>4. pH, formaldehyde and azo per the customer RSL (state the formaldehyde method: often ≤ 16–20 ppm for infants, ≤ 75 ppm for skin contact) | AATCC 135 / AATCC 8 / AATCC 61 / ASTM D1424 / AATCC 112 or ISO 14184-1 / EN ISO 14362 | One full test set per bulk dye lot | Accredited lab test engineer | No cutting on a failed test; issue a nonconformance report (NCR), retest jointly with QA and the dye house, and open an 8D. |\n| **03** | **Fabric relaxing and pre-shrinking (resting)** | Release internal stress from knitting/weaving and heat-setting, and elastic tension | 1. Wovens relaxed naturally ≥ 12 hours<br>2. High-stretch knits/spandex fabrics relaxed 24–48 hours<br>3. Record temperature and humidity | Timer / tag tracking / cut-part recovery test | Every roll tracked | Cutting supervisor / IPQC | The spreader interlocks until the relaxing time is met; unauthorized cutting is treated as a serious violation and the cut parts are sealed. |\n| **04** | **Spreading and cutting** | 1. Spreading tension and plaid matching<br>2. Size consistency between top and bottom plies<br>3. Clean cut edges, no fused edges | 1. Tension-free spreading, flat and wrinkle-free<br>2. Top-to-bottom ply deviation ≤ 1.0 mm<br>3. Stripe/plaid matching deviation ≤ 1.5 mm | Steel rule / template check / CNC automatic cutter monitoring | Compare the first and last ply of every spread; check once every 50 plies | Cutting-room inspector | Stop cutting immediately when cut parts are out of tolerance, destroy the nonconforming plies, and adjust blade speed and vacuum hold-down. |\n| **05** | **Sewing first-piece approval** | 1. Workmanship conforms to the tech pack<br>2. Key measurements<br>3. Stitch density (SPI) and thread tension | 1. Fully matches the approved tech-pack sample<br>2. Measurements within tech-pack tolerances<br>3. No skipped stitches, floating stitches or puckering; SPI as specified | Soft tape measure / approved-sample comparison / magnifier stitch counter | 3 first pieces confirmed at line start (bulk sewing only after a pass) | Line leader + QA patrol | No bulk sewing without QA's signed first-piece approval; correct templates and machines immediately on any measurement deviation. |\n| **06** | **In-line sewing patrol (IPQC)** | 1. Sewing defects (broken thread/skipped stitches/crooked seams)<br>2. Edge and seam allowance width<br>3. Pull tests on small parts/trims | 1. Even seam allowance, no open or missed seams<br>2. Never omit care labels/security labels<br>3. Buttons/snaps on children's wear pulled per the customer method (commonly 15 lbf held 10 s; prong snaps referencing ASTM D7142), both new and after laundering | Force gauge/tensile tester / visual patrol | 5 pieces per station every 2 hours; pull tests 10 per machine per shift | IPQC inspector | On a failed pull test, immediately quarantine everything that machine produced since its last check, and re-attach the trims. |\n| **07** | **Garment washing and finishing** | 1. Wash look and color<br>2. Neutralization of residual chemicals (pH)<br>3. Dryer temperature control | 1. Color matches the master sample<br>2. pH after washing stable at 5.5–7.5<br>3. Drying temperature ≤ 70 °C so elastane is not heat-damaged | pH meter / color booth / hand-feel reference card | pH measured on every wash load; color checked on the first batch of every load | Wash-room inspector | If pH is too acidic or alkaline, neutralize and rinse immediately with washing auxiliaries; if the hand is too harsh, adjust with a softener cycle. |\n| **08** | **Needle detection on every piece** | Detect ferromagnetic broken-needle fragments at the specified sensitivity (passing detection does not mean metal-free) | 1. Sensitivity per customer (commonly a 1.0–1.2 mm Fe sphere); the nine-point, multi-direction test card must alarm at every point<br>2. Automatic stop/reverse on alarm; styles with metal trims follow the alternative control route in [SYS-006](case:SYS-006) | Conveyor needle detector / test card | Every piece passes through; test-card checks before the shift, every 2 hours and after the shift; if a check fails, re-inspect everything since the last passing check | Dedicated certified needle-detection operator | Alarmed garments go into a dedicated locked quarantine box; a designated person searches for the needle with a handheld detector and logs it. |\n| **09** | **Final shipment inspection (FQC / final AQL)** | 1. Overall appearance defects and packaging<br>2. Full finished-goods measurements<br>3. Barcode and carton mark scanning | 1. Critical defects: reject if any is found in the sample (Ac = 0, triggering a full sort)<br>2. Major defects: AQL 2.5<br>3. Minor defects: AQL 4.0<br>4. Every scanned barcode must match | ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 Normal Level II single sampling / barcode scanner | Sample size from the table by lot size (e.g. 200 pieces for a 5,000-piece lot) | Dedicated QA inspector / customer-designated third party | AQL exceeded: the whole lot is rejected; the factory must sort and rework every piece and apply for re-inspection. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. QCP review and change control (ECN)\n\n1. **Triggers for a new version**:\n   - The customer changes the tech pack or tolerances, or a fabric/trim supplier changes;\n   - A major quality incident occurs in production (8D improvements must be fed back into the QCP);\n   - Production line equipment is upgraded or automated.\n2. **QCP sign-off and effectiveness**:\n   - It takes effect only when co-signed by the **QA Manager, Production Technical Director and Purchasing Manager**, and is distributed to IQC, IPQC, FQC and the boards of the cutting, sewing and finishing departments.\n"},"PB-DMAIC":{"title":"Lean Six Sigma DMAIC Project Template for Apparel and Textiles","content":"# Lean Six Sigma DMAIC Project Template for Apparel and Textiles\n\n> **DMAIC project charter and roadmap tailored for apparel and home-textile quality improvement**\n\n---\n\n## 1. When to use DMAIC\n\nDMAIC fits problems that are **long-standing, driven by several interacting factors, and hard to eliminate with a routine 8D**, such as dimensional variation after washing, dye-lot shade variation, pilling complaints, or a chronically low FTR. It demonstrates three capabilities:\n1. Data-driven decisions;\n2. Statistical and process capability analysis (MSA, Cp/Cpk, hypothesis testing, DOE);\n3. Translating quality metrics into financial results (COPQ).\n\n**Discipline**: Cpk is used only for continuous data from a stable process with an acceptable measurement system; when the mean is outside the specification, Cpk is negative and must be reported as such.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Project charter\n\n| Field | Content |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Project name** | Reduce length shrinkage and twist after washing in US-market knit fleece sweatshirts |\n| **Business context** | In 2025 Q3–Q4 the series (3 fabric mills, 2 garment factories) had a first-time-right final inspection rate (FTR) of 84.5%; about 60% of nonconformances related to post-wash dimensions or twist. The related COPQ from rework, retesting, air freight and customer chargebacks was about **$420,000 per year** (of which customer chargebacks and claims were $280,000). |\n| **Problem statement** | Fabric length shrinkage after washing (AATCC 135, 3 wash-and-dry cycles, customer spec −5.0% to +2.0%) had a mean of −4.0% and σ = 0.60%, **Cpk = 0.56**; garment twist (AATCC 179, customer spec ≤ 5.0%) had a mean of 3.9% and σ = 0.80%, **Cpk = 0.46**. The combined pass rate for the two was about 87%. |\n| **Goals** | (1) Cpk ≥ 1.33 for both; (2) FTR ≥ 96%; (3) dimension/twist-related COPQ reduced by ≥ $250,000 per year. |\n| **Scope** | In: yarn specification, circular knitting, dyeing and finishing heat-setting and compacting, fabric relaxation before cutting. Out: pattern changes, consumer laundering habits. |\n| **Timeline** | 2026-01 to 2026-06 (with 3 more months of data tracking in the Control phase) |\n| **Team** | Champion: VP Supply Chain; Black Belt: QA Senior Manager; members: fabric manager, Sourcing, dye-house process engineer, garment-factory IE |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Five-phase roadmap\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    D[Define<br>SIPOC / VOC / CTQ] --> M[Measure<br>MSA / baseline capability]\n    M --> A[Analyze<br>stratification / hypothesis tests / regression]\n    A --> I[Improve<br>DOE / pilot verification]\n    I --> C[Control<br>SPC / error-proofing / standardization]\n```\n\n### 1. DEFINE\n- **VOC → CTQ**: \"It must not get shorter after washing, and the side seams must not twist to the front\" → CTQ1 length shrinkage −5.0% to +2.0% (AATCC 135, 3 cycles); CTQ2 twist ≤ 5.0% (AATCC 179).\n- **SIPOC**: S yarn mills / dye houses / chemical suppliers → I combed cotton / polyester DTY fleece yarn, heat-set softener → P knitting → dyeing → heat-setting/compacting → relaxing and cutting → sewing → O sweatshirts → C US retailers and consumers.\n\n### 2. MEASURE\n- **MSA**: 3 operators × 10 specimens × 2 trials, measuring AATCC 135 benchmark distances and calculating shrinkage. %GRR (relative to a tolerance width of 7.0 percentage points) = **7.8%**, number of distinct categories ndc = 9: the measurement system is acceptable. A Gage R&R was also done for twist (%GRR = 12%, conditionally acceptable, with a single laying-flat and marking method prescribed).\n- **Baseline**: 30 consecutive dye lots, 3 samples per lot from head, middle and tail (n = 90):\n  - Length shrinkage: mean −4.0%, σ = 0.60%, Cpk = (−4.0 − (−5.0)) / (3 × 0.60) = **0.56**;\n  - Width shrinkage: mean +0.4%, within spec with ample capability (not a focus of this project);\n  - Twist: mean 3.9%, σ = 0.80%, Cpk = (5.0 − 3.9) / (3 × 0.80) = **0.46**.\n- Process stability: the I-MR chart showed 3 lots outside the control limits, all from the same stenter, which became a lead for Analyze.\n\n### 3. ANALYZE\n1. **Length shrinkage**:\n   - Stratified by stenter: stenter No. 2 averaged 1.1 percentage points lower shrinkage than No. 1 (t-test p < 0.01), because No. 2's actual overfeed was about 6% below setpoint (overfeed roller slipping).\n   - Regression: length shrinkage correlated significantly with stenter overfeed (R² = 0.62) and exit moisture content.\n   - No relaxation before cutting: cut parts shrank back an average of 0.8% within 24 h, adding dimensional deviation.\n2. **Twist**:\n   - Yarn twist factor: lots with twist factor 3.8 averaged 1.3 percentage points more twist than lots at 3.4 (p < 0.01);\n   - Many feeders on the circular knitting machine, all with the same twist direction; no bow/skew correction during heat-setting.\n\n### 4. IMPROVE\n- **DOE (3 × 3 × 2 full factorial, 2 replicates, 36 runs)**: A stenter overfeed (15%, 25%, 35%), B heat-set temperature (150, 160, 170 °C), C compactor pressure (2 levels: 0.3, 0.5 MPa); responses were length shrinkage and fabric weight.\n  - Conclusion: A had the largest main effect and the A×B interaction was significant; C had little effect. The optimal window was overfeed 25 ± 2%, temperature 160 ± 3 °C, compacting 0.5 MPa. In 5 confirmation lots, mean length shrinkage was −2.6%.\n- **Twist** (not a DOE factor; verified separately):\n  - Fleece face-yarn twist factor changed from 3.8 to 3.4, with alternating S/Z twist feeders on the circular machine;\n  - The stenter was fitted with skew detection and automatic correction, with a skew control target of ≤ 2%.\n- **Cutting room**: tension-free spreading + 24 h relaxation (set from measured recovery of cut parts), checking recovery every 50 plies.\n- **Stenter repair**: stenter No. 2 overfeed roller covering replaced; actual overfeed checked every shift with marked fabric.\n\n### 5. CONTROL\n- **SPC**: the 3 head/middle/tail shrinkage samples per lot form one subgroup on an $\\bar{X}$-R chart; control limits are calculated from the first 25 subgroups after improvement, and specification limits must never be used in place of control limits.\n- **Error-proofing**: heat-set recipes are called up by barcode scan; the machine alarms and locks when overfeed or temperature leaves the window; overfeed rollers are maintained weekly.\n- **Documents**: update the \"Knit Fleece Heat-Setting and Compacting Work Instruction\" (WI-PRD-088); add shrinkage and twist to the fabric release green card.\n- **Control-phase data** (40 consecutive lots after improvement): length shrinkage mean −2.6%, σ = 0.53%, Cpk = (−2.6 + 5.0) / 1.59 = **1.51**; twist mean 2.2%, σ = 0.62%, Cpk = (5.0 − 2.2) / 1.86 = **1.51**.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Project results\n\n| Metric | Before | After (3 months of Control phase) | Notes |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| Length shrinkage Cpk | 0.56 | **1.51** | 40 consecutive lots, process in control |\n| Twist Cpk | 0.46 | **1.51** | Same |\n| Combined post-wash dimension + twist pass rate | ≈ 87% | ≈ 99.9% (estimated from capability); all 40 lots passed when tested | |\n| FTR (first-time-right final inspection) | 84.5% | **96.8%** | Remaining nonconformances are mostly appearance items, outside this project's scope |\n| Dimension/twist-related COPQ | $420,000 per year | **$96,000 per year** (annualized) | New testing and maintenance cost about $30,000 per year; net saving about **$294,000 per year**, checked by Finance |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Interview talking points\n1. Explain **how the baseline was measured** (acceptable MSA, 30 lots and 90 samples, the Cpk formula).\n2. Describe the **structure of the DOE** (a 3×3×2 full factorial; do not call it a response surface) and the significant factors.\n3. Point out that the DOE could not solve twist; twist required going back to yarn twist factor and feeder arrangement.\n4. Report results as **net savings**, and state the measurement basis and observation period.\n"},"PB-FMEA":{"title":"FMEA Implementation Guide and Template for Apparel and Home Textiles","content":"# FMEA Implementation Guide and Template for Apparel and Home Textiles\n\n> **DFMEA (Design FMEA) and PFMEA (Process FMEA) tailored for textiles, apparel and home textiles**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why FMEA matters in textiles and apparel\n\nIn fast fashion, premium activewear, technical outdoor wear and high-spec US home textiles, traditional end-of-line inspection can no longer absorb the high cost of recalls, air freight and claims.\n**FMEA (Failure Mode and Effects Analysis)** is a **preventive, front-loaded quality tool**. It identifies potential risks early in development (proto / SMS sampling) and in production readiness (PP meeting / tooling), shifting the focus of control from end-of-line inspection to the design and process stages.\n\n- **DFMEA (design FMEA)**: led jointly by **QA + PDD (design/pattern) + Raw Material (fabric team)**, focusing on style construction, fabric and trim combinations, stress at seams, compatibility of functional coatings, and regulatory compliance.\n- **PFMEA (process FMEA)**: led jointly by **QA + factory IE/technical team + Sourcing**, focusing on potential process variation across spreading, cutting, printing and embroidery, sewing, washing, finishing, pressing, packing and needle detection.\n\n---\n\n## 2. S-O-D rating criteria for textiles and apparel\n\n### 1. Severity (S): effect of the failure on the end consumer and on compliance\n| Rank | Criteria | Textile/apparel/home-textile examples |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **10** | **Safety hazard or violation of a mandatory regulation (without warning)** | Hood drawstrings on children's upper outerwear in sizes 2T–12 (16 CFR 1120.3); lead in coatings/substrates of children's products over the limit (CPSIA); children's sleepwear failing flammability (16 CFR 1615/1616); a Class 3 clothing fabric (16 CFR 1610); a retained broken needle causing puncture injury. |\n| **9** | **Regulatory violation or safety risk (with warning, or perceptible before it occurs)** | California Prop 65 exposure above the safe harbor without a warning; intentionally added PFAS under AB 1817; wrong fiber-content/country-of-origin label (FTC); detention for missing UFLPA traceability documents. |\n| **8** | **Complete loss of basic function / core claim fails** | Hydrostatic pressure of a waterproof shell drops to zero, causing severe leaks; widespread down leakage at seams so the jacket no longer insulates; blackout curtain coating blocks and powders, letting light through completely. |\n| **7** | **Serious functional defect / consumer very dissatisfied and returns** | Yoga pant seams burst completely during a deep split (seam bursting); a sheet shrinks > 15% on first wash and no longer fits the mattress; a dark outer garment bleeds heavily onto underwear/furniture. |\n| **6** | **Moderate functional defect / noticeably reduced performance** | Fabric pills to grade 1–2 (ASTM D3512); moisture-wicking fails after 5 launderings; seam slippage at the knees of jeans shows white. |\n| **5** | **Moderate appearance defect / clearly visible** | Severe leg twist/skew (spirality > 8%) rotating the hem; obvious shade difference between body and sleeves (shade < grade 3–4); local cracking/peeling of a print. |\n| **4** | **Minor appearance defect / noticed by most customers** | Slight seam puckering (grade 2–3); symmetric measurements slightly out of tolerance (0.5–1 cm); occasional slight loop pulls on towels. |\n| **3** | **Minor flaw / noticed only by discerning customers** | Untrimmed thread ends (< 1/4 inch); slightly asymmetric packaging fold; inside woven label slightly crooked without affecting wear. |\n| **2** | **Very minor flaw / no effect on use or appearance** | Slight burrs on a hangtag barcode print (still scannable); slight wrinkles in carton sealing tape. |\n| **1** | **No effect** | No perceptible negative effect. |\n\n---\n\n### 2. Occurrence (O): likelihood of the failure mode\n| Rank | Probability / PPM | Corresponding textile manufacturing situation |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **10** | ≥ 10% (≥ 100,000 PPM) | New complex fabric/extreme process; supplier has no bulk experience; no process validation. |\n| **9** | 5% to < 10% | Same, but with sampling experience. |\n| **8** | 2% to < 5% | Historically high-frequency problem processes (rolled hems on ultra-thin chiffon, holes in heavy washes, sewing high-stretch four-way-stretch fabric). |\n| **7** | 1% to < 2% | Similar processes have failed several times; control relies on people. |\n| **6** | 0.5% to < 1% | Fairly mature process but highly dependent on operator skill; equipment lacks temperature/pressure calibration. |\n| **5** | 0.2% to < 0.5% | Mature process with occasional variation. |\n| **4** | 0.1% to < 0.2% | Fairly high automation (automatic cutting, template sewing), with incoming material inspection. |\n| **3** | 0.01% to < 0.1% | Statistical process control in place and the process is stable. |\n| **2** | 0.001% to < 0.01% | Error-proofing (poka-yoke) and parameter interlocks, supported by historical data. |\n| **1** | Failure eliminated by design or error-proofing | Physically impossible (e.g. drawstrings removed from the design). |\n\n> Occurrence must be scored on historical data for this or a similar process; without data take a conservative value, and never give 1–2 simply because \"it has been changed\".\n\n---\n\n### 3. Detection (D): ability to catch the failure before it reaches the next operation or the customer\n| Rank | Detection ability | Existing controls and inspection |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **10 / 9** | Almost undetectable / not inspected | No test or inspection station; the failure is a hidden chemical exceedance (e.g. formaldehyde, pH, phthalates, heavy metals) with no lot testing. |\n| **8 / 7** | Very hard to detect | Only subjective visual spot checks by end-of-line workers, no gauges, no light booth for color, very low test frequency. |\n| **6 / 5** | Moderate detection | In-process (IPQC) patrol inspection and finished-goods AQL sampling, but mainly visual, with a risk of escapes. |\n| **4 / 3** | Good detection | Validated checks: lab test release per dye lot/lot (sampling, a lot-level detection, usually 4); needle detectors verified during the shift; first-piece destructive tests (usually 3–4). |\n| **2 / 1** | Very high detection / automatic interception of every piece | 100% inline automatic inspection validated by MSA (AOI fabric inspection, automatic stop on thread break, parameter interlocks); the failure is stopped at the station. **Sample testing and supplier reports cannot be rated 1–2.** |\n\n---\n\n## 3. RPN and AP decision rules\n\n- **RPN formula**: `RPN = S (severity) × O (occurrence) × D (detection)` (range 1–1000)\n- **Triggers for action**:\n  1. **S = 9 or 10 (safety and compliance)**: an action is required regardless of RPN. Design elimination comes first (lower S, or O = 1); if only controls are possible, residual risk with O ≥ 2 after action must be signed off by the QA lead and Compliance, with lot-level verification records kept.\n  2. **RPN ≥ 120 or AP = High**: list as a key control point and write it into the QCP.\n  3. **AIAG-VDA AP principle**: judge priority in the order S → O → D, so a high-S risk is not hidden by a low RPN.\n  4. **Post-action ratings need evidence**: O is based on trial/first-lot data, D on the real capability of the detection method (see the table above). Sample testing and supplier reports are lot-level detection, so D is generally no lower than 4.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Working PFMEA template and example\n\n### Background: high-waisted paneled yoga leggings (cut-and-sew), next-season style introduced after the [APP-001](case:APP-001) incident\nThis table is the PFMEA done at sample stage for the next-season style in the same series after the APP-001 incident: the causes and actions at step 20 directly incorporate APP-001's root cause and corrective actions. APP-001 itself had no PFMEA before production, and the incident only surfaced after launch; the STAR below covers this new-style introduction, not the APP-001 incident itself.\n- **Fabric**: 75% Nylon 66 / 25% Spandex, 220 GSM, double-sided brushed\n- **Product type**: adult activewear (**not a children's product, so the CPSIA lead limits do not apply**; chemical compliance follows Prop 65 and the customer RSL)\n- **Market**: US retail brand\n\n| Step | Process step | Failure mode | Effect | S | Cause / mechanism | Current prevention | O | Current detection | D | RPN | Recommended action | Owner | After action (S-O-D-RPN) and evidence |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- | :---: | :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- | :---: | :---: |\n| **10** | Fabric relaxing and spreading | Cut-part size deviation / garment shrinkage over spec after washing | Garment runs small, returns (S=7) | **7** | Residual stress in heat-set spandex fabric not released; spreading under tension | Relax 24 h required | **6** | End-of-line garment measurement (AQL) | **6** | **252** | (1) Tension-free spreading + 24–48 h relaxation (duration set from measured cut-part recovery); (2) record heat-set temperature/overfeed; (3) AATCC 135 test per dye lot before cutting | QA + fabric mill (before PP) | 7-3-4 = **84**; evidence: shrinkage of the first 3 dye lots all within ±3%, cut-part recovery < 0.5% |\n| **20** | Waistband/gusset 4-needle 6-thread (607 flatlock) | Seam cracking | Seam breaks when squatting (S=8) | **8** | (1) Looper thread is ordinary spun polyester and looper tension is too tight, so the stitch has too little thread reserve; (2) low SPI (9–10); (3) needles damage the spandex | Spec sheet SPI = 10–12 | **5** | Operator hand-pull self-check | **5** | **200** | (1) Change looper thread to textured nylon; (2) SPI 12–14, with looper tension and differential feed locked using a seam extension curve; (3) 65/9 SES light ball-point needle; (4) first piece per machine per shift stretched to 2× original length (100% elongation) for 10 cycles per the customer method | QA + IE | 8-3-4 = **96**; evidence: seam elongation ≥ 120% in all 3 trial lots, stable extension curve |\n| **30a** | Heat-transfer logo application | Cracking and lifting edges after washing | Appearance and brand damage (S=6) | **6** | Actual press temperature 15 °C below setpoint; uneven pressure | Machine display temperature | **5** | Visual check at inspection | **7** | **210** | (1) Measure actual temperature with a thermocouple and check pressure; first-piece confirmation at changeover; (2) first piece each shift washed 3 times per the customer program (AATCC 135 conditions) and checked | QA + factory | 6-3-4 = **72** |\n| **30b** | Chemical compliance of transfer film | Transfer film contains restricted substances (e.g. phthalates, PFAS) | Breach of the Prop 65 warning obligation or AB 1817 (S=9) | **9** | Film supplier changed formulation without notice | Annual certificate only | **3** | No lot testing | **8** | **216** | (1) Put transfer film on the RSL approved-supplier list with a change-notification clause; (2) new lots/new formulations tested to the RSL by an accredited lab before receipt | QA + Purchasing | 9-2-4 = **72**; residual risk with S ≥ 9 signed off by Compliance |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Interview application tips\n\n> **Interviewer**: \"In past projects, how did you use FMEA to reduce the risk of new product development and bulk production?\"\n\n### Answer framework (STAR structure)\n1. **Situation**: \"The previous season's yoga pants in the same series had a post-launch incident with pilling and seam cracking (see APP-001). The next season was introducing an ultra-high-stretch, color-blocked style with a heat transfer, the kind of product that tends to see seam bursting, color migration and transfer peeling in bulk.\"\n2. **Task**: \"As QA lead, I had to lead a cross-functional **DFMEA and PFMEA** at sample stage, get actions in place for every item with RPN ≥ 120 or S ≥ 9 before the PP sample, and prove with trial data that the actions worked.\"\n3. **Action**:\n   - **Ran a cross-functional review**: brought together the PDD pattern maker, fabric development, factory IE and line supervisors to build a failure library by operation.\n   - **Targeted the high-RPN and high-S items**: seam bursting (S=8, RPN=200), transfer-film chemical compliance (S=9, RPN=216), transfer adhesion (RPN=210).\n   - **Implemented actions and wrote them into the QCP**: looper thread changed to textured nylon, SPI and tension locked with a seam extension curve, first-piece elongation cycling every shift; transfer film added to the RSL approved list with a change-notification clause, with new lots tested before use; press temperature measured with a thermocouple.\n4. **Result (quantified)**: \"Seam extension and post-wash transfer adhesion passed on all of the first 3 lots, and FTR rose from a historical 91% on similar styles to 97%. One quarter after launch, the physically verified quality return rate was 0.8%, below the similar-style baseline of 1.1% (on the same basis as APP-001). The total return rate (including size and preference, about 18–20%) is not something FMEA can improve, and I did not count it as a result.\"\n"},"PB-RCA":{"title":"6M Fishbone and 5-Why Root Cause Analysis Toolbox for Textiles, Apparel and Home Textiles","content":"# 6M Fishbone and 5-Why Root Cause Analysis Toolbox for Textiles, Apparel and Home Textiles\n\n> **Root cause analysis (RCA) toolbox: 6M Ishikawa fishbone and 5-Why methodology tailored for textile engineering**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why generic RCA often stays superficial in textiles and apparel\n\nMany factories and junior inspectors attribute causes to:\n- ❌ \"Careless or unskilled workers\"\n- ❌ \"Sudden equipment breakdown\"\n- ❌ \"Bad incoming material from the supplier\"\n\nSuperficial conclusions like these do not solve the problem, and quality incidents keep recurring.\nThis toolbox combines **polymer materials science, textile machinery dynamics, dyeing and finishing physical chemistry, and garment engineering** to adapt the traditional **6M model (Man, Machine, Material, Method, Measurement, Milieu)** to the textile and apparel supply chain, as a systematic dictionary for tracing failures.\n\n---\n\n## 2. 6M fishbone dimensions for textiles and apparel\n\n```\n                 Major textile/apparel quality defect (e.g. pilling/tearing/shade variation/seam bursting)\n                                       ▲\n       Man                     Machine                Material\n         │                     │                      │\n   ──────┴─────────────────────┴──────────────────────┴───────► [Quality defect]\n         ┬                     ┬                      ┬\n         │                     │                      │\n       Method               Measurement            Milieu (environment)\n```\n\n| Dimension (6M) | Key checkpoints for textiles/apparel/home textiles | Typical failure mechanisms and hazards |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Man** | - Technique and skill of knitters/weavers and sewing operators<br>- Training and first-piece comparison at shift change/for new staff<br>- Inspectors' color vision and understanding of standard tolerances<br>- Briefing on and adherence to process instructions | Operators stretching fabric while sewing cause wavy puckering; color checkers not using the buyer-specified illuminant (e.g. D65 vs CWF) make visual errors; spreaders pulling fabric destroy the tension-free state. |\n| **Machine** | - Needle size (point type: ball/sharp/round) matched to the throat-plate hole<br>- Presser-foot pressure, feed-dog height and differential ratio<br>- Dye-machine nozzle pressure and speed, stenter temperature control accuracy<br>- Inspection-machine tension sensors, cutter-blade wear and sharpness<br>- Needle detector sensitivity calibration and nine-point test | A needle that is too coarse or has a burred point cuts high-count cotton or spandex, causing needle holes; a > 8 °C temperature difference between the stenter's sides causes side-to-center shade variation; too much moisture in pressing-table steam leaves water marks. |\n| **Material** | - Raw cotton fiber length, micronaire, maturity<br>- Yarn count, twist multiplier, hairiness index<br>- Fabric construction and weave (sateen vs percale vs jacquard)<br>- Dye and auxiliary molecular weight, fixation rate, purity and heavy-metal impurities<br>- Sewing thread type (spun polyester vs high-tenacity filament polyester vs textured thread) | Immature cotton causes undyed white specks (neps); low twist multiplier worsens fuzzing and pilling; lead in the plating of metal trims exceeds the limit; uneven thread strength causes frequent breaks in high-speed sewing. |\n| **Method** | - Dyeing temperature profile, liquor ratio, hold time, after-washing and soaping process<br>- Resin finishing cure temperature, catalyst ratio and softener type<br>- Stitch type (SPI, stitches per inch; 301 lockstitch vs 514 overlock)<br>- Grain direction in marker layout (warp/weft skew) and seam allowance width<br>- Packaging fold and vacuum-packing/stacking method | Incomplete reduction clearing lets disperse dyes migrate thermally, sharply reducing crocking fastness; excessive durable press curing temperature embrittles cellulose by acid hydrolysis; a too-narrow seam allowance (< 1/4\") lets the seam slip out. |\n| **Measurement** | - Standard light-booth configuration (D65, TL84, CWF, UV)<br>- Conditioned lab environment (ASTM D1776 standard atmosphere 21 °C, 65% RH, tolerances per the current edition)<br>- Instrument calibration (tensile-tester load cells, electronic balances, abrasion-tester loads)<br>- Sampling plan and representativeness (discard 1 m at each end of the piece) | Testing strength without the conditioning the method requires distorts readings when cotton moisture regain is low; wet crock cloth pick-up not held at 65 ± 5% causes inter-lab differences in wet crocking grades; slipping tensile-tester jaws distort the tear curve; light-booth tubes past their service life (> 2,000 hours) drift spectrally. |\n| **Milieu (environment)** | - Temperature and humidity in the weaving room (to prevent static and fly entanglement)<br>- Warehouse humidity, mold and moisture prevention, ventilation<br>- Airborne sulfides/nitrogen oxides (NOx) (to prevent yellowing)<br>- Container micro-environment (\"container rain\", hot and humid holds) | Warehouse relative humidity > 75% with poor ventilation causes mold spots on fabric; BHT antioxidant in poly bags reacts with NOx in the air to cause phenolic yellowing of white garments. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. 5-Why dictionary for classic textile quality problems\n\n### Example 1: colorfastness failure. Wet crocking of jet-black cotton/spandex athletic pants fails (AATCC 8 wet grade 1.5; customer requirement ≥ 2.5)\n- **Why 1**: why is wet crocking only grade 1.5?\n  - *Answer*: Large amounts of unfixed and hydrolyzed reactive dye remain on the fabric surface, and the wet crock cloth picks them up.\n- **Why 2**: why is there so much loose dye?\n  - *Answer*: To reach a very deep jet black, reactive black was dosed at 8.5% o.w.f., close to or beyond that dye's effective fixation capacity on cotton, leaving a large unfixed share.\n- **Why 3**: why was the loose dye not removed in soaping?\n  - *Answer*: Only one 80 °C soaping was done, with no anti-backstaining agent, and the soaping end point was not judged by the color of the residual liquor. Because of the spandex, the soaping temperature cannot simply be raised above 95 °C (that would damage spandex recovery); the right approach is more soaping baths, optimized temperature and time, an anti-backstaining agent, and an end point judged by residual liquor color and wet crocking grade.\n- **Why 4**: why did the dye house not tailor a multi-bath high-temperature soaping and fixing process to the high-concentration jet-black recipe?\n  - *Answer*: To save water, power and steam and shorten the dyeing cycle, the dye house applied its routine soaping SOP for light and medium shades to the extra-deep black.\n- **Why 5 (system root cause)**: why did Purchasing and fabric QA not review the process recipe and fastness capability at supplier onboarding and first-lot release?\n  - *Answer*: **QA had no special process audit or mandatory first-lot test matrix for \"high-risk deep shades\" (a deep-shade colorfastness protocol)**, and extra-deep shades had no separate approved-process list.\n\n---\n\n### Example 2: physical defect. Buttons on a children's woven shirt tear out with the fabric after washing (button tear-out)\n- **Why 1**: why, after 3 washes, does the button tear out with the fabric in the pull test?\n  - *Answer*: Under the customer's children's-wear attachment pull method (referencing the use-and-abuse conditions of 16 CFR 1500.51–1500.53, commonly 15 lbf held for 10 s; ASTM F963 is a toy standard and not a basis for apparel), the fabric at the placket button position tears first.\n- **Why 2**: why is only a single layer of fabric taking the load at the placket?\n  - *Answer*: The fusible interlining in the placket delaminated over a large area after washing and no longer reinforced it.\n- **Why 3**: why did the interlining delaminate?\n  - *Answer*: Purchasing switched to a cheaper **low-density polyethylene (LDPE)** dot-coated interlining with a low melting point and poor wash durability; the measured fabric-surface temperature during fusing was only 120 °C (the interlining supplier's recommended adhesive window was 135–145 °C), so the adhesive did not penetrate the fabric properly.\n- **Why 4**: why did the tech pack not lock down the interlining?\n  - *Answer*: The tech pack only said \"White Interlining\", with no interlining code, adhesive type or fusing parameters. For shirts that need hot-water washing, **HDPE or PES (polyester) adhesive interlinings** have good wash durability; **PA (polyamide) adhesive interlinings** are mainly for dry-clean durability and generally do poorly in hot washing, so \"PA is washable\" should not be stated as a blanket rule.\n- **Why 5 (system root cause)**: why was there no trim compatibility and destructive pull review at sample and pre-production stage?\n  - *Answer*: **There was no QA–PDD trim selection library and no mandatory \"attachment pull after washing\" gate for children's wear**; the trim substitution did not go through an ECN and did not trigger a reassessment of the testing behind the CPC.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Using 5-Why in quality interviews (the three-level rule)\n\nWhen answering \"how do you find the root cause?\" in an interview, walk through **three levels**:\n1. **Level 1: physical/chemical mechanism**\n   - Explain the phenomenon in technical terms (e.g. fiber breakage, thermal dye migration, excessive resin crosslinking, unbalanced interlacing resistance).\n2. **Level 2: process and tooling failure**\n   - Point to parameter drift on the shop floor, missing equipment capability or mismatched tooling (e.g. failed temperature control, wrong needle type, wrong differential ratio).\n3. **Level 3: system and management failure**\n   - Dig out missing standards, uncontrolled ECN changes, insufficient early DFQ involvement, or blind spots in supplier tiering and control.\n   *(Reaching the third level is what separates a quality director or senior manager from a regular QC inspector.)*\n"},"SOP-COMPLIANCE":{"title":"QA and Compliance Collaboration SOP: US Market Entry, Safety Regulations and Recall Defense","content":"# QA and Compliance Collaboration SOP: US Market Entry, Safety Regulations and Recall Defense\n\n> **US regulatory compliance and recall defense SOP: CPSC, CPSIA, Prop 65, FTC, UFLPA and certificate protocols (CPC/GCC)**\n\n> Regulatory provisions and values come from a single source, the [US Regulatory Guide](doc:REF-US-REGS); this SOP only defines the division of work and actions between QA and Compliance.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Real consequences of compliance risk (not exaggerated)\n\n- **At the port**: CPSC can sample at the port; nonconforming goods may be refused admission, and the importer must re-export, recondition under supervision (if approved) or destroy them within the set period. Goods subject to the UFLPA presumption are detained until the importer rebuts it or re-exports.\n- **In the market**: nonconforming products already in commerce may trigger the CPSA Section 15(b) reporting duty and a recall (usually a voluntary recall negotiated with CPSC).\n- **Penalties**: failing to report on time or selling banned products can lead to civil penalties; after the 2021 adjustment, the statutory maximum for a related series of violations is about **$17.15M**. A penalty is not an automatic consequence of a detention; it depends on knowledge, timeliness of reporting, violation history and other factors.\n- **QA's role**: gatekeeper of the technical evidence and product determinations; legal characterization and external communication belong to Legal, and QA does not make legal commitments externally on its own.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Regulation–product matching matrix\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    P[Product definition: children's/adult · size range · sleepwear/daywear · home-textile category · state of sale · channel] --> C1[CPSC flammability: 1610 / 1615-1616 / 1630-1631 / 1632]\n    P --> C2[Children's products: lead / phthalates for toys and child care articles only / drawstrings / small-part risk / tracking labels / CPC]\n    P --> F[FTC: 303 fiber content and origin / 423 care labels / 260 environmental claims]\n    P --> S[State law: Prop 65 warnings / PFAS laws such as AB 1817]\n    P --> U[CBP: UFLPA supply-chain traceability]\n```\n\n| Regulation | Products | Core requirement | Method / evidence | Risk of violation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **16 CFR 1610** | Adult and children's clothing textiles | Class 3 may not be sold; plain-surface fabric ≥ 3.5 s is Class 1; raised-surface classes per the regulatory guide | 45° flammability tester; or a testing exemption (plain-surface fabric ≥ 2.6 oz/yd², or made entirely of acrylic/modacrylic/nylon/olefin/polyester/wool) | Stop sale, recall |\n| **16 CFR 1615/1616** | Children's sleepwear in sizes 0–14 | Average char length ≤ 7.0 in, as received and after 50 wash-and-dry cycles; or meets the tight-fitting dimension table with the prescribed label | Vertical flame test | Recall, penalties |\n| **CPSIA lead** | Children's products for ages 12 and under | Coatings ≤ 90 ppm (Sec. 101(f)/16 CFR 1303); substrates ≤ 100 ppm | CPSC-CH-E1001/2/3 (accredited lab) | May not be sold |\n| **16 CFR 1307 phthalates** | **Children's toys and child care articles** (incl. sleepwear and bibs for ages 3 and under) | Each of 8 ≤ 0.1% | CPSC-CH-C1001-09.4 | May not be sold; ordinary children's apparel follows the customer RSL |\n| **16 CFR 1120.3 / ASTM F1816** | Children's upper outerwear | No hood/neck drawstrings in 2T–12 (incl. size 12/L); waist/bottom drawstrings in 2T–16 must meet F1816; hood/neck drawstrings in 14–16 are not addressed by regulation and follow customer requirements | Size-range rule + physical check | Substantial product hazard, recall |\n| **16 CFR 1632** | Mattresses, mattress pads | Cigarette-ignition char length ≤ 2 in | Prototype test records | May not be sold |\n| **16 CFR 1630/1631** | Carpets and rugs | Pill test | Prototype testing | May not be sold / must be labeled |\n| **FTC 423** | Textile apparel and some home textiles that need care | At least one complete set of care instructions, with a reasonable basis (tests, experience or industry expertise) | ASTM D3938 guide; verification with AATCC 135/150/61, etc. | FTC enforcement |\n| **FTC 303 / 300** | Textile and wool products | Generic names, ±3 percentage point tolerance (not applicable to \"100%\"), origin, RN | AATCC 20/20A | FTC enforcement |\n| **Prop 65** | Products sold in California | Exposure above the safe harbor requires a warning in the prescribed format | Screening against settlement benchmarks | 60-day notice, settlement |\n| **CA AB 1817** | Textile articles | No intentionally added PFAS; TOF < 100 ppm (< 50 ppm from 2027) | TOF + declaration | State enforcement |\n| **UFLPA** | Especially cotton products | Supply chain must not involve Xinjiang or listed entities | Tier-by-tier traceability package | Detention, refusal of admission |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Certificates and eFiling\n\n- **CPC**: children's products must be certified on the basis of tests by a **CPSC-accepted third-party lab**, issued by the US importer or domestic manufacturer; material or supplier changes require retesting (16 CFR 1107/1109).\n- **GCC**: applies only to non-children's products subject to a CPSC rule, ban or standard (e.g. adult clothing requiring 1610 testing, mattress pads under 1632, carpets under 1630/1631), issued on the basis of a test or a reasonable testing program.\n- **eFiling**: from **2026-07-08**, imported products requiring a certificate must have certificate data filed electronically with CBP/CPSC at entry (from 2027-01-08 for entries through foreign trade zones). Wrong or missing data can delay release or lead to sampling; there is no promise of \"instant automatic release\".\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Factory as Manufacturer/supplier\n    participant Lab as CPSC-accepted third-party lab\n    participant QA as QA & Compliance\n    participant Broker as Customs broker / CBP / CPSC\n\n    Factory->>Lab: Send production-representative samples with every trim component (incl. colors/lots)\n    Lab->>Lab: Test to the applicable regulations\n    Lab-->>QA: Test report\n    QA->>QA: Check style, color, material lots and BOM against the report; confirm no undeclared changes\n    QA->>QA: Issue the CPC/GCC and maintain the certificate database\n    QA->>Broker: Provide eFiling certificate data / certificate number\n    Broker-->>QA: Entry result; if sampled or detained, start the Section 4 process\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. Responding to port detentions and market problems\n\n1. **0–24 h**: freeze all goods of the same style in transit, in stock and in production; preserve samples and production records; notify Legal and the customer's compliance contact.\n2. **24–72 h**: confirm the facts of the violation and the applicable provisions (size range, product definition, method edition); assess whether similar products have already entered commerce, and Legal decides on the Section 15(b) reporting duty.\n3. **Disposition**: recondition (requires written CPSC/CBP approval, applied to every piece with independent re-verification), re-export or destroy. If reconditioning involves a material change, retest at a third-party lab and reissue the CPC.\n4. **Root cause and system**: tech pack review, BOM change control, trim lot testing, size-range rules in the PLM.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Interview scenario drill\n\n> **Interviewer**: \"A lot of children's hoodies in sizes 4–12 is about to ship. On site you find a drawstring threaded through the hood with plastic cord stoppers. The factory says the length is no more than 3 inches and asks to ship first. What do you do?\"\n\n**Key points of the answer**:\n1. **Characterize it**: for sizes 2T–12, a hood/neck drawstring is itself a substantial product hazard under 16 CFR 1120.3, regardless of length; \"3 inches\" is a rule for waist/bottom drawstrings and does not apply here. Hood/neck drawstrings in sizes 14–16 are outside 16 CFR 1120.3 and F1816 and are judged by the customer manual; but if the labeled size range covers any size of 12 or below, the whole garment falls in the prohibited range. Conclusion: **do not ship**.\n2. **Contain**: issue a shipping hold and freeze every unit of the style in stock, in transit and in production.\n3. **Correct**: remove the drawstring and cord stoppers under an approved engineering change, and close or remove the drawstring channel (e.g. switch to an elasticated hood); **rework every piece and have an independent person re-check every piece**, keeping records and photos. Sample inspection can only audit the rework process; it cannot replace piece-by-piece confirmation.\n4. **Certificate**: if the construction or materials change, confirm the tests behind the CPC are still valid, and retest if needed.\n5. **Root cause**: why did the tech pack and PP sample not catch it? Set drawstring rules by size range in the PLM and add mandatory sign-off at Gate 1/Gate 5.\n"},"SOP-DFQ":{"title":"QA and Product Design & Development (PDD) Collaboration: Design for Quality (DFQ) SOP","content":"# QA and Product Design & Development (PDD) Collaboration: Design for Quality (DFQ) SOP\n\n> **Design for Quality (DFQ) SOP: early QA engagement in product design and development**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why quality must engage early in design and development (PDD)\n\nIn apparel and home-textile supply chains, a substantial share of bulk defects can be traced back to design: unsound construction, incompatible fabrics and trims, or no consideration of manufacturability. The actual share should come from the company's own 8D and returns root-cause database; do not quote an unsourced \"industry percentage\".\n- The traditional \"catch quality at the end\" model: design finished → factory forces it through → serious defects in bulk → rework/delays/air freight/compensation.\n- **The modern QA model (DFQ / gatekeeper)**: quality feasibility reviews start at sketch, sampling (proto / SMS) and tech-pack stages, **removing manufacturing hazards and regulatory red lines at the design source**.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Five-gate QA review in PDD (stage-gate review)\n\n```mermaid\ngraph TD\n    G1[Gate 1: Concept and sketch review<br>Design concept & sketch risk] --> G2[Gate 2: Proto sample and physical assessment<br>Proto sample & fabric compatibility]\n    G2 --> G3[Gate 3: Sales sample and full testing<br>SMS / fit sample comprehensive lab test]\n    G3 --> G4[Gate 4: Tech pack freeze<br>Tech pack freezing & DFMEA]\n    G4 --> G5[Gate 5: Pre-production meeting and trial release<br>Pre-production meeting & PP sign-off]\n```\n\n### Gate inputs, outputs and review criteria\n\n| Gate | Responsible | QA core tasks and review points | Key deliverables | Kill criteria |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Gate 1: concept and sketch** | Designer + QA | 1. Structural safety review (do children's styles include hazardous drawstrings or hard/sharp decorations?);<br>2. First assessment of color-migration risk in color-blocked panels;<br>3. Sewability of complex shaped panels. | Design-stage quality risk assessment matrix (RAM) | Violates a mandatory US regulation (e.g. hood/neck drawstrings in sizes 2T–12 violate 16 CFR 1120.3; children's sleepwear designed under the tight-fitting exemption does not meet the 16 CFR 1615/1616 dimension table). |\n| **Gate 2: proto sample** | Pattern maker + QA + PDD | 1. Matching fabric construction to the stresses on seams;<br>2. Seam allowance design for ultra-thin/high-stretch fabrics (to prevent seam slippage);<br>3. Strength and thickness compatibility of key trims (zippers, snaps, hook-and-loop). | Proto sample process review report (proto DFM) | Needle cutting that a needle change cannot solve; severe distortion/twist > 8% after washing. |\n| **Gate 3: fit and sales sample (fit/SMS)** | Fit model + QA + third-party lab | 1. Full physical and chemical testing at a third-party lab;<br>2. Wear and stretch stress tests (bursting test on 4-needle 6-thread seams, abrasion pilling assessment);<br>3. Care label recommendations. | Full lab compliance test report; care-label regulatory determination | Failure of key AATCC/ASTM physical/chemical tests (e.g. fastness < grade 3, formaldehyde/pH over the limit, tear strength too low). |\n| **Gate 4: tech pack freeze** | Tech designer + QA lead | 1. Review that grading and tolerance tables are sound;<br>2. Specify stitch type and stitches per inch (SPI);<br>3. Lock trim specifications and restricted-substance limits (RSL). | Approved tech pack and DFMEA | Tolerances too tight and inconsistent with the fabric's physical shrinkage behavior; missing trim technical parameters. |\n| **Gate 5: PP sample** | Factory + Sourcing + QA | 1. Sample made on the production line and sealed;<br>2. Fabrics, trims and packaging used are the actual bulk materials;<br>3. Pre-production meeting (PPM) held. | Golden sample tag; PPM minutes | Any Critical/Major defect in the PP sample; bulk starts only after correction and a passing resubmission. |\n\n---\n\n## 3. DFQ in practice: high-risk design scenarios and solutions\n\n### 1. Color-blocking design\n- **Typical risk**: in black/white or red/white color blocking, dark dye migrates onto light panels during washing or perspiration (AATCC 61 laundering staining, AATCC 107 colorfastness to water failures).\n- **QA DFQ strategy**:\n  1. **Stop it at the fabric**: rate dark fabrics by AATCC 61 (staining onto the actual fiber of the light fabric) and AATCC 15/107; color-blocked styles usually require staining ≥ grade 4 (≥ 3–3.5 for regular solid colors). Verify real color transfer with a \"dark + light panel garment wash\" rather than testing single fabrics;\n  2. **Process recommendations**: tighten soaping end-point control on dark reactive-dyed cotton (multiple high-temperature soapings + fixing, with the end point judged by residual liquor color and staining grade), or choose a dye combination with higher fixation; the light panel can switch to a fiber less prone to staining. Dye transfers through the wash liquor during laundering, so a piping strip in the seam cannot block it and is not a solution;\n  3. **Care label**: add warnings such as \"Wash with like colors / Wash separately\" and \"Do not soak\", but warnings only supplement and never replace meeting the fastness requirement (FTC 423 requires a reasonable basis for care instructions).\n\n### 2. Seam slippage on delicate/fine fabrics (high-density silk and high-count cotton)\n- **Typical risk**: in high-density sateen bedding or ultra-thin chiffon, yarns slip and open at the seam under load (ASTM D1683 or the customer's finished-seam method; ASTM D434 was withdrawn in 2004). For bedding labeled above 600TC, first check the yarn count and whether plied yarns were counted as multiple (FTC and ASTM D3775 count warp and weft yarns, with a plied yarn counting as one), to avoid inflated thread counts.\n- **QA DFQ strategy**:\n  1. **Wider seam allowance**: widen from 1/4\" (0.6 cm) to 3/8\" (1.0 cm) or 1/2\" (1.2 cm), stated in the tech pack;\n  2. **Construction choice**: bedding preferably uses French seams or overlock + lockstitch; chiffon uses French seams or binding;\n  3. **Stitch and density**: the 301 lockstitch itself is not the cause of slippage; what matters is the combination of SPI, seam allowance, thread tension and the fabric's slippage resistance. A 516 five-thread safety stitch (401 chainstitch + 504 three-thread overlock) can be used, verified by D1683;\n  4. **Fabric side**: when slippage resistance is insufficient, first adjust softener type and dosage and fabric construction with the fabric team, rather than adding hot-melt tape inside the seam (high risk to wash durability and hand).\n\n### 3. Bonded and seam-taped outerwear (functional hot-melt adhesives and laminate peeling)\n- **Typical risk**: seam tape on outdoor shells delaminates and blisters after machine washing, and hydrostatic pressure drops straight to zero.\n- **QA DFQ strategy**:\n  1. **Membrane–adhesive compatibility**: specify in the tech pack a tape type approved jointly by the membrane and tape suppliers (e.g. PU tape on PU membrane); never tape fabrics directly where silicone or DWR can migrate. Fluorine-free DWR has higher surface energy than fluorinated DWR but still requires tape compatibility testing;\n  2. **Process window set from equipment and tape measurements**: on a hot-air seam-taping machine **the nozzle setpoint is usually several hundred degrees Celsius**; what is really controlled is the actual adhesive temperature at the nip (the tape supplier's recommended activation temperature), roller pressure and speed. The window must be set with temperature measurement and a peel-strength DOE; the adhesive activation temperature must never be used as the machine setpoint;\n  3. **Acceptance**: measure peel strength (e.g. ≥ the customer value in N/25 mm) and seam hydrostatic pressure after the customer-specified number of launderings, and record the failure mode (adhesive failure / membrane failure / fabric failure).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Common interview questions and answers\n\n> **Interviewer**: \"Design (PDD) tends to chase visual appeal and novel styles, while QA pursues stability and low risk. When the two departments clash, how do you coordinate and drive DFQ?\"\n\n### Director-level answer logic\n1. **State the philosophy**: \"QA's job is not to kill creativity (not a blocker, but an enabler); it is to use materials science and process engineering to **turn a bold design into a safe, high-quality product that can be mass-produced**.\"\n2. **Let data and facts speak**: \"I don't oppose a design on opinion; I rely on the early **RAM risk matrix and lab destructive-test data**. For example, I put the actual color-blocked sample after 5 washes and the grade 2.0 staining report in front of the designer, and cite the actual share and cost of 'color transfer' returns in this category, rather than a made-up return rate.\"\n3. **Bring alternative engineering solutions**: \"When I object to an approach, I always bring 2–3 alternatives to the meeting. For color-blocking bleed, I don't ask the designer to drop black-and-white blocking; I work with the fabric team to optimize the black fabric's dye combination and soaping end point, raise staining on the white panel to grade 4, and verify it with a color-blocked garment wash. If needed, the white panel switches to a fiber less prone to staining. The design is kept and the quality floor is held.\"\n4. **Make it systematic**: \"Run a **DFQ stage-gate sign-off**, so major quality risks are eliminated before the tech pack is frozen.\"\n"},"SOP-FABRIC":{"title":"QA and Raw Material Collaboration: Fabric Lifecycle Risk Interception SOP","content":"# QA and Raw Material Collaboration: Fabric Lifecycle Risk Interception SOP\n\n> **Raw material quality assurance SOP: mill qualification, 4-point system, shade band and fabric release green card**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why fabric quality is central in apparel and home textiles\n\n\"Fabric is the soul of apparel and home textiles\": most high-frequency quality problems, such as shade variation, shrinkage, pilling, strength and fastness, originate in the fabric. QA should report the actual share of fabric causes in the company's 8D and returns root-cause data every quarter, as a basis for allocating resources.\nThe Raw Material Department focuses on new fiber development, hand, cost and lead time; **QA's job is to set a strict physical and chemical standards matrix, shade control limits and a pre-production interception mechanism**, so that every meter of fabric entering the garment factory is fully under control.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Four pillars of QA–Raw Material collaboration\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    P1[1. Weaving and dye mill qualification<br>Mill qualification] --> P2[2. Color and dye-lot control<br>Lab dip & shade band]\n    P2 --> P3[3. 4-point physical inspection<br>ASTM D5430 4-point]\n    P3 --> P4[4. Bulk testing and green card release<br>Testing & green card]\n```\n\n### 1. Mill technical qualification\nBefore a fabric supplier is onboarded, QA and the fabric team audit the weaving/dye mill on site:\n- **Equipment capability**: computerized automatic dye dispensing; temperature control accuracy of continuous pad-dyeing/overflow dyeing machines; stenter overfeed and automatic width control.\n- **Lab qualification**: an in-house lab approved by the brand or a third party (lab certification), with standard light booths (with calibrated tube-hour timers), electronic tensile testers, standard wash-fastness testers and conditioning chambers.\n- **Environmental and compliance**: per customer requirements, check OEKO-TEX Standard 100 certificates (certificate scope and product class), ZDHC MRSL conformance and wastewater testing, bluesign certification (if applicable), discharge permits, and the yarn/fiber origin documents needed for UFLPA.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Color approval and bulk shade band management\n\n```\n        Light limit <------ Master standard ------> Dark limit\n                                   │\n                           Reddish / Greenish\n                           Yellowish / Bluish (hue tolerance boundaries)\n```\n\n- **Lab dip approval**:\n  - Visual assessment per AATCC EP9 under the buyer-specified primary illuminant (usually D65) and secondary illuminants (US retail commonly uses CWF or LED store lighting; TL84 is more common in European/Asian retail; home settings often add Incandescent A);\n  - Instrumental measurement per AATCC EP6: under the primary illuminant $\\Delta E_{CMC(2:1)} \\le 0.8$ (or the customer value); metamerism index MI (D65 vs the secondary illuminant) as defined by the customer, commonly ≤ 0.5–0.8. Keep a single MI acceptance value company-wide, written in the customer color manual; do not write a different number in each document.\n- **Building the bulk shade band**:\n  - When bulk is dyed in multiple lots, the dye mill must collect representative swatches from each lot and make a **shade band (light–center–dark tolerance cards)**.\n  - QA signs and seals the shade band and distributes it to the garment cutting room; **the cutting room cuts strictly by dye lot, in separate spreads and bundles; mixing lots in one garment, which causes visible shade differences between panels, is prohibited**.\n\n---\n\n### 3. 4-point fabric inspection system (ASTM D5430)\n\n#### (1) Defect penalty points\n| Size of defect | Penalty points |\n| :--- | :---: |\n| Defect length $\\le 3$ inches (7.5 cm) | **1 point** |\n| 3 inches < length $\\le 6$ inches (15 cm) | **2 points** |\n| 6 inches < length $\\le 9$ inches (22.5 cm) | **3 points** |\n| Length $> 9$ inches (22.5 cm) | **4 points** |\n| Hole/opening ≤ 1 inch | **2 points** |\n| Hole/opening > 1 inch | **4 points** |\n| Continuous or full-width defects (continuous creases, side-to-center shade, skew over the limit) | **4 points per yard** (and judge per customer rules whether to downgrade the whole roll) |\n\n*Note: no more than 4 points may be counted in any one linear yard.*\n\n#### (2) Points per 100 square yards\n$$\\text{Points / 100 sq yd} = \\frac{\\text{Total penalty points} \\times 36 \\times 100}{\\text{Inspected yards} \\times \\text{Cuttable width in inches}}$$\n\n#### (3) Acceptance criteria\nThere is no single ASTM acceptance limit; it is set by the customer in the contract. A common US retailer practice is to set both a \"single-roll maximum\" and a \"lot-average maximum\"; this library uses the following internal baseline (stricter where the customer is stricter):\n- **Premium/luxury**: lot average ≤ 20 points/100 sq yd, single roll ≤ 28 points;\n- **Regular apparel/home textiles**: lot average ≤ 28 points/100 sq yd, single roll ≤ 40 points;\n- Rolls over the single-roll maximum may not be cut; they are graded, returned, or accepted only with a written customer concession; if the lot average is over the limit, the whole lot is quarantined for review.\n- Inspection rate: at least 10% of rolls per lot (and no fewer than the customer-specified number), tightened to 20% or full inspection for high-risk fabrics or new suppliers. The IQC acceptance values in the QCP cite this section and set no separate values.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Fabric release green card protocol\n\n```mermaid\nsequenceDiagram\n    autonumber\n    participant Mill as Fabric mill\n    participant Lab as Testing lab\n    participant QA as Material QA\n    participant Factory as Garment factory\n\n    Mill->>Lab: Draw representative samples from the first bulk lot and every dye lot\n    Lab->>Lab: Run the full physical/chemical test set (AATCC/ASTM/RSL)\n    Lab-->>QA: Issue the full test report (pass/fail)\n    Mill->>QA: Submit the 4-point inspection report and shade band cards\n    alt All tests pass and the 4-point result meets the limits\n        QA->>Factory: Issue the bulk fabric release green card\n        Factory->>Factory: Cutting room checks the green card against the dye lot, then starts cutting\n    else A test fails\n        QA->>Mill: Issue a nonconformance report (NCR) and lock the fabric\n        QA->>Mill: Open an 8D / retest / re-weave with new yarn\n    end\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Major fabric defects and QA solutions\n\n| Defect | Test standard | Failure mechanism | QA preventive solution |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fastness loss from thermal migration of disperse dyes** | AATCC 61 / AATCC 8 / AATCC 107 | After dyeing polyester (incl. polyester/spandex), disperse dyes migrate toward the fiber surface and into the softener/surfactant phase during high-temperature heat-setting or heat treatment; cationic/nonionic auxiliaries and some amino silicones make migration worse. | 1. Use sublimation-resistant/low-migration disperse dye combinations and thorough reduction clearing after dyeing (end point judged by clearing-liquor color and crocking fastness);<br>2. Use low-migration softeners; pre-set before dyeing where possible, and lower the post-dyeing heat-set temperature as far as dimensional stability allows, retesting shrinkage. |\n| **Low tear strength in high-density cotton** | ASTM D1424 (Elmendorf) | Excessive durable press resin crosslinking, or acid catalysts with high-temperature curing, degrades cellulose; crosslinking stops yarns moving and bunching in the tear triangle, so strength drops. | 1. Control resin and catalyst dosage, curing temperature and time, using strength retention as the process-window metric;<br>2. Use polyethylene/silicone softeners to improve yarn mobility (retesting seam slippage so it does not worsen); liquid ammonia finishing can improve strength retention at the same durable press grade but must be verified. |\n| **Skew and spirality in knit fabrics** | AATCC 179 | Residual yarn torque (twist multiplier, twist direction), wale skew from many feeders, and skew left uncorrected during heat-setting. | 1. Reduce yarn twist liveliness or alternate S/Z twist feeders;<br>2. Set skew correction during open-width heat-setting, pre-set a counter-skew if needed, and verify by post-wash twist. |\n| **Pilling resistance deteriorates badly after washing** | ASTM D3512 / ASTM D4970 | Uneven staple length, high hairiness, low twist; pills formed by high-tenacity fibers (polyester, nylon) do not break off easily. | 1. Specify combed, compact or Siro-compact spinning, controlling short-fiber content and twist multiplier;<br>2. Cotton and cellulosic fibers can be singed + cellulase bio-polished; **cellulase does not work on polyester or nylon**, which rely on singeing/shearing, low-pill fibers or anti-pilling finishes. |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Common interview questions and answers\n\n> **Interviewer**: \"Bulk delivery is very tight, and the fabric test report shows light fastness or wash shrinkage slightly out of spec (fail). The fabric team and merchandising are pushing to start cutting. As QA lead, how do you decide?\"\n\n### Answer framework\n1. **Classify the risk (separate regulatory/safety red lines from physical performance)**:\n   - **Absolute red lines are non-negotiable**: if it involves hazardous substances (PFAS, lead, azo) or safety requirements, **there is zero tolerance; it is an automatic veto, and cutting is never conceded**.\n2. **Quantify the risk (risk assessment matrix)**:\n   - If wash shrinkage is slightly out of spec (e.g. the requirement is −3.0% and the result is −4.2%):\n     - **First choice: fix it at the fabric**, e.g. re-compact (rubber-blanket/felt compacting, relaxed drying) and retest so the fabric itself meets the requirement;\n     - **Garment-wash option**: if the style allows, add a garment wash to pre-shrink, and reconfirm the pattern against post-wash measurements;\n     - **Adding allowance to the pattern is a concession, not a fix**: enlarging the pattern only brings post-wash measurements back within tolerance; ex-factory measurements will be oversized and the customer's AATCC 135/150 shrinkage test will still fail. It can be executed as a concession only after the customer accepts \"oversized ex-factory measurements + shrinkage deviation\" in writing and the basis for the care label has been assessed.\n   - If light fastness is slightly out of spec (e.g. the customer requires grade 4.0 after 40 AFU of AATCC 16.3 exposure and the result is 3.5):\n     - Assess the end use (for indoor home textiles and underwear, light exposure risk is relatively low; for outdoor curtains or upholstery, light fastness is a core service-life metric).\n     - For high-risk categories, insist that the dye mill apply a UV-absorber after-treatment by padding, and release only after a passing retest.\n3. **Close the loop with sign-off and accountability**:\n   - Any process change or concession/deviation release must come with a complete risk assessment and remediation verification records, and a deviation permit co-signed by the purchasing lead and the buyer's technical representative, so QA never carries hidden liability alone.\n"},"SOP-SOURCING":{"title":"QA and Sourcing Collaboration: Technical Factory Audit, Supplier Quality Onboarding and Performance Scorecard SOP","content":"# QA and Sourcing Collaboration: Technical Factory Audit, Supplier Quality Onboarding and Performance Scorecard SOP\n\n> **Vendor quality management SOP: technical factory audit, QMS assessment, vendor scorecard and QBR**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why QA must work closely with Sourcing\n\nIn today's global supply chains, Sourcing tends to prioritize **unit price (FOB), capacity and lead time**, while QA must act as **the verifier of real capability and the brake on risk**.\nWithout a sound technical factory audit and dynamic supplier tiering, placing difficult orders with low-capability factories leads later to huge rework costs, air freight, claims and buyer chargebacks.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Vendor lifecycle quality governance\n\n```mermaid\ngraph LR\n    A1[1. Initial screening and desk review<br>Desk review & compliance] --> A2[2. On-site technical audit QMS<br>On-site factory audit]\n    A2 --> A3[3. Pilot verification and capacity match<br>Pilot order & capacity match]\n    A3 --> A4[4. Dynamic quality scorecard QBR<br>Quarterly scorecard & tiering]\n    A4 --> A5[5. Develop or phase out<br>Strategic upgrade / phase-out]\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. Technical quality audit model (QMS audit framework, 100 points)\n\nWhen QA audits a new factory (apparel sewing, home-textile sewing and quilting, weaving and dyeing mills) for onboarding, it scores the following **eight core modules**:\n\n| Audit module | Weight | Critical on-site checkpoints | Zero-tolerance / auto-fail items |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. QMS and organization** | 10% | An independent QC department; QC staffing (reference: 1 inline QC per 15–25 sewing operators, more for difficult styles; judge adequacy by the actual escape rate, not the headcount ratio); quality training records; SOP/work instruction coverage. | QC reports to production and has no independent veto. |\n| **2. Incoming inspection and warehouse (IQC & warehouse)** | 15% | 4-point fabric inspection machine, light booth (D65/TL84/CWF), dye-lot segregation (shade band), size of the relaxing area, and protection from humidity, mold and sunlight. | No inspection machine or no controlled light booth; fabric stored outdoors and exposed to moisture. |\n| **3. Sample room and technical preparation** | 10% | CAD pattern system, shrinkage compensation in grading, PP sample sealing and archiving, first-piece and technical briefing capability. | Cutting starts without PP meeting minutes; tech-pack measurements not checked. |\n| **4. Cutting control** | 15% | Automatic spreader tension control, ply limits (heavy fabric ≤ 50 plies, light fabric ≤ 100 plies), top/bottom ply alignment checks, stripe/plaid matching and bundle-ticket traceability. | Top-to-bottom ply deviation beyond the factory QCP (library baseline ±1.0 mm; heavy fabric or customer may set otherwise) and not corrected. |\n| **5. Sewing and assembly process control (sewing IPQC)** | 20% | Line first-piece approval card (signed at the start of every style), station patrol records (every 2 hours), broken-needle control (old-for-new exchange, broken-needle log), destructive pull sampling. | **No broken-needle recovery control; a new needle is issued before the broken one is fully reassembled.** |\n| **6. Washing, finishing and packing** | 10% | Pressing temperature and pressure control, pH neutralization checks on wash liquor, thread trimming and cleanliness, fold symmetry and barcode/carton mark accuracy. | Misplaced barcodes or mixed-up carton packing lists. |\n| **7. Needle detection and safety** | 10% | Separate, fully enclosed needle-detection area; detector test-card check records before, during and after each shift (at customer sensitivity, commonly a 1.0–1.2 mm Fe sphere; nine points, multiple directions); alarm quarantine box with two people and two locks. | **Needle detector broken and out of use; no physical separation in the detection area, with uninspected garments mixed in.** |\n| **8. In-house lab and final inspection (in-house lab & final AQL)** | 10% | In-house routine physical/chemical testing (shrinkage/fastness/strength/pull); independent pre-shipment ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 AQL final inspection reports on file. | Falsified final inspection data; AQL-rejected lots shipped without rework. |\n\n---\n\n### Factory audit grades and onboarding rules\n- **Level A (≥ 85 points)**: **strategic partner**, given priority for high-value, technically complex and large orders; may apply for the DQA self-inspection program, with eligibility determined by the [SYS-003](case:SYS-003) scorecard (Tier 1 or Tier 2, no red lines) and the personnel assessment in [SYS-001](case:SYS-001). DQA self-release is still subject to unannounced audits; it is not an inspection waiver.\n- **Level B (70–84 points)**: **qualified vendor**, may take regular orders; QA assigns follow-up patrol inspection and a corrective action plan (CAP) for weak areas within 30 days.\n- **Level C (60–69 points)**: **conditional vendor**, limited to small orders of simple styles; every lot must pass a final AQL inspection by a third party or resident QA before release.\n- **Level D (< 60 points, or any zero-tolerance item)**: **disqualified**, may not enter the sourcing pool.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Quarterly vendor scorecard and QBR\n\nThe scorecard model, weights, targets, red lines and tier rules **follow the single definition in** [SYS-003 Dynamic Vendor Quality Scorecard](case:SYS-003); this SOP does not define a second version. Key points:\n\n| Dimension | Weight | Data basis |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| FTR (first-time-right final inspection rate) | 35% | Calculated from brand QA, third-party or unannounced audit results; DQA self-inspection results for comparison only |\n| Lab first-pass rate | 20% | By test request; **a regulatory failure is not scored but triggers a red-line review directly** |\n| Vendor-attributable quality return rate | 15% | Returns physically verified as caused by manufacturing defects / units shipped; size/preference, product-page and fulfillment reasons excluded |\n| 8D timeliness and effectiveness | 10% | ICA within 24 h, root cause within 7 days, effectiveness verified as planned; points deducted when actions fail |\n| On-site audit / QMS | 10% | Converted from onboarding and unannounced audit scores |\n| Documentation and traceability | 10% | Test reports, certificates, lot traceability and UFLPA documents on time, complete and verifiable |\n\nEvery dimension is scored by linear interpolation, so no score drops off a cliff to zero at a threshold.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Balancing capacity and quality: preventing overload-driven collapse\n\nIn peak season Sourcing often concentrates too many orders on a few low-cost factories, which then **run overloaded and subcontract without authorization**; this is the biggest cause of quality collapse on US orders.\n- **QA capacity warning formula**:\n  $$\\text{Factory load rate} = \\frac{\\text{Committed standard allowed minutes (SAM)}}{\\text{Available monthly capacity (minutes)}} \\times 100\\%$$\n- Available capacity is actual available working minutes × historical efficiency (not theoretical full output), minus orders already scheduled for other customers.\n- **QA decision rules**:\n  - Load rate $> 85\\%$ (for more than 2 consecutive weeks in peak season): yellow warning; Sourcing must review capacity and delivery before adding orders;\n  - Load rate $> 95\\%$: red warning; in principle no more orders; any addition must be approved, routed to an audited secondary factory, with resident or unannounced checks;\n  - Load rate $> 100\\%$ means sustained overtime or subcontracting and is itself a high-risk signal of unauthorized subcontracting; check subcontracting records, working hours and where WIP is going; unaudited subcontractors may never take part in production.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Common interview questions and answers\n\n> **Interviewer**: \"Sourcing wants to onboard a factory with a very attractive price but weak quality management, and it failed your audit. Business pressure is intense. As quality lead, how do you break the deadlock?\"\n\n### Answer techniques\n1. **Clear principles, without being just a blocker**: \"I understand Sourcing's business goal of reducing cost, but as QA lead I am accountable for delivery risk and brand reputation, and I can't let a short-term cost gain turn into enormous claims later.\"\n2. **Risk-tiered onboarding**:\n   - Don't reject it outright; issue a detailed gap analysis and corrective action plan (GAP analysis & CAP) that names the factory's specific hard gaps in broken-needle control, 4-point inspection and temperature-controlled pressing;\n   - Set a correction window: the factory fixes the high-risk red-line items in hardware and procedures within 15 days, and QA re-audits;\n3. **Put a safety net in place**:\n   - Start small and simple: the first phase allows only pilot orders of simple, non-color-blocked, low-risk basic styles, limited to 20% of the factory's total capacity;\n   - Tightened inspection and resident coaching: a brand resident QA oversees the key operations (cutting first piece, mid-line sewing, final shipment) full time, until the factory's FTR is stable above 95% for 3 consecutive bulk lots; only then is it formally released into the qualified vendor list.\n"},"REF-TEST-MATRIX":{"title":"AATCC / ASTM / ISO Test Methods Quick Reference for Textiles and Home Textiles","content":"# AATCC / ASTM / ISO Test Methods Quick Reference for Textiles and Home Textiles\n\n> **US testing standards protocol matrix: AATCC, ASTM and regulatory testing quick reference**\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> The \"common reference values\" in these tables are magnitudes often seen in US retailer/brand manuals and are for internal screening; **they are not legal limits**. Contract acceptance always follows the method edition, specimen direction, laundering program and decision rules in the customer's tech pack/test manual. Cite current editions; withdrawn standards (e.g. ASTM D434) may no longer be used for acceptance.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Principles\n1. **Write the method and the acceptance criterion separately**: the method says how to test, the criterion says what passes; every criterion states its source (regulation / customer / internal).\n2. **Ordinal results** (gray scales, pilling, smoothness) are ordered data in 0.5-grade steps, evaluated by pass rate, distribution or kappa agreement; **no Cpk is calculated**. Cpk for continuous results (strength, shrinkage, weight) is calculated only once the measurement system is acceptable, the process is stable and there is enough data.\n3. **Conditioning**: before physical testing, condition in the ASTM D1776 standard atmosphere (21 °C, 65% RH, tolerances per the current edition); record the specimen state and conditioning time in the report.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Colorfastness (AATCC)\n\n| Property | Method | Key points | Common reference value (not legal) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Crocking | AATCC 8 (crockmeter); AATCC 116 (rotary, for small areas/prints) | 9 N downward force, 10 strokes; wet crock cloth pick-up must be held at 65 ± 5% (the most sensitive variable) | Dry ≥ 3.5–4; wet ≥ 2.5–3 (often relaxed for dark shades and denim) |\n| Laundering | AATCC 61 (1A/2A/3A, etc.) | 2A: 49 °C, 45 min, steel balls, roughly equal to 5 home medium-temperature washes; 3A: 71 °C | Color change ≥ 4; multifiber staining ≥ 3–3.5; color-blocked styles often ≥ 4 |\n| Light | AATCC 16.3 (xenon-arc) | Apparel commonly 20 AFU; curtains and outdoor 40–80 AFU | ≥ 4 (home curtains at the customer's exposure) |\n| Water | AATCC 107 | 38 °C, 18 h, under pressure | Color change ≥ 4; staining ≥ 3–3.5 |\n| Perspiration | **AATCC 15 (acid perspiration only, pH 4.3)**; use ISO 105-E04 when alkaline perspiration is needed | 38 °C, 6 h, under pressure | Color change ≥ 4; staining ≥ 3–3.5 |\n| Chlorine | AATCC 162 (pool water); AATCC 188 (sodium hypochlorite bleach in laundering) | Swimwear at the customer's available chlorine and number of cycles | Swimwear often ≥ 4 |\n| Non-chlorine bleach | AATCC 172 | Peroxide bleaches | Color change ≥ 4 |\n| Color assessment | **AATCC EP1 Gray Scale for Color Change, EP2 Gray Scale for Staining**; **EP9 visual assessment of color difference**; **EP6 instrumental color measurement**; ΔE CMC(2:1) per ASTM D2244/customer formula | Assess dye-lot variation, set matching and metamerism with EP9/EP6 under multiple illuminants; **do not use EP1 to judge shade lots** | Lab dip ΔE CMC ≤ 0.8–1.0; MI defined by the customer |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Physical and mechanical properties (ASTM)\n\n| Property | Method | Scope | Common reference value (not legal) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Tensile | ASTM D5034 (grab) / D5035 (strip) | Wovens | Lightweight ≥ 25 lbf; regular ≥ 40–50 lbf; workwear ≥ 80–100 lbf |\n| Tear | ASTM D1424 (Elmendorf) / D2261 (tongue) | Wovens | Apparel and bedding often ≥ 2.5–3.5 lbf; denim bodies are usually far higher, with destructed areas specified separately |\n| Burst | ASTM D3786 (hydraulic) / **D3787 (ball burst, common for knits)** | Knits | Underwear on the order of ≥ 40 psi; sweatshirts ≥ 50–75 psi; ball burst is reported in lbf |\n| Pilling | ASTM D3512 (random tumble) / D4970 (Martindale) | All categories | Before washing ≥ 3.5; after washing ≥ 3–3.5 (relaxed per customer for fleece and brushed fabrics) |\n| Woven seams | **ASTM D1683** (failure in sewn seams of woven apparel: slippage, thread break, fabric rupture) | Woven apparel | Per customer: e.g. opening ≤ 6 mm at a specified load, or seam strength ≥ 15–25 lbf |\n| Upholstery seam slippage | **ASTM D4034** | Upholstery fabrics | Per customer |\n| ~~ASTM D434~~ | **Withdrawn in 2004**, no direct replacement | — | Where it appears in historical material, convert to D1683 / D4034 / the customer method |\n| Stretch seams | Customer seam extension/cycling method (specified elongation, number of cycles, gauge length) | High-stretch knits | Per customer; D1683 cannot be used directly to judge knit stretch seams |\n| Abrasion | ASTM D4157 (Wyzenbeek) / D4966 (Martindale) | Upholstery, apparel | General commercial ≥ 15,000 double rubs; heavy commercial ≥ 30,000 |\n| Tuft bind | **ASTM D1335** | Carpets | Common residential floors: cut pile ≥ 3.0 lbf, loop pile ≥ 6.25 lbf (e.g. HUD requirements) |\n| Snaps | **ASTM D7142** (prong-snap holding strength); ASTM D4846 (unsnapping force) | Apparel | Children's wear often ≥ 15 lbf held 10 s (retailer method) |\n| Zippers | ASTM D2061 | Zippers | By size and customer |\n| Salt spray | ASTM B117 | Metal trims | By customer hours and corrosion criteria |\n| Retroreflection | ASTM E810; ANSI/ISEA 107 (contract-specified edition) | High-visibility apparel | Per standard and class |\n| Hydrostatic pressure | AATCC 127 | Waterproof fabrics/seams | Per product claim and customer (seam specimen preparation per customer) |\n| Down-proofness | IDFB down-proofness tests (tumbler/rubbing, per the customer-specified part and edition); EN 12132-1; FTMS 191A 5530 (historical military method) | Down products | Per the customer counting rule, e.g. ≤ 10–15 fibers |\n| Absorbency | AATCC 79 (drop test) / AATCC 198 (horizontal wicking) | Towels, functional fabrics | Towels often ≤ 3–5 s |\n\n> **ASTM D4850 is a textile terminology standard, not a down-proofness method.**\n\n---\n\n## 4. Dimensional stability and appearance\n\n| Property | Method | Common reference value (not legal) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Fabric dimensional change after laundering | AATCC 135 | Wovens −3% to +1%; knits −5% to +2%; bedding −3% to −5% (per customer) |\n| Garment dimensional change after laundering | AATCC 150 | Per key-location tolerances |\n| Twist | AATCC 179 (as %) | ≤ 3–5% |\n| Durable press smoothness | AATCC 124 | ≥ 3.5 for durable press claims |\n| Seam smoothness | AATCC 88B | ≥ 3–3.5 |\n| Wool machine washability | Woolmark TM31 / ISO 6330 + ISO 5077 | Per Woolmark or customer area shrinkage requirement |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Chemical testing (mostly RSL, not federal law)\n\n| Substance | Method | Basis | Common requirement |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Formaldehyde | AATCC 112 (sealed jar release) / ISO 14184-1 (water extraction, corresponding to the Japanese Law 112 method) | **No federal textile formaldehyde limit**; retailer RSL; Prop 65 warning | Infants ≤ 16–20 ppm, skin contact ≤ 75 ppm (state the method: results of the two methods are not directly comparable) |\n| pH | AATCC 81 / ISO 3071 | Customer requirement | 4.0–7.5 or 5.5–7.5 (per customer) |\n| Azo arylamines | EN ISO 14362-1/-3 | EU REACH Annex XVII entry 43, 30 mg/kg; in the US, retailer RSL | RSL often ≤ 20 mg/kg |\n| PFAS | Total organic fluorine (combustion ion chromatography) + targeted LC-MS/MS | California AB 1817 and other state laws | TOF < 100 ppm (< 50 ppm from 2027, California) |\n| Lead | CPSC-CH-E1001/E1002/E1003 | CPSIA (children's products) | Coatings ≤ 90 ppm; substrates ≤ 100 ppm |\n| Cadmium | Validated digestion/ICP method | **No federal apparel cadmium limit**; children's product laws in states such as Washington, retailer RSL; soluble cadmium for toys per ASTM F963 | RSL often ≤ 40–100 ppm (per customer) |\n| Phthalates | CPSC-CH-C1001-09.4 | 16 CFR 1307 (toys and child care articles); Prop 65; RSL | Each ≤ 0.1% |\n| Flame retardants | GC-MS / LC-MS | Prop 65, state laws, RSL | Per the customer banned list |\n| Odor | No single US standard: sensory evaluation per the customer's trained-panel procedure; ISO 17299 is an **instrumental method** (detector tubes/GC) for deodorant performance | Customer requirement | Per customer grade |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Flammability quick reference\n| Regulation | Method essentials |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| 16 CFR 1610 | 45°, 1 s ignition; plain/raised-surface classes; testing exemptions per the regulatory guide |\n| 16 CFR 1615/1616 | Vertical flame, as received and after 50 wash-and-dry cycles, average char length ≤ 7.0 in |\n| 16 CFR 1630/1631 | Methenamine pill test for carpets and rugs |\n| 16 CFR 1632 | Cigarette ignition of mattresses and mattress pads, char length ≤ 2 in |\n| NFPA 701 | Method 1 (≤ 700 g/m², except vinyl-coated blackout linings) / Method 2 |\n| CA TB 117-2013 | Smolder resistance of upholstered furniture cover fabrics, char length ≤ 1.8 in |\n"},"REF-US-REGS":{"title":"US Regulatory and Safety Compliance Guide for Textiles, Home Textiles and Apparel","content":"# US Regulatory and Safety Compliance Guide for Textiles, Home Textiles and Apparel\n\n> **US textile, apparel and home-textile regulatory compliance guide (working reference, not legal advice)**\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> This document helps quality and compliance staff quickly locate the applicable regulations. Regulations are amended, and applicability depends on the product definition, size/age, state of sale and sales channel. Formal determinations must go back to the eCFR text, official CPSC/FTC/CBP guidance, the customer manual and legal counsel. Last content review: 2026-10-26 (review status still pending sign-off by the compliance lead).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview of the US regulatory system\n\n| Agency / level | Main responsibilities (textile-related) | Typical regulations |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **CPSC** | Consumer product safety: flammability, children's products (lead, phthalates, small parts, drawstrings), recalls and certificates | FFA, CPSA, FHSA, CPSIA; 16 CFR 1610/1615/1616/1630/1631/1632/1633, 1120, 1303, 1307 |\n| **FTC** | Truthful labeling and deception: fiber content, country of origin, care, environmental and \"Made in USA\" claims | Textile Act (16 CFR 303), Wool Act (16 CFR 300), Care Labeling Rule (16 CFR 423), Green Guides (16 CFR 260), FTC Act Sec. 5 |\n| **CBP** | Port enforcement: detention, refusal of admission, forced-labor enforcement, assisting CPSC sampling | UFLPA, Section 307 WROs, 19 CFR |\n| **State law** | Chemical warnings and restrictions, stuffed-bedding registration, etc. | California Prop 65, California AB 1817 (PFAS), PFAS and children's-product chemical laws in New York, Washington, Maine and other states |\n\n**Size of penalties (without exaggeration)**: after the 2021 inflation adjustment, the statutory maximum CPSA civil penalty is about **$120,000** per violation and about **$17,150,000** for a related series of violations (adjusted every five years). The vast majority of recalls are voluntary recalls negotiated between the company and CPSC (including Fast Track); there is no FDA-style \"Class I recall\" classification.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Flammability regulations\n\n### 1. 16 CFR Part 1610: flammability of clothing textiles\n- **Scope**: textiles used in adult and children's clothing. Hats, gloves, footwear and certain interlinings are outside the scope per 1610.1; children's sleepwear must also meet 1615/1616.\n- **Method**: specimen held at 45°, 16 mm flame applied for 1 second; tested as received and after one dry-cleaning and one laundering refurbishment as specified.\n- **Classes**:\n  - **Plain surface**: burn time ≥ 3.5 s is Class 1; < 3.5 s is Class 3.\n  - **Raised surface**: burn time > 7.0 s, or 0–7.0 s with surface flash only and no base burn, is Class 1; 4.0–7.0 s with base burn is Class 2; < 4.0 s with base burn is Class 3.\n- **Consequence**: Class 3 is \"dangerously flammable\" and may not enter commerce under the Flammable Fabrics Act; CPSC can require a stop sale and recall and pursue liability.\n- **Testing exemptions (not exemptions from the standard)**:\n  1. **Plain-surface fabrics** (meaning a surface without intentional pile, loops or tufts, **unrelated to a plain weave**) of any fiber content weighing ≥ 2.6 oz/yd² (about 88 g/m²);\n  2. Fabrics of any weight, plain or raised surface, made **entirely** of one or more of acrylic, modacrylic, nylon, olefin, polyester and wool.\n  - **Silk, cotton, viscose and their blends with the fibers above are not on the exemption list**; the exemption is from the testing obligation, and the product must still meet the standard. Where adult clothing falls under an exemption, CPSC has stated it is exercising enforcement discretion on the GCC requirement, but the company should still keep evidence of fiber content.\n\n### 2. 16 CFR Parts 1615 / 1616: children's sleepwear\n- **Scope**: 1615 covers sizes 0–6X and 1616 sizes 7–14 (by size, not age). Infant garments 9 months and under that meet the dimensional conditions, and tight-fitting sleepwear that meets the dimension table, are not required to be flame resistant.\n- **Requirement**: average char length of 5 specimens ≤ 7.0 in (17.8 cm), with no single specimen charring the full length (10.0 in); fabric, seams and trims tested as received and **after the 50 wash-and-dry cycles specified in the standard**.\n- **Tight-fitting sleepwear**: must meet the maximum dimension table for each location, with a hangtag and permanent label in the format prescribed by 1615/1616; the warning reads:\n  > *\"For child's safety, garment should fit snugly. This garment is not flame resistant. Loose-fitting garment is more likely to catch fire.\"*\n  Hangtag color, type size and placement follow the regulatory text.\n\n### 3. Carpets / mattresses / furniture\n| Regulation | Scope | Core requirement |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **16 CFR 1630 / 1631** | Large carpets (area > 24 ft² and one dimension > 6 ft) / small rugs | Methenamine pill test for surface flammability; small rugs that fail may be sold with a warning label. Changing the backing adhesive, pile yarn or backing requires reconfirmation |\n| **16 CFR 1632** | Mattresses and **mattress pads** | Cigarette ignition: char length ≤ 2 in from the nearest point of the cigarette; prototype testing and records |\n| **16 CFR 1633** | Mattresses and mattress sets (not mattress pads) | Open-flame test |\n| **California TB 117-2013** | Upholstered furniture cover fabrics and filling | Cigarette smolder: cover fabric char length ≤ 1.8 in (45 mm); TB 117-2013 compliance label and SB 1019 flame retardant disclosure statement |\n| **NFPA 701** | Curtains, draperies, etc. (when cited by project/building codes) | See section 5 |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Children's products (CPSIA / FHSA)\n\n**Definition of a children's product**: designed or intended primarily for children 12 and under. Children's products must be tested by a **CPSC-accepted third-party lab** and certified with a **CPC** issued by the US importer or domestic manufacturer; material, supplier or process changes that could affect compliance require retesting (16 CFR 1107/1109), and tracking labels are required on the product and packaging (CPSIA Sec. 103).\n\n### 1. Total lead\n| Category | Limit | Regulation | Method |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Paint, ink and surface coatings (prints, painted zipper pulls, baked-enamel metal buttons) | ≤ 90 ppm | CPSIA Sec. 101(f); 16 CFR 1303 | CPSC-CH-E1003 |\n| Accessible substrates (metal buttons, zipper teeth, plastic parts, faux leather) | ≤ 100 ppm | CPSIA Sec. 101(a) | CPSC-CH-E1001 (metal) / E1002 (non-metal) |\n- Under 16 CFR 1500.91, dyed or undyed textile fibers themselves (excluding coatings, prints, metal or plastic components) have been determined not to exceed the lead limits and are exempt from third-party lead testing; trims, coatings and prints are not.\n- XRF is screening only; determinations rely on an accredited lab's digestion/ICP method.\n\n### 2. Phthalates (16 CFR 1307)\n- Restricts 8 phthalates (DEHP, DBP, BBP, DINP, DIBP, DPENP, DHEXP, DCHP), each ≤ 0.1%.\n- **The statutory scope is children's toys and child care articles only** (products designed to help children 3 and under sleep, eat, suck or teethe). CPSC guidance treats sleepwear and bibs for children 3 and under as child care articles. **Ordinary children's apparel (rainwear, printed T-shirts, etc.) is outside the statutory scope of 1307**, but retailer RSLs usually set equal or stricter requirements, which should be followed per the customer manual.\n\n### 3. Drawstrings (16 CFR 1120.3 / ASTM F1816)\n- Children's upper outerwear in sizes **2T–12**: hood/neck drawstrings are deemed a substantial product hazard (SPH) and **must not be used**.\n- Waist/bottom drawstrings in sizes **2T–16**: must meet ASTM F1816, i.e. no more than 3 in exposed when the garment is expanded flat, no toggles/knots or other attachments on the free ends, and drawstrings through a channel bartacked at the midpoint, among other requirements.\n- Hood/neck drawstrings in sizes **14–16**: neither 16 CFR 1120.3 nor ASTM F1816 addresses them, so they are neither legally prohibited nor legally conditioned; whether they are allowed follows customer requirements or internal rules. Size 12 (and L) falls in the prohibited 2T–12 range; if a labeled size range covers any size from 2T–12 (e.g. 10–14), the whole garment is treated as in the prohibited range. PLM rules should be set by size range rather than \"all children's wear\".\n- 16 CFR 1120.3 is a CPSA 15(j) rule, not a consumer product safety rule, and does not trigger third-party testing or a CPC; drawstrings are controlled by design rules and physical checks, and the CPC covers only the applicable children's product safety rules such as lead.\n\n### 4. Small parts and attachment strength\n- 16 CFR 1501 (small-parts cylinder: 1.25 in inside diameter, slanted bottom 1.0–2.25 in deep) applies to toys and articles for children under 3 and **expressly exempts children's clothing and accessories**. However, if detachable buttons, snaps or decorations on clothing create a choking risk, CPSC can still treat them as a substantial product hazard under the FHSA/CPSA Section 15.\n- Industry practice: reference the use-and-abuse pull/torque conditions of 16 CFR 1500.51–1500.53, or a retailer method (commonly 15 lbf held 10 s); use **ASTM D7142** for prong-ring snap holding strength and ASTM D4846 for unsnapping force. Specific force values follow the customer manual.\n\n---\n\n## 4. FTC labeling regulations\n\n### 1. 16 CFR Part 423: care labeling\n- At least **one** complete regular care method must be given: washing (or dry-cleaning), drying, ironing, bleaching, plus reasonably foreseeable warnings (e.g. \"Wash with like colors\").\n- **Reasonable basis**: may be reliable testing of the product or fabric, past experience, industry expertise, etc.; the rule does **not** require third-party testing and does **not** specify a number of launderings (\"5 washes\" is a company or customer verification practice). It is a violation if following the label damages the product or the instructions lack a sufficient basis.\n- **Temperature terms** (Appendix A): Cold ≤ 86 °F (30 °C); Warm 87–111 °F (31–44 °C); Hot 112–145 °F (45–63 °C). Appendix A gives only verbal definitions of cool/warm/hot iron; the common 110 °C, 150 °C and 200 °C settings come from the iron dots in the ASTM D5489 care symbol guide, not from the FTC rule.\n\n### 2. 16 CFR Part 303: fiber content and country of origin\n- Use FTC-recognized generic fiber names; bamboo-derived viscose must be labeled with a compliant term such as \"rayon (made from bamboo)\", never as \"bamboo\" fiber.\n- Fibers ≥ 5% must be listed by name (with rule-based exceptions for functional elastic fibers and the like); fibers < 5% may be listed as \"other fiber\".\n- **Tolerance**: the stated percentages may deviate by **±3 percentage points**; the tolerance does not apply to products labeled \"100%\" or \"All\". Ornamentation of no more than 5% may be stated as \"exclusive of ornamentation\".\n- Country of origin: garments with a neck must carry it on the inside center of the neck midway between the shoulder seams or close to the neck; an RN number or company name is required.\n- Wool products are also subject to the Wool Products Labeling Act (16 CFR 300).\n\n### 3. Environmental and origin claims\n- **Green Guides (16 CFR 260)**: claims such as \"recycled\", \"GRS\" and \"sustainable\" need competent substantiation; recycled content must be stated at the actual proportion, with certificates and chain-of-custody evidence kept.\n- **Made in USA**: 16 CFR 323 requires \"all or virtually all\" made in the US.\n\n### 4. Feather and down products\n- The FTC \"Guides for the Feather and Down Products Industry\" (former 16 CFR 253) were **rescinded in August 1998**; there is currently no dedicated federal down labeling rule. Down content, fill power, \"hypoallergenic\", origin and RDS claims are governed by the anti-deception principle of FTC Act Section 5 and must be substantiated.\n- The industry usually sets acceptance values for down cluster content, fill power, cleanliness (turbidity), oxygen number and odor using the IDFB testing regulations and retailer specifications; these are **industry/customer requirements, not federal statutory limits**.\n- Some states (e.g. Pennsylvania, Massachusetts, Ohio) have registration or statutory label requirements for stuffed bedding and plush products; verify for each state of sale.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Home textiles and contract projects\n\n### 1. NFPA 701 (curtains, draperies)\n- NFPA 701 is a test method standard; whether it is mandatory depends on the building occupancy, local codes and the authority having jurisdiction (AHJ). Retail home curtains generally have no federal mandatory requirement.\n- **Method 1**: for single- or multi-layer fabrics with areal density ≤ 700 g/m², but **vinyl-coated blackout linings and lined curtains using them require Method 2**; criteria are mean afterflame of drips ≤ 2.0 s, mean weight loss ≤ 40%, and no specimen deviating from the mean by more than 3 standard deviations.\n- **Method 2**: fabrics > 700 g/m², films, multi-layer assemblies and the coated linings above.\n- Flame-resistant products claimed as washable or dry-cleanable should be retested after the cleaning/durability procedure specified in the standard; a test report is not an NFPA \"certification\".\n\n### 2. Mattress pads, weighted blankets, etc.\n- 16 CFR 1632.1 defines a mattress pad as \"a thin, flat mat or cushion, and/or ticking filled with resilient material for use on top of a mattress\", including absorbent pads. Filled mattress pads are clearly covered; no specific CPSC interpretation was found on whether waterproof protectors without a fill layer fall under the definition, and industry usually treats them as covered (cigarette-ignition prototype testing, records kept, GCC issued). Specific determinations follow the registry and compliance counsel.\n- Weighted blankets currently have no dedicated federal mandatory standard, but CPSC has issued safety warnings about weighted products for infants and young children; products should be labeled as not for infants or for children unable to remove the blanket themselves, and should reference ASTM standards under development and retailer requirements.\n\n---\n\n## 6. State law and chemicals\n\n### 1. California Proposition 65\n- Prop 65 is a **warning obligation**, not a ban limit: if exposure exceeds the safe harbor level (NSRL/MADL), products sold in California must carry a warning in the regulatory format (the standard warning with the yellow triangle, or the short-form warning). Where a company cannot show exposure below the safe harbor, it usually manages to the concentration benchmarks in settlements (e.g. ≤ 1,000 ppm for certain phthalates, migration or content benchmarks for BPA in socks and sports bras).\n- Enforcement is mainly through **60-day notices** from private enforcers, with settlements and attorney fees ranging from tens of thousands to hundreds of thousands of dollars.\n- The short-form warning content was revised from 2025, with a transition period for the old format; verify label updates against the latest text.\n\n### 2. PFAS\n- **California AB 1817**: from 2025-01-01 intentionally added PFAS are banned in textile articles, and \"regulated PFAS\" is determined by **total organic fluorine (TOF)**: ≥ 100 ppm (through 2026-12-31) and ≥ 50 ppm from 2027-01-01. Outdoor apparel for severe wet conditions has transition and disclosure provisions through 2028.\n- New York, Washington, Maine and other states also restrict PFAS in apparel or textiles; verify thresholds and timelines under each state's law.\n- **QA response**: require no-intentional-addition declarations and TOF testing from chemical suppliers; fluorine-free DWR has higher surface energy than fluorinated DWR, so water repellency and wash durability must be re-verified, as must compatibility with downstream processes such as seam taping.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Import and supply-chain compliance\n\n### 1. UFLPA (Uyghur Forced Labor Prevention Act)\n- Since 2022-06-21, CBP applies a **rebuttable presumption** to goods produced wholly or in part in Xinjiang or by entities on the UFLPA Entity List: they are denied entry unless the importer proves by \"clear and convincing evidence\" that forced labor was not involved.\n- Cotton products (including blends) are high risk. QA/compliance should build a tier-by-tier traceability package from garment to fabric, yarn and cotton fiber (gin/origin): purchase contracts, invoices, packing lists, production records and transport documents. Isotope and similar testing can only supplement, never replace, the document chain.\n\n### 2. CPSC certificate eFiling\n- Under the final rule published on 2025-01-08, imported products subject to a CPSC rule and requiring a certificate must have certificate data filed electronically with CBP/CPSC at entry from **2026-07-08** (from 2027-01-08 for entries through foreign trade zones).\n- A GCC applies only to products subject to a CPSC rule, ban or standard (e.g. adult clothing subject to 1610 without a testing exemption, mattress pads subject to 1632, carpets subject to 1630/1631); not \"every product\" needs a certificate.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Key points for QA\n1. Define the product first (children's/adult, sleepwear/daywear, size range, state of sale, whether it is a contract project), then match the regulations.\n2. Distinguish **legal requirements**, **retailer/brand requirements** and **industry practice**; never blur the three into a \"regulatory limit\".\n3. With every material, supplier or process change, decide whether it triggers retesting, a reissued certificate or a label update.\n4. Regulatory provisions follow the eCFR text; for verification of the values in this document, see the sources in the registries.\n"},"INT-CONFLICT":{"title":"Cross-Functional Quality Conflicts: Scenario Casebook","content":"# Cross-Functional Quality Conflicts: Scenario Casebook\n\n> **Mastering high-stakes supply chain conflicts: delivery vs quality, cost vs compliance, design vs DFQ**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why quality director interviews always test cross-functional conflict\n\nIn a real business, QA always sits between competing interests:\n- **PDD (design and development)**: chases striking, novel designs and can overlook material compatibility and manufacturability;\n- **Sourcing (purchasing/merchandising)**: measured on unit cost and on-time delivery (OTD), and sees strict QA as an obstacle to shipping;\n- **Vendors/factories**: pursue production profit and scheduling efficiency, and tend to hide process variation and pressure the buyer;\n- **Compliance / QA**: responsible for the brand's legal compliance and its reputation with consumers.\n\n**What the interviewer is testing**: are you a **rigid inspector who can only say no**, or a **leader with business perspective and materials-engineering knowledge, who quantifies risk with data and brings alternative solutions**?\n\n---\n\n## 2. Scenario 1: delivery vs quality. On the eve of Black Friday, a 100,000-piece bulk order fails final AQL badly\n\n### 1. The dilemma\n- **Background**: 100,000 outdoor fleece jackets (goods value $2,500,000) are Black Friday merchandise, and the customer's latest in-DC date is **24 days away**. From the factory to a US West Coast DC, the expedited vessel takes about 11 days, plus about 5 days for booking, customs clearance and DC receiving, so the factory must ship **within 7 days** at the latest.\n- **The conflict**: during brand QA's ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 final inspection, **severe skipped stitches, wavy puckering at the zipper and open seams at the pocket edges** are found; Major defects are 3 times the AQL 2.5 limit, and QA rules **reject**.\n- **The positions**:\n  - **Sales VP**: \"If we miss the in-DC date, the customer will charge us under the contract or even cancel, and the Black Friday slot will be empty. QA must release it now; we'll sort out the problems in the US!\"\n  - **Factory manager**: \"Workers stayed up all night to make these. They're all minor workmanship issues that don't affect wear. Stop nitpicking!\"\n\n---\n\n### 2. A QA lead's five-step way out\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    Step1[1. Cool things down and classify the facts<br>separate regulatory/safety items from appearance/function] --> Step2[2. Stratify defects and sort fast<br>Pareto to find the defect sources behind 80%]\n    Step2 --> Step3[3. Plan staged release while sorting<br>release in waves by store shipping priority]\n    Step3 --> Step4[4. Mobilize targeted rework with fixtures<br>QA on site full time to re-inspect]\n    Step4 --> Step5[5. Sign a compliant concession and risk agreement<br>name commercial responsibility and cost split]\n```\n\n1. **Step 1: classify quickly and hold the safety floor**\n   - Immediately check for legal red lines such as broken needles or heavy-metal exceedances (confirmed: no safety or regulatory defects, purely sewing workmanship variation).\n2. **Step 2: data-driven Pareto stratification (defect categorization)**\n   - Four senior inspectors quickly sort a 1,000-piece sample from the rejected lot:\n     - **72% of defects are puckering at the center-front zipper**, caused by excessive presser-foot pressure on the feed dog when sewing the zipper;\n     - Only **8% are serious open pocket seams**; the remaining 20% are loose threads and thread ends that can be cleared quickly.\n3. **Step 3: a staged-release plus targeted-rework rescue plan**\n   - No \"the whole 100,000 waits\": the factory immediately forms a **50-person rework team** that unpicks and reworks only the zipper and open pocket seams;\n   - The 100,000 pieces are split into **4 waves of 25,000 each** by US warehouse priority;\n   - The first 25,000 pieces are reworked and every piece re-inspected within 36 hours and shipped on the original booking; each remaining wave is completed every 36–48 hours, all within the 7-day shipping window. Each reworked wave gets a new final AQL inspection and is released only if it passes;\n4. **Step 4: logistics recovery (expedited ocean instead of air)**\n   - With Logistics, the last three waves are rebooked from standard ocean to an expedited US West Coast service (about 11 days, roughly 5–7 days faster than the standard service), absorbing the time lost to rework without air freight. The expedited space and the freight difference were confirmed before the decision.\n5. **Step 5: close the loop and review accountability**\n   - All 100,000 pieces were received before the in-DC date; after launch, the verified workmanship return rate was in line with similar styles;\n   - The factory bore the expedited freight difference and the rework labor; at the review, \"zipper puckering\" was added to that factory's PFMEA and first-piece check, and QA built trust with the business team.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Scenario 2: cost vs compliance. Purchasing switches a trim supplier without approval to cut cost\n\n### 1. The dilemma\n- **Background**: facing an 8% annual cost-down KPI, Purchasing switched the metal snaps on an infant one-piece (PO 80,000 pieces, a children's product) from the original approved supplier to a cheaper local trim maker without telling QA, saving $0.15 per piece ($12,000 for the order).\n- **The conflict**:\n  With 30,000 pieces already produced, QA's line sampling (customer children's-wear attachment method referencing the use-and-abuse pull conditions of 16 CFR 1500.51–1500.53; the customer requires 15 lbf held for 10 seconds; prong-snap strength referencing ASTM D7142) finds the snaps come off at **11 lbf after 5 seconds**, and a detached snap fits completely inside the small-parts test cylinder. 16 CFR 1501 itself exempts children's clothing, but detachable small parts on infant clothing are treated by CPSC as a choking hazard and can be handled as a substantial product hazard: a **critical defect**.\n  In addition, the snaps are a new part from a new supplier, so the third-party testing behind the original CPC (including lead) no longer covers them.\n- **Purchasing's argument**: \"We've already made 80,000 pieces and the unit price is very low. Replacing every snap would lose tens of thousands of dollars, and adults always watch babies when they're dressed. It'll be fine.\"\n\n---\n\n### 2. A QA lead's strategy\n1. **Escalate to executives with regulatory facts**\n   - No private compromise with Purchasing. Compile public CPSC recall notices for snaps detaching from infant clothing as factual evidence, issue a stop-shipment notice, and copy the Purchasing VP, Legal and the Supply Chain VP; explain that this is not only a pull-strength problem but also invalidates the basis of the CPC.\n2. **Mechanism analysis and disposition**\n   - Dissect the failed snaps on site: the metal did not break; the **snap prongs were too short and the setting die pressure too low**, so they could not penetrate and grip the infant's double-layer cotton binding.\n   - Disposition: after removing the failed snaps, the fabric at the original holes was damaged. A re-setting trial on 50 pieces (adding a reinforcing interlining and using the original approved supplier's snaps) gave ASTM D7142 holding strength of 13–16 lbf with wide scatter, which cannot reliably meet 15 lbf. Infant products leave no room for concession, so the 30,000 pieces already produced were **scrapped** (about $135,000 on an FOB basis, 11 times the $12,000 \"cost saving\"); the 50,000 unproduced pieces used the original supplier's snaps;\n   - The new snap lot was tested for lead at a CPSC-accepted lab and the CPC reissued; the snap-setting machines were fitted with digital air-pressure sensors and interlocks, and the dies adjusted.\n3. **Set up joint accountability for trim engineering-change (ECN) violations**\n   - Embed it in policy: replacing a children's-wear trim supplier must go through an ECN and may be used only after pull testing, third-party chemical testing and a CPC update; unauthorized substitutions are handled under the policy, with rework cost borne by whoever is responsible. Cost-down KPIs are also recalculated as \"net savings after quality and compliance costs\".\n\n---\n\n## 4. Scenario 3: design vs DFQ. A bold color-blocked design on a fabric highly prone to color transfer\n\n### 1. The dilemma\n- **Background**: the Design Director created a visually striking black-and-white high-contrast color-blocked athletic sweatshirt (large solid black and pure white panels across the chest).\n- **The conflict**:\n  At the proto gate QA runs AATCC 61 2A and sews black and white panels into a small mock-up for a garment wash: staining of the black knit onto the white cotton is only **grade 2.0** (the buyer requires ≥ 4.0 for color-blocked styles). After washing, the white areas look gray, with obvious color transfer.\n- **Design's argument**: \"This is our runway hero style for the new season. The black-and-white contrast is the soul of the collection. We absolutely can't change the colors or switch to a print! QA, figure it out!\"\n\n---\n\n### 2. A QA lead's strategy (from blocker to technical partner)\n\n```\n[Original design]: ordinary reactive-dyed black cotton knit + white cotton, black fabric soaped only once ===> color transfer after garment wash (grade 2.0)\n                                  │\n                                  ▼ (QA with fabric engineering)\n[QA solution]:\n  1. Black fabric: switch to a high-fixation reactive black combination, multiple soapings + fixing, end point judged by soaping liquor color and staining grade (staining 4.0–4.5)\n  2. White fabric: keep pure white cotton, verified with a color-blocked garment wash using the same lot; no piping in the seam (dye moves through the wash liquor, so piping cannot stop it)\n  3. Care label: add \"Wash with like colors / Wash inside out\" warnings, as a supplement rather than a substitute\n```\n\n- **Negotiation and presentation**:\n  - QA brings **2 sets of washed color-blocked samples** to the design review:\n    - Sample A (original approach): white areas visibly gray after 5 washes;\n    - Sample B (improved dyeing and finishing): crisp black and white after 5 washes, staining 4.0–4.5, with the same hand as the original;\n  - The Design Director approves the look and hand of sample B and the solution goes ahead; fabric cost rises by about $0.12 per yard, which Merchandising accepts.\n- **Interview takeaway**:\n  > \"Good QA never kills a designer's creativity; it **uses hard engineering in materials physics and dyeing chemistry to give ambitious designs a safe way to reach production**.\"\n\n---\n\n## 5. Scenario 4: delivery vs specification. Fabric shrinkage is over spec, and the business wants to \"skip testing and cut on concession\"\n\n### 1. The dilemma\n- Before peak season, the fabric for 200,000 knit sweatshirts shows lengthwise shrinkage of −5.5% (customer spec −3.0%). Merchandising and Purchasing ask to \"skip testing and cut on concession\".\n\n### 2. Approach\n1. **Quantify the risk**: within 4 hours, wash 5 garments; body length shrinks about 3.5 cm after washing, close to a full size.\n2. **Fix the fabric first**: the mill re-compacts the uncut fabric on a felt compactor; the retest is −2.6%, a pass. Reprocessing capacity in 3 days covers only 188,000 pieces' worth.\n3. **Concede honestly on the remainder**: fabric for the other 12,000 pieces is already relaxing at the garment factory awaiting cutting, and sending it back would miss the vessel. Explain to the customer what adding pattern allowance means: post-wash measurements return to tolerance, but ex-factory measurements are oversized and the garment shrinkage test still fails. The customer agrees to the concession in writing, and the basis for the care label is updated.\n4. **Sign a deviation form**: Merchandising, Purchasing, QA and the pattern room co-sign it, stating responsibility and costs.\n\n### 3. Result\n188,000 pieces were produced to spec and 12,000 shipped under the written concession; the overall delay was 3 days with no air freight; after launch, size-related returns were in line with similar styles.\n\n> Key point: adding pattern allowance is not a fix, only a concession; the customer must know and agree in writing.\n"},"INT-QA-BANK":{"title":"Common Interview Questions and Answer Frameworks for Quality Directors and Senior QA in Apparel and Home Textiles","content":"# Common Interview Questions and Answer Frameworks for Quality Directors and Senior QA in Apparel and Home Textiles\n\n> **10 high-frequency QA leadership interview questions and answer frameworks (US market focused)**\n\n---\n\n## Module 1: Strategy, system building and leadership\n\n### Q1: How do you define the core value of an excellent QA team in an apparel/home-textile brand's supply chain?\n- **Ordinary answer**: \"QA means strict inspection: finding loose threads, holes and measurement problems so defective goods don't ship to customers.\"\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > \"I see QA's core value as **moving from a reactive 'quality police' role after the fact to a lifecycle quality enabler and risk architect (quality architect and business enabler)**.\n  > Excellent QA rests on three pillars:\n  > 1. **Prevention up front (DFQ & prevention)**: engage in design and fabric development, identifying and removing manufacturability and compliance risks as far as possible before the tech pack is frozen;\n  > 2. **Lower cost and faster flow (COPQ & speed)**: reduce the cost of poor quality and shorten lead times by running supplier DQA self-inspection certification and standardizing processes;\n  > 3. **Brand compliance firewall (brand reputation & compliance)**: hold firm on CPSC/CPSIA/FTC/Prop 65 and UFLPA requirements, controlling regulatory risk at design, sourcing and release to reduce recall and detention risk.\"\n\n---\n\n### Q2: If you joined us as QA Head, what would your first 90 days look like (30-60-90 day plan)?\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > - **First 30 days (listen, learn and assess: diagnosis and baseline)**:\n  >   - Map the current state: current tech packs, AATCC/ASTM inspection standards, QCPs and lab testing workflow;\n  >   - Dig into data: cost of poor quality (COPQ) over the last 1–2 years, a Pareto of return reasons, the top 5 complaint pain points and supplier inspection pass rates (FTR);\n  >   - Cross-functional interviews: talk with PDD, Sourcing, Raw Material and key supplier leaders to find process breaks and collaboration pain points.\n  > - **Days 30–60 (prioritize, pilot and align: choose the battles and pilot improvements)**:\n  >   - Pick 2–3 high-impact breakthrough projects (e.g. an 8D or DMAIC on frequent pilling/shrinkage problems);\n  >   - Upgrade the supplier quality scorecard and tiering criteria, and set up a cross-functional DFQ stage-gate sign-off;\n  >   - Pilot DQA self-inspection and a standardized PP meeting with 3–5 leading factories.\n  > - **Days 60–90 (scale, standardize and deliver: embed the system and roll out)**:\n  >   - Lock in results: publish a controlled quality SOP manual and add quality metrics to the quarterly supplier QBR;\n  >   - Digital enablement: move mobile inspection and lab data to the cloud, and present early quality ROI to management (e.g. FTR up 5%, complaints down 20%).\"\n\n---\n\n### Q3: With a supply chain shipping tens of millions of units a year, how do you balance limited QA headcount with high quality standards?\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > \"The core strategy is **risk-based quality governance plus supplier DQA enablement**, not blanket inspection of everything:\n  > 1. **Product risk tiering**: separate high-risk categories (e.g. children's wear, high-stretch high-density fabrics, color-blocked styles, functional coatings) from low-risk basics. High-risk categories get full-time on-site oversight and tightened lot sampling;\n  > 2. **Vendor tiering**: factories at scorecard Tier 1/Tier 2 with no red lines and with DQA staff who have passed assessment run DQA self-release (eligibility per [SYS-003](case:SYS-003) and [SYS-001](case:SYS-001)), and brand QA moves to 15–20% unannounced audits; Tier C factories get tightened resident inspection of every lot, with the factory paying the inspection cost;\n  > 3. **Move the gates upstream and control the source**: shift most QA resources from end-of-line inspection to fabric and trim release plus first-piece confirmation at the PP meeting, eliminating lot-wide defects at the source and lowering the end-of-line inspection load (the exact split depends on category risk and supplier tier).\"\n\n---\n\n## Module 2: Quality tools and technical root cause analysis\n\n### Q4: Explain in detail how you use FMEA in apparel development. How does it reduce bulk production risk?\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > \"We run a **two-track prevention approach, DFMEA + PFMEA**:\n  > - In the **DFMEA (design stage)**: QA reviews the tech pack with PDD. For example, when developing color-blocked yogawear, we identified the failure mode 'dark dye migrating in washing (S=7)' and removed the design flaw by requiring laundering staining $\\ge 4.0$ for dark fabrics in the design specification and specifying a 'Wash Separately' care label;\n  > - In the **PFMEA (production readiness)**: for flatlock seams on high-stretch fabric we identified 'seam bursting (S=8, O=5, D=5, RPN=200)'. The root cause was insufficient thread reserve in the looper thread, not 'insufficient thread elongation'. The actions were textured nylon looper thread, SPI 12–14 with tension and differential feed locked using a seam extension curve, light ball-point needles, and first-piece elongation cycling per machine per shift. After the actions, trial data supported O=3 and D=4, RPN 96. Sample testing cannot justify D = 1–2.\"\n\n---\n\n### Q5: A customer reports widespread seam slippage or insufficient tear strength after washing. What are your 8D steps?\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > \"I would start a standard closed-loop 8D immediately:\n  > 1. **D2 quantified description**: quantify the strength loss and seam opening load using ASTM D1424 (tear) and the customer's finished-seam method (referencing ASTM D1683; ASTM D434 was withdrawn in 2004), stratified by finishing lot;\n  > 2. **D3 containment**: lock all goods in transit and in US warehouses and stop cutting at the factory, so the defect does not spread;\n  > 3. **D4 6M root cause**: analyze the physical/chemical mechanism: did excess resin for durable press appearance cause acid degradation and embrittlement of the cellulose, or did slick high-count yarn and too-narrow seam allowances (< 1/4\") let yarns pull out?\n  > 4. **D5–D6 permanent fix and verification**: lower the resin and curing temperature, switch to a mainly polyethylene softener (balancing tear and slippage), and change sewing to French seams with the allowance widened to 1/2\"; at the same time decide the disposition of finished fabric and goods: strength loss from crosslinking is irreversible and rework can only improve the seams. Close the 8D only after consecutive production lots verify the fix;\n  > 5. **D7 embed in the system**: update the 'High-Density Fabric Durable Press Finishing and Sewing Engineering SOP' and make fabric tear strength a mandatory green-card prerequisite for bulk cutting.\"\n\n---\n\n### Q6: What is the core mechanism behind dimensional change (shrinkage) and leg twist/skew (spirality) in bulk knitwear? How do you control it systematically?\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > \"- **Mechanism**: knit fabric is made of interlooped stitches, and yarn twist leaves residual torque from circular knitting; if lengthwise overfeed is insufficient during tentering and heat-setting, the fabric is held under lengthwise tension. In home machine washing (AATCC 135 water and agitation), the internal stress is released at once: the loops contract, causing heavy lengthwise shrinkage, while residual torque tilts the loops, so the side seams twist sharply toward the front or back (leg twist).\n  > - **Corrective measures**:\n  >   1. **At the yarn**: feed alternating S/Z twist yarns to cancel residual torque;\n  >   2. **Dyeing and finishing engineering**: the stenter must use **sufficient overfeed of +25% to +30%**, followed by a rubber-blanket compactor to release shrinkage stress; preset a 2–3% counter-skew angle during tentering (skew setting);\n  >   3. **Cutting control**: enforce **tension-free relaxation for 24–36 hours** so cut parts are not stretched.\"\n\n---\n\n## Module 3: US regulations, compliance and recall defense\n\n### Q7: Name the 3 most critical US regulatory red lines for childrenswear. How should QA build defenses?\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > \"The three most critical red lines are:\n  > 1. **16 CFR 1120.3 & ASTM F1816 (children's drawstrings)**: no hood/neck drawstrings on upper outerwear in sizes 2T–12; waist/bottom drawstrings in 2T–16 exposed ≤ 3 inches with no toggles or knots on free ends; hood/neck drawstrings in 14–16 are not addressed by regulation and follow customer requirements (size 12/L is in the prohibited range). *Defense: size-range rules in the PLM and compliance sign-off at the PP sample.*\n  > 2. **CPSIA lead and third-party testing/CPC**: lead in coatings ≤ 90 ppm (Sec. 101(f)), in substrates ≤ 100 ppm; children's products must be tested by a CPSC-accepted lab and certified with a CPC, and trim changes require retesting. The statutory scope of phthalates (16 CFR 1307, each of 8 ≤ 0.1%) is toys and child care articles (incl. sleepwear and bibs for ages 3 and under); ordinary children's apparel follows the customer RSL. *Defense: test trims by color/lot, screen with XRF at IQC, confirm positives with the CPSC methods.*\n  > 3. **16 CFR 1615/1616 (children's sleepwear)**: sleepwear in sizes 0–14 (by size, not age) must have average char length ≤ 7.0 inches, tested as received and after 50 wash-and-dry cycles. The tight-fitting exemption requires meeting the dimension table, with a hangtag and permanent warning label in the regulatory format.\"\n\n---\n\n### Q8: What specific requirements do California Proposition 65 and California AB 1817 (the PFAS ban) place on our textile exports?\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > \"- **California Prop 65**: it is a **warning obligation**, not a ban limit. The list covers nearly a thousand carcinogens/reproductive toxicants (e.g. lead, cadmium, BPA, certain phthalates, flame retardants such as TCEP/TDCPP). If exposure exceeds the safe harbor level (NSRL/MADL), products sold in California must carry a warning in the regulatory format; otherwise a private enforcer may send a 60-day notice and seek a settlement. In practice, companies mostly manage to the concentration benchmarks in settlement agreements;\n  > - **California AB 1817 (PFAS)**: from 2025, intentionally added PFAS are banned in textile articles, judged by **total organic fluorine (TOF)**: ≥ 100 ppm is regulated PFAS, falling to 50 ppm from 2027. QA requires no-intentional-addition declarations and TOF testing from chemical suppliers and phases out C6/C8 fluorinated repellent finishes; after switching to fluorine-free DWR, water repellency, wash durability and seam-tape compatibility all need re-verification.\"\n\n---\n\n### Q9: What is the 'reasonable basis' principle of the FTC Care Labeling Rule (16 CFR Part 423)?\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > \"FTC 16 CFR 423 requires care instructions on the label to have a **reasonable basis**. The basis can be reliable testing of the product or fabric, past experience or industry expertise; the rule does not require a third-party lab and does not specify a number of launderings.\n  > In practice, QA verifies instructions such as 'Machine Wash Warm, Tumble Dry Medium' by AATCC 135/150 (company or customer practice is usually 3–5 cycles) and keeps records of dimensions, colorfastness and appearance, while observing the FTC's temperature definitions (Warm is 87–111 °F, i.e. 31–44 °C). If following the label damages the product, or there is no basis, it is a violation, and the FTC can seek civil penalties under the rule.\"\n\n---\n\n## Module 4: Cross-functional collaboration, conflict resolution and supply-chain negotiation\n\n### Q10: Sourcing and the factory say \"make the vessel or face huge air freight\" and pressure you to sign a concession releasing a defective bulk lot. What do you do?\n- **Director-level answer**:\n  > \"I take a four-step approach: **firm principles, quantified data, alternative engineering solutions, tiered decisions**:\n  > 1. **Hold the legal safety floor (non-negotiable, zero tolerance)**: if the defect touches a CPSC regulatory red line (e.g. lead over the limit, drawstrings, flammability failure), **it is an automatic veto with no possibility of concession**;\n  > 2. **Quantify the business risk rather than argue opinions**: for appearance or non-critical physical defects (e.g. slight shade variation or shrinkage over spec), I organize limit wash samples within 2 hours, estimate the potential post-launch returns and claim amounts, and lay out an objective comparison for the business team of 'air freight cost vs returns, claims and brand damage';\n  > 3. **Deliver an engineering workaround (DFM workaround)**: work with the technical team on on-site remedies (e.g. reprocessing and re-compacting the fabric, where adding pattern allowance is only a concession the customer agrees to in writing; an expedited garment pre-shrink wash; local reinforcement stitching);\n  > 4. **Formalize the concession sign-off (deviation release)**: if the business director decides, after assessment, to accept within a commercially tolerable range, it must go through an engineering deviation/concession form (ECN/deviation approval) that names who is commercially accountable and who bears the risk, keeping the QA process compliant and the risk controlled.\"\n"},"TPL-REVIEW":{"title":"Case Review Record Template","content":"# Case Review Record Template\n\nCopy into the case folder and name it `review-YYYYMMDD.md`.\n\n| Item | Content |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Case ID / version | |\n| Review type | Technical / Compliance |\n| Reviewer (must not be the author) | |\n| Date | |\n| Validator result | 0 errors, n warnings (list reasons for keeping them) |\n\n## Check results (against GOV-06)\n- A Technical:\n- B Compliance:\n- C Numbers and definitions:\n- D Consistency:\n\n## Conclusion\n- [ ] Pass\n- [ ] Conditional pass (required changes: )\n- [ ] Return (reason: )\n\n## Evidence archive (anonymized-real / composite only)\n- Location of authorization documents:\n- Location of test reports / financial records:\n"},"INT-STAR-INDEX":{"title":"Case STAR Talking-Points Index","content":"# Case STAR Talking-Points Index\n\n> This page is generated by the build script from each case's `case.json`, so its numbers always match the cases. Read the [Interview Use Policy](doc:GOV-08) first: illustrative cases are for learning structure and reasoning and must not be told as your own experience.\n\n## Apparel\n\n| Case | Type | Stage | Key result | Loss basis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| [APP-001 High-stretch yoga leggings: pilling in wear and flatlock seam cracking](case:APP-001) | Quality incident (8D) | Post-market | Quality return rate 7.4 ➔ 0.9 % | actual cost $1,444,100 |\n| [APP-002 Children's hoodies detained at port for hood drawstrings and lead in zipper-pull coating](case:APP-002) | Quality incident (8D) | Shipment / Arrival | Port detention ➔ released after supervised reconditioning | actual spend $340,000 |\n| [APP-003 Vintage-wash jeans: embrittlement, acidic residue and odor](case:APP-003) | Quality incident (8D) | Shipment / Arrival | Tear strength 1.3 ➔ 2.6 lbf | FOB $1,740,000 |\n| [APP-004 Waterproof down jacket: down leakage through the inner shell and loss of hydrostatic pressure at taped shell seams](case:APP-004) | Quality incident (8D) | In production | Hydrostatic pressure 1800 ➔ 9000 mm | actual cost $406,800 |\n| [APP-005 Training swimwear: chlorine degradation of spandex and seam cracking when wet](case:APP-005) | Quality incident (8D) | Post-market | Elongation retention after cycling 62 ➔ 91 % | actual cost $335,200 |\n| [APP-006 Non-iron shirts: twist, collar shrinkage and sleeve-length drift after laundering](case:APP-006) | Quality incident (8D) | Post-market | Garment twist after laundering 6.5 ➔ 1.8 % | actual cost $260,000 |\n| [APP-007 High-visibility flame-resistant workwear: reflective tape delamination and retroreflectivity loss after laundering](case:APP-007) | Quality incident (8D) | In production | Retroreflection after laundering 186 ➔ 286 cd/lx/m² | actual cost $36,500 |\n| [APP-008 Plus-size women's blazer: distorted grading and restricted movement in wear testing, caught before production](case:APP-008) | Pre-production gate catch | Pre-production / Development | Dynamic wear-test discomfort rate 42 ➔ 6 % | — |\n| [APP-009 Basic T-shirt: size-related returns and size recommendation improvement](case:APP-009) | Continuous improvement | Post-market | Size/fit return rate 8.6 ➔ 6.1 % | — |\n| [APP-010 Knit loungewear: screen color mismatch, metamerism and returns for unmatched sets](case:APP-010) | Continuous improvement | Post-market | Color return rate 3.2 ➔ 1.4 % | — |\n\n## Home Textiles\n\n| Case | Type | Stage | Key result | Loss basis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| [HT-001 400TC Egyptian cotton sateen bedding: tear strength and seam slippage](case:HT-001) | Quality incident (8D) | In production | Tear strength 1.4 ➔ 3.9 lbf | actual cost $1,070,000 |\n| [HT-002 Full blackout curtains: coating blocking and delamination, and NFPA 701 failure](case:HT-002) | Quality incident (8D) | Shipment / Arrival | Mean afterflame of drips 8.5 ➔ 0 s | actual cost $680,000 |\n| [HT-003 Zero-twist bath towels: lint shedding and loss of absorbency](case:HT-003) | Quality incident (8D) | Post-market | Lint loss in laundering 2.85 ➔ 0.32 % | actual cost $1,300,000 |\n| [HT-004 800 FP goose down duvet inserts: oxygen number, odor and microbial counts over the limits](case:HT-004) | Quality incident (8D) | Shipment / Arrival | Oxygen number 28.5 ➔ 4.8 mg O₂/100 g | actual cost $690,000 |\n| [HT-005 Mattress protector: TPU membrane leaking after laundering](case:HT-005) | Quality incident (8D) | Post-market | Hydrostatic pressure 620 ➔ 8500 mm | actual cost $406,600 |\n| [HT-006 Tufted rugs: tuft pull-out, powdering backing adhesive and wet-crocking color transfer](case:HT-006) | Quality incident (8D) | Post-market | Tuft bind 1.9 ➔ 4.1 lbf | actual cost $234,640 |\n| [HT-007 Fitted sheets: elastic fatigue and corners slipping off](case:HT-007) | Quality incident (8D) | Post-market | Elastic recovery after laundering 58 ➔ 86 % | actual cost $156,900 |\n| [HT-008 Weighted blanket: fill bead migration and pocket rupture caught in pre-production verification](case:HT-008) | Pre-production gate catch | Pre-production / Development | Corner weight deviation 31 ➔ 7 % | — |\n| [HT-009 Fitted sheets: mattress depth fit and product-page information](case:HT-009) | Continuous improvement | Post-market | Fit-to-product return rate 6.9 ➔ 2.8 % | — |\n| [HT-010 Throw pillows: cover-to-insert fit and loft recovery from compression packaging](case:HT-010) | Continuous improvement | Post-market | Loft return rate 4.4 ➔ 1.9 % | — |\n\n## Materials & Trims\n\n| Case | Type | Stage | Key result | Loss basis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| [RM-001 Recycled polyester fleece: metamerism and thermal migration of disperse dyes](case:RM-001) | Quality incident (8D) | Pre-production / Development | Metamerism index (MI) 2.85 ➔ 0.28 | actual cost $69,600 |\n| [RM-002 Metal zipper pulls corroding in salt spray and prong snaps with insufficient holding strength](case:RM-002) | Quality incident (8D) | In production | Salt spray: substrate corrosion at 12 h ➔ no failure at ≥ 96 h (requirement 48 h) / snaps 8.2 ➔ 28.5 lbf | actual cost $95,000 |\n| [RM-003 Water-based plastisol-alternative print: wet crocking, cracking and loss after laundering](case:RM-003) | Quality incident (8D) | In production | Wet crocking grade 1.5 ➔ 4 grade | actual cost $52,300 |\n| [RM-004 Merino knit fabric: felting shrinkage caught at pre-production approval](case:RM-004) | Pre-production gate catch | Pre-production / Development | Area shrinkage after laundering 12.6 ➔ 3.1 % | — |\n\n## Systems & Supply Chain\n\n| Case | Type | Stage | Key result | Loss basis |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| [SYS-001 From end-of-line inspection to supplier designated-QA self-inspection with unannounced audits](case:SYS-001) | System building | In production | Same-cohort audited FTR 86.4% ➔ 95.1% / annual net saving in brand inspection spend about $0.89M | — |\n| [SYS-002 COPQ accounting, failure cost reduction and supplier claim recovery](case:SYS-002) | System building | In production | Cost of poor quality (before recovery) 2100000 ➔ 1350000 USD/yr | — |\n| [SYS-003 Dynamic vendor quality scorecard linked to order allocation](case:SYS-003) | System building | Pre-production / Development | FTR (audited basis) 86.9 ➔ 91.9 % | — |\n| [SYS-004 Conflicting colorfastness verdicts across labs and rebuilding the measurement system](case:SYS-004) | System building | Pre-production / Development | Inter-lab agreement 75 ➔ 94 % | — |\n| [SYS-005 Broken traceability across mixed multi-factory lots and mock recalls](case:SYS-005) | System building | In production | Trace time 9 days ➔ 6 hours | — |\n| [SYS-006 Tracing broken needle fragments and the needle-detection blind spot for styles with metal trims](case:SYS-006) | Quality incident (8D) | In production | Broken-needle isolation time 95 ➔ 25 min | actual cost $22,500 |\n| [SYS-007 Return reason master data, Pareto and cross-functional PDCA](case:SYS-007) | Continuous improvement | Post-market | Attributable return share 72 ➔ 94 % | — |\n| [SYS-008 Warehouse mis-picks, missing set pieces and closing the returns loop](case:SYS-008) | Continuous improvement | Shipment / Arrival | Mis-ship return rate 2.7 ➔ 0.6 % | — |\n\n"},"PB-AQL":{"title":"AQL Sampling Plan Design Handbook for Textiles and Apparel","content":"# AQL Sampling Plan Design Guide for Textile and Apparel\n\n> **AQL Sampling Plan Design Guide per ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 for Apparel & Home Textile**\n\n---\n\n## 1. AQL Fundamentals\n\n**AQL (Acceptable Quality Limit)** is not \"the allowable defect rate,\" but rather the **worst tolerable quality level** for the process average in continuous lot-by-lot inspection. A plan with AQL = 2.5 means: if the true process defect rate stays around 2.5%, approximately 95% of lots will be accepted.\n\nKey concepts:\n- AQL protects the **balance between consumer risk and producer risk**, not \"2.5% defective product is acceptable\"\n- Acceptance of a single lot does not mean the lot is defect-free — sampling inspection is a probabilistic decision\n- A smaller AQL (e.g., 1.0) requires a larger sample size and provides greater detection power, but at higher cost\n\n---\n\n## 2. ANSI/ASQ Z1.4 Plan Quick Reference\n\n### 2.1 Inspection Levels\n\n| Level | Sample Size Relative to Normal/II | Applicable Scenarios |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| S-1 to S-4 | Smaller | Destructive testing (e.g., tear, burn) — limited number of specimens |\n| I (Reduced) | ~40% of II | Low-risk products, stable quality history |\n| **II (Normal)** | **Baseline** | **Default choice** |\n| III (Tightened) | ~160% of II | High-value / high-risk products |\n\nMost brands default to **Normal / Level II**.\n\n### 2.2 Common AQL Values and Sample Sizes (Normal/II Excerpt)\n\n| Lot Size | Sample Size Code | Sample Size (n) | AQL 1.0 Ac/Re | AQL 2.5 Ac/Re | AQL 4.0 Ac/Re |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| 2–8 | A | 2 | 0/1 | 0/1 | 0/1 |\n| 151–280 | G | 32 | 0/1 | 1/2 | 2/3 |\n| 501–1200 | J | 80 | 2/3 | 5/6 | 7/8 |\n| 1201–3200 | K | 125 | 3/4 | 7/8 | 10/11 |\n| 3201–10000 | L | 200 | 5/6 | 10/11 | 14/15 |\n| 10001–35000 | M | 315 | 7/8 | 14/15 | 21/22 |\n\nAc = Accept number, Re = Reject number. The ratio of conforming to nonconforming items in the lot determines the probability of acceptance.\n\n### 2.3 Defect Classification and AQL Assignment\n\n| Defect Severity | Definition | Typical AQL |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: |\n| **Critical** | Safety / regulatory violation (broken needle, lead exceedance, drawstring violation, flammability failure) | **0** (zero acceptance) |\n| **Major** | Defects affecting product function or appearance (seam failure, severe shade variation, print delamination, out-of-spec dimensions) | **2.5** |\n| **Minor** | Slight cosmetic blemishes (loose threads, minor wrinkles, internal label misalignment) | **4.0** |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Switching Rules\n\nThe Z1.4 switching rules are the foundation of risk-graded dynamic sampling; see [SYS-009](case:SYS-009) for details.\n\n```\nNormal → Tightened: 2 out of 5 consecutive lots rejected\nTightened → Normal: 5 consecutive lots accepted\nTightened → Discontinue: 5 cumulative lots still under Tightened (unable to restore Normal) → suspend acceptance\nNormal → Reduced: 10 consecutive lots accepted + stable production + approval\nReduced → Normal: 1 lot rejected, or unstable production, or other conditions\n```\n\n**Note**: Many companies use only the Normal plan and never switch — this effectively abandons the most valuable risk-adjustment mechanism in Z1.4. [SYS-009](case:SYS-009) demonstrates how to implement dynamic switching in practice.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Fabric Inspection: Four-Point System vs. AQL\n\nFinished goods use attribute-based AQL (pass/fail), while fabrics use the Four-Point System (ASTM D5430) with penalty points:\n\n| Defect Length | Points |\n| :--- | :---: |\n| ≤ 3 in | 1 |\n| 3–6 in | 2 |\n| 6–9 in | 3 |\n| > 9 in | 4 |\n| Hole ≤ 1 in | 2 |\n| Hole > 1 in | 4 |\n\n**Acceptance criterion**: Average penalty points per 100 linear yards. Customer limits are typically ≤ 28 or ≤ 40 points/100 yards, as specified by contract.\n\nKey differences between the Four-Point System and AQL:\n- AQL is a lot acceptance scheme based on \"pass/fail\"; the Four-Point System is a continuous evaluation weighted by defect severity\n- Fabrics can use both the Four-Point System (roll-by-roll scoring) and AQL (lot-level accept/reject by number of rolls)\n- Refer to [RM-007](case:RM-007) for a case where Four-Point defect scores were reduced from 42 to 18\n\n---\n\n## 5. Common Misconceptions in Sampling Plan Design\n\n| Misconception | Correct Understanding |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| \"AQL 2.5 means 2.5% defective is acceptable\" | AQL is the risk threshold for process average quality, not the upper limit of defect rate for any single lot |\n| \"Passing inspection = the lot is fine\" | Sampling inspection is a probabilistic decision; an AQL 2.5 / n=80 plan has approximately 25% probability of passing a lot with 5% defect rate |\n| \"Increasing sample size catches all problems\" | Doubling the sample size only moderately improves detection power and cannot substitute for process control |\n| \"Use the same AQL for all products\" | AQL should be stratified by product category risk and supplier quality history; refer to [SYS-009](case:SYS-009) |\n| \"Tightened is a punishment\" | Tightened is a risk management tool that enables early interception when quality deteriorates |\n| \"Skip-Lot means no inspection\" | Skip-Lot still includes periodic sampling (e.g., 1 out of every 5 lots), and requires DQA authorization plus a record of consecutive acceptance |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Sampling Considerations for Special Scenarios\n\n### 6.1 Small Lots / High Mix Low Volume\n\nFast fashion and e-commerce frequently involve small lots of 200–500 pieces per style. Z1.4 requires a high sample-to-lot ratio for small lots (e.g., lot size 200, Normal/II sample 32, i.e., 16%), making it costly. Options:\n- Keep Normal/II unchanged (safe but expensive)\n- For low-risk styles, reduce to Level I (sample ~13, a 60% reduction)\n- For high-risk small lots, consider 100% inspection instead\n\n### 6.2 Destructive Testing\n\nTests such as tear strength, burst strength, flammability, and colorfastness consume specimens. Use Special Inspection Levels S-1 to S-4, with 2–8 specimens per lot, combined with type testing (full testing on the first lot + subsequent random testing).\n\n### 6.3 Raw Material Incoming Inspection\n\nSampling plans for raw materials (fabrics and trims) should be designed separately from finished goods. Refer to [SOP-DFQ](doc:SOP-DFQ) for fabric qualification requirements and [SYS-009](case:SYS-009) for the risk-grading model.\n"},"PB-DOE":{"title":"DOE Experiment Design Template for Textiles and Apparel","content":"# Design of Experiments (DOE) Template for Textile and Apparel\n\n> **Design of Experiments (DOE) for Textile Process Optimization**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Textile Process Optimization Requires DOE\n\nThe traditional \"One-Factor-At-a-Time\" (OFAT) approach is inefficient in textile processes and easily misses interactions. For example:\n- The effects of fusing press temperature and pressure on interlining peel strength are not independent — the optimal temperature depends on the pressure level\n- The joint effects of dyeing temperature, time, and auxiliary concentration on colorfastness cannot be discovered by adjusting only one factor at a time\n\nDOE systematically varies multiple factors simultaneously, extracting maximum information from a minimum number of experimental runs and quantifying factor interactions.\n\n**DOE applications in the case library**: [RM-008](case:RM-008) uses DOE to optimize a C0 DWR finishing process (3-factor, 2-level full factorial design + center points).\n\n---\n\n## 2. DOE Type Selection\n\n| DOE Type | No. of Factors | No. of Runs | Applicable Scenarios |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Full Factorial** | 2–4 | $2^k$ to $3^k$ | Few factors; complete interaction information needed |\n| **Fractional Factorial** | 5–8 | $2^{k-p}$ | Many factors; screen for key factors first |\n| **Central Composite Design (CCD)** | 2–5 | $2^k + 2k + c$ | Need to fit a quadratic response surface and find the optimum |\n| **Taguchi Orthogonal Array** | 3–13 | L9/L18, etc. | Known noise factors; robust design sought |\n| **Plackett-Burman** | ≤ 11 | 12 runs | Pure screening; no interaction estimation |\n\n**Recommended path for textiles**:\n1. Factors ≤ 4 and resources allow → **Full factorial + center points** (e.g., [RM-008](case:RM-008))\n2. 5–8 factors and uncertainty about which are important → **Fractional factorial screening** → Full factorial on key factors\n3. Need to find optimal process parameters → **CCD response surface**\n\n---\n\n## 3. Six Steps for DOE Implementation\n\n### Step 1: Define the Objective and Response Variable\n\n| Element | Description | Example (DWR Optimization) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Objective** | State in one sentence what to optimize | Maximize water repellency after 20 washes while maintaining initial C0 DWR repellency ≥ 90 rating |\n| **Response variable (Y)** | Measurable output | AATCC 22 spray rating (0–100) |\n| **Measurement method** | Test standard for the response variable | AATCC 22, after 20 AATCC 135 wash cycles |\n\nThe response variable must be a metric already defined in `registry/metrics.json`, or registered before starting the DOE.\n\n### Step 2: Select Factors and Levels\n\n| Factor | Type | Low Level (−) | High Level (+) | Basis for Selection |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| A: DWR concentration | Continuous | 30 g/L | 60 g/L | Supplier recommended range |\n| B: Curing temperature | Continuous | 150°C | 170°C | Fabric heat tolerance limit |\n| C: Curing time | Continuous | 60 s | 90 s | Production line speed constraint |\n\n**Principles for level selection**:\n- Range must be wide enough for effects to be detectable (signal > noise)\n- Must not exceed equipment capability or product safety limits\n- Add 3–5 center points to detect curvature and estimate pure error\n\n### Step 3: Determine the Experimental Plan\n\n3 factors × 2 levels full factorial + 3 center points = **11 experimental runs**.\n\n| Run | A: Concentration | B: Temperature | C: Time | Randomized Order |\n| :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| 1 | − | − | − | 7 |\n| 2 | + | − | − | 3 |\n| 3 | − | + | − | 11 |\n| 4 | + | + | − | 1 |\n| 5 | − | − | + | 9 |\n| 6 | + | − | + | 5 |\n| 7 | − | + | + | 2 |\n| 8 | + | + | + | 8 |\n| 9 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 4 |\n| 10 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 6 |\n| 11 | 0 | 0 | 0 | 10 |\n\n**Critical disciplines**:\n- **Full randomization**: Execute in the \"Randomized Order\" column sequence, not in Run number order\n- **Independent sampling for each run**: Do not cut 11 specimens from the same piece of fabric (unless within-fabric uniformity has been verified by MSA)\n- **Environmental condition logging**: Record ambient temperature, humidity, and equipment status for each run\n\n### Step 4: Execute the Experiment and Record Data\n\nRecord for each run:\n- Actual factor values (not just set values — e.g., if set to 170°C but actual is 168°C, record 168)\n- Response variable measurements (at least 2–3 replicate specimens per condition, averaged)\n- Anomaly records (equipment malfunction, operational deviation)\n\n### Step 5: Analyze Results\n\n| Analytical Tool | Purpose |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Main Effects Plot** | Magnitude and direction of each factor's main effect |\n| **Interaction Plot** | Whether interactions exist between factors (crossing lines = interaction present) |\n| **ANOVA Table** | p-values for factors/interactions (p < 0.05 is significant) |\n| **Normal Probability Plot** | Residual check — whether the model is adequate |\n| **Response Surface Plot** | Locate the optimal process window (used with CCD) |\n\n**Common findings in textile DOE**:\n- The temperature × time interaction is almost always significant (high temp / short time ≈ low temp / long time, but extreme combinations carry risk)\n- Concentration usually has the largest main effect, but with a saturation point (no additional benefit beyond a certain concentration)\n\n### Step 6: Confirmation Run\n\nConduct **3–5 independent confirmation runs** at the optimal conditions derived from the analysis, verifying that:\n- The response mean falls within the predicted confidence interval\n- The process is reproducible under production conditions\n\nIf confirmation fails → review model assumptions, noise factors, or expand the experimental range.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Special Considerations for DOE in Textiles\n\n### 4.1 Fabric Uniformity\n\nFabric is not a uniform material — there can be variation along warp/weft directions, between head/middle/end, and between left/center/right. DOE specimen sampling must control for positional variability:\n- Take all specimens from adjacent positions on the same roll\n- Or include position as a \"Block\" factor in the design\n\n### 4.2 Destructive Testing\n\nMany textile tests (tear, pilling, colorfastness) are destructive — the same specimen cannot be tested repeatedly. Solutions:\n- Prepare enough specimens per experimental condition (number of response variables × number of replicates)\n- Use a Split-Plot design to handle the nested structure of \"finishing the entire fabric piece → then cutting specimens to test different responses\"\n\n### 4.3 Lot-to-Lot Variation\n\nIf DOE spans multiple fabric lots (e.g., screening experiments over several days), lot-to-lot differences may mask process factor effects. Countermeasures:\n- Use fabric from the same lot whenever possible\n- If multiple lots are unavoidable, include lot as a block factor\n\n### 4.4 Factory-Floor Execution\n\nDOE is typically conducted in the laboratory or on a pilot line, but must ultimately be confirmed on the production line. Factory-floor noise (operator variability, equipment fluctuations) may invalidate laboratory-optimal conditions — confirmation runs must be performed under actual production conditions.\n\n---\n\n## 5. DOE Applications in the Case Library\n\n| Case | DOE Application | Factors |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| [RM-008](case:RM-008) | C0 DWR finishing optimization | DWR concentration, curing temperature, curing time |\n| [APP-009](case:APP-009) | T-shirt sizing A/B test | Size chart change (A/B test is a special form of DOE) |\n| [HT-003](case:HT-003) | Zero-twist towel absorbency optimization (potential scenario) | Softener dosage, drying temperature, wash cycles |\n\n---\n\n## 6. Relationship Between DOE and Other Quality Tools\n\n| Tool | Relationship with DOE |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [PB-FMEA](doc:PB-FMEA) | High-RPN items in FMEA → Use DOE to identify root causes and optimal process parameters |\n| [PB-SPC](doc:PB-SPC) | After DOE identifies optimal parameters → Use SPC to monitor process stability |\n| [PB-MSA](doc:PB-MSA) | Before DOE → The measurement system must be confirmed adequate (GRR %Study Var ≤ 10% excellent, 10–30% marginally acceptable, > 30% unacceptable); otherwise response variable noise comes from measurement rather than the process |\n| [PB-RCA](doc:PB-RCA) | RCA identifies root cause factors → DOE quantifies factor effects and optimizes |\n| DMAIC | DOE is typically used in the Improve phase of DMAIC |\n"},"PB-MSA":{"title":"Measurement System Analysis (MSA) and Gage R&R Template for Textiles and Apparel","content":"# Measurement System Analysis (MSA) and Gage R&R Template for Textile and Apparel\n\n> **Measurement System Analysis & Gage R&R for Textile Testing**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Why Textile Testing Especially Requires MSA\n\nSources of variation in textile testing are more complex than in metalworking: specimen sampling location, temperature/humidity conditioning, tester technique, and instrument calibration status all affect results. If the measurement system itself accounts for a large proportion of total variation, SPC control charts and Cpk become meaningless — you are monitoring measurement noise, not the process.\n\nThe inter-laboratory comparison case in [SYS-004](case:SYS-004) demonstrates this problem: colorfastness ratings for the same blind sample differed by as much as 1.5 grades across different laboratories.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Five Properties of MSA\n\n| Property | Definition | Manifestation in Textile Testing |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Bias** | Difference between the measurement mean and the true value | Laboratory gray scale ratings systematically higher or lower |\n| **Linearity** | Change in bias across the measurement range | Tensile tester accuracy differs between low-force and high-force values |\n| **Stability** | Change in bias over time | Aging lamps in the color matching light booth cause visual assessment drift |\n| **Repeatability** | Variation when the same tester, same instrument, and same specimen are measured multiple times | Difference between two consecutive Four-Point System scores by the same fabric inspector on the same section of fabric |\n| **Reproducibility** | Variation across different testers (or different instruments, different laboratories) on the same specimen | Colorfastness rating differences across different laboratories on the same blind sample |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Gage R&R Study Design\n\n### 3.1 Continuous Data: Crossed Gage R&R (ANOVA Method)\n\nApplicable to continuous metrics such as tear strength, burst strength, fabric weight, and garment dimensions.\n\n**Experimental design**:\n- **Parts**: Select ≥ 10 representative samples covering the specification range (do not select all mid-range values)\n- **Operators**: ≥ 2 (ideally 3), representing the range of personnel in routine operations\n- **Replicates**: Each operator measures each sample ≥ 2 times (ideally 3 times)\n- **Randomization**: Measurement order fully randomized; operators must not see the previous result\n\n**Example**: 3 testers × 10 fabric samples × 3 replicates = 90 measurements\n\n### 3.2 Acceptance Criteria\n\n| Metric | Formula | Acceptable | Marginal | Unacceptable |\n| :--- | :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: |\n| **%Study Var (GRR/TV)** | $\\frac{\\sigma_{GRR}}{\\sigma_{TV}} \\times 100\\%$ | ≤ 10% | 10–30% | > 30% |\n| **%Tolerance (GRR/Tol)** | $\\frac{6\\sigma_{GRR}}{USL - LSL} \\times 100\\%$ | ≤ 10% | 10–30% | > 30% |\n| **ndc (Number of Distinct Categories)** | $\\frac{\\sigma_{Parts}}{\\sigma_{GRR}} \\times 1.41$ | ≥ 5 | 3–4 | < 3 |\n\n- **%Study Var ≤ 10%**: Excellent measurement system, suitable for SPC and Cpk\n- **10–30%**: Marginally acceptable, depending on the application and the cost of improvement\n- **> 30%**: Measurement system unacceptable; must be improved and reassessed — Cpk is meaningless in this case\n\n### 3.3 Ordinal/Attribute Data: Attribute Agreement Analysis\n\nApplicable to ordinal or binary data such as colorfastness gray scale ratings, pilling grades, and visual inspection pass/fail decisions.\n\n**Experimental design**:\n- **Samples**: ≥ 30, covering all grades (including borderline samples)\n- **Appraisers**: ≥ 2\n- **Replicates**: Each appraiser independently rates each sample ≥ 2 times\n- **Reference standard**: The \"true\" grade for each sample predetermined by an expert panel\n\n**Acceptance metrics**:\n- **Within-appraiser agreement**: Proportion of times the same appraiser gives the same rating (target ≥ 90%)\n- **Between-appraiser agreement**: Proportion of times all appraisers give the same rating (target ≥ 80%)\n- **Appraiser vs. standard agreement**: Proportion of times the appraiser matches the expert standard (target ≥ 85%)\n- **Cohen's kappa**: Agreement coefficient corrected for chance agreement (kappa ≥ 0.80 is good)\n\nRefer to the inter-laboratory comparison case in [SYS-004](case:SYS-004) and the DQA blind sample proficiency test in [SYS-001](case:SYS-001).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Special Considerations for MSA in Textile Testing\n\n### 4.1 Non-Repeatability of Specimens\n\nMany textile tests are destructive (tear, burst, pilling) — the same specimen cannot be measured repeatedly. Solutions:\n- Take multiple specimens from adjacent positions of the same fabric lot, assuming that the differences between specimens are primarily from inherent local variation in the fabric rather than the measurement system — this assumption requires that sampling positions be as close together as possible\n- Use a Nested GRR design instead of a crossed design: each operator tests different specimens, and parts and operators are not crossed in the ANOVA\n\n### 4.2 Conditioning Requirements\n\nTextile physical properties are significantly affected by temperature and humidity (ASTM D1776 standard atmosphere: 21°C / 65% RH). Before MSA, confirm that:\n- All specimens have been conditioned in the same conditioning room for at least 4 hours (moisture-dependent tests such as cotton fabrics may require longer)\n- Test environment temperature and humidity are within tolerance\n\n### 4.3 Subjectivity of Gray Scale Rating\n\nGray scale rating (color change, staining) is highly subjective and one of the items with the poorest reproducibility in textile MSA:\n- Light source must be consistent (D65 + specified secondary illuminant); light booth lamps should be replaced after exceeding 2,000 hours of use or the manufacturer's recommended lifespan\n- Appraisers should have annual vision tests (normal color vision)\n- Calibrate using physical gray scale reference standards; do not rely on \"experience\"\n\n---\n\n## 5. MSA Frequency and Trigger Conditions\n\n| Trigger Condition | Frequency | Scope |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| New instrument commissioned | Before commissioning | Full Gage R&R |\n| Instrument major repair or calibration | After repair | Bias + repeatability verification |\n| New tester onboarding | Before starting work | Add to existing Gage R&R as a new operator |\n| Annual review | Annually | Full Gage R&R for key metrics (metrics used for SPC/Cpk) |\n| SPC control chart out-of-control signal | Event-triggered | Rule out measurement system causes |\n| Inter-laboratory comparison reveals discrepancy | Event-triggered | Attribute Agreement Analysis + Gage R&R |\n\n---\n\n## 6. MSA Linkage with the Case Library\n\n| Case | MSA-Related Point |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| [SYS-004](case:SYS-004) | Inter-laboratory colorfastness rating inconsistency → Attribute Agreement Analysis |\n| [SYS-001](case:SYS-001) | DQA blind sample proficiency test → kappa ≥ 0.80 |\n| [RM-001](case:RM-001) | Instrument color difference vs. visual assessment in metamerism → MSA of instrument vs. visual methods |\n| [PB-SPC](doc:PB-SPC) | Prerequisite for Cpk → Gage R&R %Study Var ≤ 30% |\n"},"PB-SPC":{"title":"SPC Control Chart and Process Capability (Cpk) Analysis Template for Textiles and Apparel","content":"# SPC Control Charts and Process Capability (Cpk) Analysis Template for Textile and Apparel\n\n> **Statistical Process Control Charts & Process Capability (Cpk) for Textile Quality**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Applicability and Limitations of SPC in Textile and Apparel\n\nSPC control charts and Process Capability Index (Cpk) are applicable to quality characteristics that are **continuous, relatively stable in process, and backed by a qualified measurement system**. In the textile and apparel industry, typical applicable and non-applicable scenarios:\n\n| Applicable | Not Applicable |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Fabric weight (continuous, measurable lot by lot) | Colorfastness grade (ordinal, Cpk not computable) |\n| Tear strength, burst strength | Pilling grade |\n| Garment critical dimensions | Defect count / defect rate (use p-chart / c-chart, not Cpk) |\n| Fabric pH | Subjective hand feel rating |\n| Hydrostatic pressure | \"Pass/fail\" binary decisions |\n\n**Usage disciplines** (GOV-02 §3.3, GOV-03 §5):\n- Cpk shall **only be applied to** continuous metrics with `cpkAllowed: true` in `registry/metrics.json`;\n- Must state the number of subgroups (≥ 25 subgroups) and MSA results (Gage R&R %Study Var three-tier classification; see [PB-MSA](doc:PB-MSA));\n- **Do not calculate Cpk** when the process is unstable (control chart has unresolved out-of-control points) — stabilize first, then assess capability;\n- When the mean falls outside the specification, Cpk is negative and must be reported as-is, without concealment.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Control Chart Type Selection\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart TD\n    A[Quality Characteristic] --> B{Data Type?}\n    B -->|Continuous| C{Subgroup Size?}\n    B -->|Attribute/Ordinal| D{Defect Count or Defect Rate?}\n    C -->|n = 1| E[I-MR Individual & Moving Range Chart]\n    C -->|n = 2–10| F[Xbar-R Mean & Range Chart]\n    C -->|n > 10| G[Xbar-S Mean & Std Dev Chart]\n    D -->|Defect Rate| H[p-chart]\n    D -->|Defect Count| I[c-chart / u-chart]\n```\n\n### Common Scenarios in Textile and Apparel\n\n| Scenario | Recommended Control Chart | Subgroup Definition | Example |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Fabric weight per-roll inspection | **I-MR** | 1 sample per roll | 1 point per roll, subgroup n = 1 |\n| Garment critical dimension sampling | **Xbar-R** | 5 pieces sampled per hour | n = 5, ~8 subgroups per shift |\n| Tear / burst strength | **Xbar-R** | 3–5 specimens per lot | n = 3–5 |\n| AQL final inspection defect rate | **p-chart** | One sampling result per lot | Variable sample size → use p-chart |\n| Fabric Four-Point System defects | **u-chart** | Defect points per 100 yards per roll | Unequal roll lengths → use u-chart |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Xbar-R Control Chart Procedure (Garment Chest Width Example)\n\n### 3.1 Data Collection\n\n- **Specification**: Chest width 54.0 cm ± 2.0 cm (USL = 56.0, LSL = 52.0)\n- **Subgroup**: Randomly sample 5 pieces from the production line every 2 hours and measure chest width; collect ≥ 25 consecutive subgroups\n- **Measurement**: Same gage, same measurer (or a measurement system verified by MSA)\n\n### 3.2 Control Limit Calculation\n\n| Statistic | Formula |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Subgroup mean | $\\bar{X}_i = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{j=1}^{n} X_{ij}$ |\n| Subgroup range | $R_i = X_{max} - X_{min}$ |\n| Grand mean | $\\bar{\\bar{X}} = \\frac{1}{k}\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\bar{X}_i$ |\n| Average range | $\\bar{R} = \\frac{1}{k}\\sum_{i=1}^{k} R_i$ |\n| Xbar chart control limits | $UCL_{\\bar{X}} = \\bar{\\bar{X}} + A_2 \\bar{R}$, $LCL_{\\bar{X}} = \\bar{\\bar{X}} - A_2 \\bar{R}$ |\n| R chart control limits | $UCL_R = D_4 \\bar{R}$, $LCL_R = D_3 \\bar{R}$ |\n\nFor n = 5: $A_2 = 0.577$, $D_3 = 0$, $D_4 = 2.114$\n\n### 3.3 Stability Rules (Western Electric / Nelson)\n\nAny of the following patterns on the control chart indicates an **out-of-control** condition requiring investigation:\n\n| Rule | Description |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Rule 1 | 1 point beyond the 3σ control limits |\n| Rule 2 | 9 consecutive points on the same side of the center line |\n| Rule 3 | 6 consecutive points steadily increasing or decreasing |\n| Rule 4 | 14 consecutive points alternating up and down |\n\nOut-of-control points must be investigated and the cause documented (traceable to lot, shift, equipment, or process change). After confirming the cause as a special cause, remove the point and recalculate control limits. Never remove a data point without investigation.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Process Capability Cpk\n\n### 4.1 Prerequisites\n\n1. The control chart is in a state of statistical control (no unresolved out-of-control points);\n2. Data is approximately normally distributed (confirm with a normal probability plot or Anderson-Darling test; textile data often exhibits right skew — apply Box-Cox transformation if necessary);\n3. MSA has passed (Gage R&R %Study Var ≤ 10% is excellent, 10–30% is marginally acceptable, > 30% is unacceptable; see the three-tier classification in [PB-MSA](doc:PB-MSA)).\n\n### 4.2 Calculation\n\n$$C_p = \\frac{USL - LSL}{6\\hat{\\sigma}}$$\n\n$$C_{pk} = \\min\\left(\\frac{USL - \\bar{\\bar{X}}}{3\\hat{\\sigma}},\\; \\frac{\\bar{\\bar{X}} - LSL}{3\\hat{\\sigma}}\\right)$$\n\nWhere $\\hat{\\sigma} = \\bar{R} / d_2$ (for n = 5, $d_2 = 2.326$).\n\n### 4.3 Interpretation\n\n| Cpk | Meaning | Action |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- |\n| ≥ 1.67 | Excellent — process centered with low variation | Consider reducing inspection frequency |\n| 1.33–1.67 | Good | Maintain control |\n| 1.00–1.33 | Marginally acceptable — virtually no margin | Reduce variation or center the process |\n| < 1.00 | Unacceptable — out-of-spec product expected | Must improve and reassess |\n| < 0 | Mean is outside specification | Report as-is; take immediate corrective action |\n\n### 4.4 Typical Cpk Benchmarks for Textile and Apparel\n\nThe following are industry reference ranges (not standards); companies should establish their own baselines:\n\n| Metric | Typical Cpk Range | Remarks |\n| :--- | :---: | :--- |\n| Fabric weight | 0.8–1.5 | Affected by yarn lot and loom tension fluctuations |\n| Garment critical dimensions | 0.7–1.3 | High sewing process variation; stretch fabrics are harder to control |\n| Tear strength | 1.0–2.0 | Relatively stable at the fabric level |\n| Fabric pH | 1.2–2.5 | Low variation when washing is thorough |\n\n---\n\n## 5. I-MR Control Chart (Per-Roll Fabric Weight Monitoring)\n\nWhen each subgroup has only 1 data point (e.g., 1 weight sample per fabric roll), use the I-MR chart:\n\n- **I Chart** (Individual): $UCL_I = \\bar{X} + 2.66 \\bar{MR}$, $LCL_I = \\bar{X} - 2.66 \\bar{MR}$\n- **MR Chart** (Moving Range): $UCL_{MR} = 3.267 \\bar{MR}$\n\nWhere $\\bar{MR}$ is the average of the absolute differences between consecutive data points (moving range span = 2).\n\n---\n\n## 6. SPC Applications in the Case Library\n\n| Case | Applicable SPC Metric | Control Chart Type |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| [RM-001](case:RM-001) | ΔE value, fabric weight | I-MR |\n| [RM-006](case:RM-006) | Stretch recovery rate | Xbar-R (3–5 specimens per lot) |\n| [APP-001](case:APP-001) | Seam slippage elongation | Xbar-R |\n| [HT-007](case:HT-007) | Elastic band recovery rate | Xbar-R |\n| [SYS-004](case:SYS-004) | Inter-laboratory colorfastness ratings | p-chart (consistent/inconsistent) |\n"},"SOP-8D":{"title":"Customer Complaint and 8D Closure Standard Operating Procedure","content":"# Customer Complaint and 8D Closure Standard Operating Procedure\n\n> **Customer Complaint & 8D Closure SOP**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Purpose and Scope\n\n### 1.1 Purpose\n\nStandardize the end-to-end process from customer complaint/return signal receipt through 8D closure, ensuring:\n- Every customer complaint is recorded, classified, and responded to\n- Root cause analysis is rigorous, and corrective actions are verifiable\n- Closure timelines are controlled, and recurring issues are systematically eliminated\n- Claim recovery is supported by documented evidence\n\n### 1.2 Scope\n\n- Brand retail customer complaints (e-commerce return remarks, customer service tickets, social media complaints)\n- B2B customer quality complaints (retailer/wholesaler quality chargeback notices, nonconformance notifications)\n- Internal brand quality findings (DQA final inspection rejection, IQC rejection, warehouse discoveries)\n\n### 1.3 Referenced Documents\n\n- [SYS-011](case:SYS-011) — 8D Closure SLA Case\n- [PB-RCA](doc:PB-RCA) — Root Cause Analysis Methodology\n- [PB-FMEA](doc:PB-FMEA) — FMEA Methodology\n- [SYS-009](case:SYS-009) — Supplier Quality Grading and AQL Linkage\n\n---\n\n## 2. Complaint Receipt and Registration\n\n### 2.1 Signal Sources and Routing\n\n| Source | Receiver | Routing Method |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| E-commerce return remarks | Customer Service team | System auto-flags returns containing quality keywords |\n| Customer service tickets (phone/email/chat) | Customer Service team | Tickets with quality issue descriptions routed to QA |\n| Social media complaints | PR / Social Media team | Posts containing product quality or safety keywords routed to QA |\n| Retailer quality chargeback notice | Procurement / Finance | Routed to QA |\n| DQA final inspection rejection | QA on-site | Inspection system auto-creates 8D |\n| IQC incoming material rejection | QA IQC | Refer to nonconformance handling in [SOP-IQC](doc:SOP-IQC) |\n\n### 2.2 Registration Content\n\nEach complaint is registered in the quality management system with the following information:\n- **Complaint number** (system auto-generated)\n- **Complaint date**\n- **Product information**: SKU, PO, lot number (if traceable), supplier/factory\n- **Problem description**: Defect type, severity, quantity/proportion\n- **Evidence**: Photos, test reports, customer emails\n- **Customer/channel information**: Complaint source, customer name\n- **Initial classification**: Defect type (classified per failureModes in taxonomy.json)\n\n---\n\n## 3. Complaint Severity Grading and Response Timelines\n\n### 3.1 Severity Grading\n\n| Severity | Definition | 8D Timeline | Escalation Condition |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Critical** | Safety risk, regulatory violation, consumer injury | **14 calendar days** | D4 not completed within 7 days → escalate to VP |\n| **Major** | Functional defect, severe cosmetic defect, bulk customer return | **30 calendar days** | D4 not completed within 15 days → escalate to Director |\n| **Minor** | Slight cosmetic blemish, isolated return | **60 calendar days** | D4 not completed within 30 days → escalate to Manager |\n\nRefer to the SLA framework in [SYS-011](case:SYS-011).\n\n### 3.2 Automatic Escalation Mechanism\n\n- **D4 (interim containment) not completed within 50% of the timeline** → System auto-sends escalation email to superior\n- **D8 (closure) not completed within the timeline** → System flags as overdue; included in monthly quality review\n- **Supplier with 2 consecutive overdue 8Ds** → Triggers supplier quality grade downgrade review\n\n---\n\n## 4. 8D Process Steps\n\n### D0 — Problem Awareness (Complaint Registration)\n\nComplaint registration described in Section 2 above constitutes D0.\n\n### D1 — Form the Team\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **8D Lead** (QA Engineer) | Lead the investigation, coordinate with the supplier, drive progress per timeline |\n| **Supplier Quality Contact** | Root cause investigation and corrective action execution at the factory |\n| **Technical Support** (as needed) | Laboratory testing, process analysis |\n\nFor Critical-severity 8Ds, the QA Manager personally serves as the lead or provides daily follow-up.\n\n### D2 — Problem Description\n\nDescribe the problem using 5W2H:\n- **What**: What is the defect?\n- **Where**: Which part/location?\n- **When**: When was it discovered? Which lots are affected?\n- **Who**: Who discovered it? Whose product?\n- **Why (surface)**: Preliminary judgment (not the root cause — only the observable phenomenon)\n- **How much**: Affected quantity, proportion, monetary value\n- **How (detection method)**: Final inspection, incoming inspection, consumer return, customer complaint\n\n### D3 — Interim Containment Actions\n\nBefore the root cause is determined, control the scope of impact:\n- **Work-in-process**: Notify the factory to halt the line for inspection / 100% sorting\n- **Finished goods in warehouse**: Lock suspect lots in WMS; 100% re-inspection or tightened sampling\n- **In-transit goods**: Notify the warehouse to isolate and inspect upon receipt\n- **Already sold**: Evaluate whether to initiate a recall procedure (refer to [SOP-RCL](doc:SOP-RCL))\n\nContainment actions must be in place and confirmed effective within **48 hours**.\n\n### D4 — Root Cause Analysis\n\nUse the methods in [PB-RCA](doc:PB-RCA):\n- **5-Why**: Ask \"why\" layer by layer from the direct cause until reaching the systemic cause\n- **Fishbone Diagram**: Expand from six dimensions — Man, Machine, Material, Method, Environment, Measurement\n- **Verification**: The root cause must explain \"why the defect occurred here and not elsewhere\" and \"why it didn't happen before / why it is happening now\"\n\n**Common pitfalls**:\n- Accepting \"operator inattention\" as a root cause → Should ask \"Why inattentive? Was there no training, no work instruction, or were working conditions inadequate?\"\n- Accepting \"supplier fabric was defective\" as a root cause → Should ask \"Why didn't incoming inspection catch it? Was inspection not performed, or was it performed but the defect not detected?\"\n\n### D5 — Corrective Action Development\n\nDevelop permanent corrective actions targeting the root cause (not interim containment):\n- Each action has a **responsible person, due date, and verification method**\n- Actions are categorized as: process improvement, inspection tightening, specification revision, supplier management, training, etc.\n- If the root cause involves design, update the FMEA (refer to [PB-FMEA](doc:PB-FMEA))\n\n### D6 — Corrective Action Implementation and Verification\n\n- Supplier submits implementation evidence for corrective actions (photos, revised work instructions, pilot production test data, etc.)\n- QA verification: Focused check on corrective action effectiveness during the next incoming/outgoing shipment\n- For continuous metrics, use SPC control charts (refer to [PB-SPC](doc:PB-SPC)) to verify process changes before and after improvement\n\n### D7 — Prevent Recurrence\n\n- Horizontal deployment: Could the same root cause affect other products/suppliers?\n- Systemic improvement: Do SOPs, inspection standards, or supplier requirements need updating?\n- Knowledge capture: Document the root cause and solution in the case library as input for future FMEA and training\n\n### D8 — Closure and Recognition\n\n- QA Manager reviews completeness of D1–D7 and confirms closure\n- Critical-severity 8Ds require QA Director sign-off\n- Closure criteria: Corrective actions implemented + verified effective + no signs of recurrence (observe at least 2–3 lots)\n\n---\n\n## 5. Claim Recovery\n\n### 5.1 Claim Basis\n\n| Cost Type | Calculation Method | Supporting Documents |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Defective product cost | Number of defective units × unit price | Inspection report + nonconforming quantity records |\n| Rework cost | Rework labor hours × labor rate | Rework records |\n| Inspection cost | Additional labor for tightened inspection + testing fees | Inspection records + laboratory invoices |\n| Freight | Return / replacement / expedited air freight charges | Logistics documents |\n| Customer chargeback | Retailer quality chargeback amount | Chargeback notice |\n| Consumer refund | Total e-commerce refund amount | Refund records |\n\n### 5.2 Claim Process\n\n1. After 8D closure, QA compiles the total loss amount and supporting documents\n2. Procurement/Finance issues a claim notice (Debit Note) to the supplier\n3. Supplier confirms or negotiates\n4. Claim amount is deducted from subsequent payments or settled separately\n5. Claim recovery rate is included in monthly quality metrics (refer to claim recovery rate improvement from 28% to 61% in [SYS-011](case:SYS-011))\n\n---\n\n## 6. Recurring Issue Management\n\n### 6.1 Definition of Recurring Issue\n\nThe same supplier × same failure mode triggers an 8D again within **12 months**.\n\n### 6.2 Recurring Issue Handling\n\n| Recurrence Count | Action |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **2nd occurrence** | 8D is escalated to Critical handling (regardless of current severity); QA Manager leads a review of the previous 8D corrective action effectiveness |\n| **3rd occurrence** | Supplier quality grade downgraded to \"Watch\"; senior management meeting with the supplier's leadership |\n| **≥ 4th occurrence** | Initiate supplier phase-out evaluation |\n\nRefer to the reduction in recurring issue rate from 34% to 12% in [SYS-011](case:SYS-011).\n\n---\n\n## 7. Monthly and Quarterly Reviews\n\n### 7.1 Monthly Quality Review\n\n| Metric | Data Source | Responsible Person |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 8D cases opened / closed / overdue | Quality management system | QA Manager |\n| Median 8D closure cycle time (by severity) | Quality management system | QA Manager |\n| Recurring issue rate (rolling 12 months) | Quality management system | QA Manager |\n| Claim recovery rate | Financial system | Procurement / Finance |\n| Top 5 failure mode Pareto | Quality management system | QA Engineer |\n\n### 7.2 Quarterly Supplier Quality Review\n\n- 8D performance is included in the supplier scorecard, linked to AQL inspection levels (refer to [SYS-009](case:SYS-009))\n- Suppliers with recurring issues are placed on a watch list with a dedicated improvement plan\n"},"SOP-IQC":{"title":"Incoming Fabric and Trim Inspection Standard Operating Procedure","content":"# Incoming Quality Control (IQC) Standard Operating Procedure for Fabrics, Trims & Accessories\n\n> **Incoming Quality Control (IQC) SOP for Fabrics, Trims & Accessories**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Purpose & Scope\n\n### 1.1 Purpose\n\nStandardize the incoming inspection process for fabrics, trims, and packaging materials to intercept non-conforming raw materials before production, thereby reducing finished-product defect rates and rework costs.\n\n### 1.2 Scope\n\n- All externally sourced fabrics (woven, knit, functional fabrics)\n- Trims and accessories (fusible interlining, zippers, buttons, elastic bands, sewing thread, labels, hang tags)\n- Packaging materials (poly bags, cartons, filler materials)\n\n### 1.3 Referenced Documents\n\n- [SYS-009](case:SYS-009) — Risk-Graded Dynamic AQL Sampling Plan\n- [PB-AQL](doc:PB-AQL) — AQL Sampling Plan Design Playbook\n- [RM-005](case:RM-005)–[RM-008](case:RM-008) — Raw Material Quality Cases\n- ASTM D5430 — Four-Point System Fabric Inspection\n- AATCC Evaluation Procedure Series — Colorfastness Gray Scale Rating\n- 16 CFR 1610 — Flammability Testing of Clothing Textiles\n- CPSIA Sec. 101 — Lead Content Limits for Children's Products\n\n---\n\n## 2. Material Classification & Inspection Levels\n\n### 2.1 Material Risk Classification\n\n| Risk Level | Definition | Typical Materials | Inspection Intensity |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **A — High Risk** | Directly affects safety/regulatory compliance, or has a decisive impact on finished-product appearance quality | All fabrics, fusible interlining, children's product trims | Mandatory inspection per lot + laboratory testing |\n| **B — Medium Risk** | Affects finished-product function or appearance, but governed by industry-standard specifications | Zippers, buttons, elastic bands, sewing thread | Sampling inspection per lot per AQL plan |\n| **C — Low Risk** | Does not directly affect product quality | Hang tags, cartons, poly bags, filler materials | First-lot inspection + subsequent skip-lot |\n\n### 2.2 Supplier Quality Level Linkage\n\nIncoming inspection intensity is linked to the supplier's quality tier (refer to the risk-grading model in [SYS-009](case:SYS-009)):\n\n| Supplier Tier | Normal Inspection | Tightened Trigger | Skip-Lot Trigger |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Preferred (Skip-Lot Qualified)** | 1 out of every 5 lots | 1 non-conforming lot reverts to Normal | 20 consecutive conforming lots + DQA authorization |\n| **Qualified (Normal)** | Every lot at Normal/II | 2 non-conforming lots in 5 consecutive lots → Tightened | — |\n| **Probation (Tightened)** | Every lot at Tightened/II | 5 consecutive non-conforming lots → supply suspension | 5 consecutive conforming lots → Normal |\n| **New Supplier** | First 3 lots: 100% inspection + full-panel testing | — | — |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Fabric Incoming Inspection Process\n\n### 3.1 Receiving & Preliminary Verification\n\n1. **Document verification**: Cross-check the packing list against the PO for item name, color, quantity, and lot number (Lot#)\n2. **Outer packaging inspection**: Record and photograph any shipping damage (water stains, breakage, compression marks)\n3. **Roll label information**: Verify that each roll's fabric number, color code, dye-lot number, roll length, and GSM markings match the supplier's inspection report\n\n### 3.2 Fabric Appearance Inspection (Four-Point System)\n\nInspect per the ASTM D5430 Four-Point System:\n\n- **Sampling ratio**:\n  - Class A fabrics / Tightened suppliers: ≥ 30% of rolls\n  - Normal suppliers: ≥ 15% of rolls\n  - Skip-Lot suppliers: ≥ 5% of rolls (or per skip-lot frequency)\n- **Inspection speed**: Inspection machine speed ≤ 15 yards/min (dark / fine fabrics ≤ 10 yards/min)\n- **Acceptance criteria**: Average defect points per 100 linear yards ≤ customer-specified limit (typically ≤ 28 or ≤ 40 points/100 yards)\n- **Documentation**: Defect location, type, and penalty points for each roll are recorded in the inspection report and archived as scanned copies\n\n### 3.3 Fabric Physical / Chemical Testing\n\n| Test Item | Test Method | Frequency | Applicable Conditions |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| GSM (fabric weight) | ASTM D3776 | Every lot | All fabrics |\n| Color difference (vs. approved sample) | AATCC 173 / Instrumental ΔE | Every dye lot | Dyed fabrics |\n| Shrinkage | AATCC 135 / 150 | Every lot (every dye lot for new suppliers) | All fabrics |\n| Colorfastness (washing, crocking) | AATCC 61 / 8 | Every lot | Dyed fabrics |\n| Tear strength | ASTM D1424 | Every lot | Woven fabrics |\n| Bursting strength | ASTM D3786 | Every lot | Knit fabrics |\n| Formaldehyde content | AATCC 112 | First lot + annual retest | All fabrics |\n| Lead content | CPSC-CH-E1001 | First lot + annual retest | Children's product fabrics |\n| Flammability | 16 CFR 1610 | First lot + annual retest | Clothing fabrics |\n| Water repellency | AATCC 22 | Every lot | Functional water-repellent fabrics |\n| pH | AATCC 81 | First lot + annual retest | All fabrics |\n\n**Notes**:\n- \"First lot\" refers to the first incoming shipment of a given fabric variety (item name + composition + supplier);\n- Supplier-provided test reports may serve as reference but cannot substitute for brand self-testing — unless the supplier's laboratory has passed MSA correlation with kappa ≥ 0.80 (refer to [PB-MSA](doc:PB-MSA)).\n\n### 3.4 Special Inspection Items for Trims & Accessories\n\n| Trim Type | Inspection Focus | Reference Case |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Fusible interlining** | Bond peel strength (180° peel ≥ customer minimum), shrinkage, compatibility with shell fabric | [RM-005](case:RM-005) |\n| **Zippers** | Smooth operation, lateral strength, slider tension, color matching | — |\n| **Buttons** | Pull strength test (≥ 15 lbs, ASTM D7842), color/gloss matching, small-parts test for children's products | — |\n| **Elastic bands** | Stretch recovery rate, elastic retention after multiple washes, width tolerance | — |\n| **Sewing thread** | Thread size/color confirmation, breaking strength, shrinkage | — |\n| **Labels/hang tags** | Content accuracy (RN number, fiber composition, country of origin, care instructions), adhesion fastness | — |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Non-Conforming Material Disposition\n\n### 4.1 Disposition Levels\n\n| Level | Definition | Action |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Accept** | All inspection items conforming | Release to warehouse for production use |\n| **Conditional Accept** | Minor deviation that does not affect finished-product quality (e.g., color difference ΔE at the borderline) | QA Manager approval + deviation record + increased monitoring |\n| **Reject** | Any critical item non-conforming | Quarantine + supplier 8D + return/replacement |\n| **Hold** | Supplementary testing required before disposition | Quarantine pending results; must not proceed to production |\n\n### 4.2 Non-Conformance Handling Process\n\n1. **Quarantine**: Non-conforming material is physically isolated in the warehouse and marked with a red tag; status locked in WMS\n2. **Supplier notification**: Issue a Non-Conformance Report (NCR) within 24 hours, accompanied by inspection report and photographs\n3. **8D requirement**: Major / Critical non-conformances require the supplier to submit an 8D report within the stipulated timeframe (refer to [SYS-011](case:SYS-011) SLA requirements)\n4. **Return/replacement**: Rejected lots are arranged for return or replacement within 5 business days\n5. **Record keeping**: Non-conformance records are entered into the supplier quality file and factored into quality tier evaluations\n\n---\n\n## 5. Records & Data Management\n\n### 5.1 Mandatory Records\n\n| Record | Retention Period | Format |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Fabric inspection report (Four-Point System scores) | 3 years | Electronic + scanned copies |\n| Laboratory test reports | 5 years | Electronic |\n| Non-Conformance Reports (NCR) | 5 years | Electronic |\n| Conditional acceptance approval records | 3 years | Electronic |\n| Fabric color swatches / approved samples | Current season + 1 year | Physical |\n\n### 5.2 Data Utilization\n\n- Monthly incoming pass rates are summarized and incorporated into the supplier quality scorecard\n- Pareto analysis of non-conformance patterns (failure type x supplier x material) is presented at quarterly reviews\n- Four-Point System defect trends are monitored via u-chart (refer to [PB-SPC](doc:PB-SPC))\n"},"SOP-PPM":{"title":"Pre-Production Meeting Standard Operating Procedure","content":"# Pre-Production Meeting (PPM) Standard Operating Procedure\n\n> **Pre-Production Meeting (PPM) SOP**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Purpose & Scope\n\n### 1.1 Purpose\n\nStandardize the timing, required participants, standard agenda, red-flag hold rules, and action-item tracking mechanisms for pre-production meetings, transforming PPMs from a formality into a critical risk-interception gate before mass production.\n\n### 1.2 Scope\n\nA pre-production meeting must be held for all brand POs before cutting. The following exemptions apply:\n- Repeat orders (not first orders) for the same factory x same style x same fabrics and trims, where the previous order passed FTR → may use a simplified PPM (confirm change points only)\n\n### 1.3 Referenced Documents\n\n- [APP-012](case:APP-012) — Pre-Production Meeting Standardization Case\n- [SOP-DFQ](doc:SOP-DFQ) — Design-Stage Quality Front-Loading SOP\n- [SOP-IQC](doc:SOP-IQC) — Incoming Quality Control SOP for Fabrics, Trims & Accessories\n- [PB-FMEA](doc:PB-FMEA) — FMEA Methodology\n\n---\n\n## 2. PPM Timing\n\n| Event | PPM Timing |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| New style, first order | After all fabrics and trims are on hand + pre-production sample approved, **≥ 3 business days before cutting** |\n| Repeat order (with changes) | After change points are confirmed, **≥ 2 business days before cutting** |\n| Repeat order (no changes, exempted) | Simplified confirmation (email or phone call); no formal meeting required |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Required Participants\n\n### 3.1 Factory Side (Must Attend in Person)\n\n| Role | Responsibility | If Absent |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Quality Manager (Chairperson) | Chair the meeting, record conclusions | Cannot be absent |\n| Production Planning Supervisor | Confirm production schedule and delivery feasibility | Deputy may attend |\n| Cutting Room Supervisor | Confirm fabric spreading and marker plan, fabric consumption | Cannot be absent |\n| Sewing Floor Supervisor | Confirm technical difficulties and sewing sequence | Cannot be absent |\n| Special Process Supervisor | Printing / embroidery / washing / fusing, etc. (as applicable) | Cannot be absent when special processes apply |\n\n### 3.2 Brand Side\n\n| Role | Responsibility |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **QA Engineer** (not a junior QC) | Risk assessment, red-flag determination, QCP linkage |\n| Merchandiser (may attend remotely) | Delivery confirmation, commercial issue coordination |\n\n---\n\n## 4. Standard Agenda & Mandatory Checklist\n\n### 4.1 Eight-Point Mandatory Checklist\n\n| No. | Agenda Item | Inspection Content | Red-Flag Rule (any trigger → hold cutting) |\n| :---: | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | **Fabric confirmation** | Compare bulk fabric against approved sample (color difference, hand feel, GSM, shrinkage) | ΔE (CMC 2:1, D65/10°) > 1.0, or GSM deviation > 5%, or shrinkage out of tolerance |\n| 2 | **Trims confirmation** | Physical verification of fusible interlining, zippers, buttons, elastic bands, etc. | Any trim not received or not matching approved sample |\n| 3 | **Fabric-trim compatibility** | Fusing press trial for interlining, zipper color difference, elastic shrinkage matching | Peel strength < minimum value, or shrinkage rate difference > 2% |\n| 4 | **Dimensions & fit** | Confirm latest Tech Pack version, grading table cross-check, key measurements of pre-production sample | Pre-production sample key measurement deviation > 1 cm |\n| 5 | **Special processes** | Confirm printing / embroidery / washing parameters, pilot-run sample confirmation | No pilot-run sample available or pilot-run sample not approved |\n| 6 | **Regulatory compliance** | Label content, test plan/status, CPC requirements (children's products) | Critical tests incomplete or label errors |\n| 7 | **Packaging** | Folding method, hang-tag placement, shipping-mark specifications, barcodes | Not confirmed |\n| 8 | **Lessons learned** | Query the case library: historical quality issues for the same factory / same category / same fabric | Corrective actions for similar issues not verified |\n\n### 4.2 Red-Flag Hold Mechanism\n\n- Any red-flag rule triggered → PPM conclusion is **hold cutting**\n- The QA Engineer has the authority to issue a hold order without Director approval\n- Hold reason, expected resolution date, and responsible person are recorded in the system\n- Cutting may proceed only after the QA Engineer signs off on red-flag clearance\n\n---\n\n## 5. Action-Item Tracking\n\n### 5.1 Action-Item Recording\n\nEach action item arising from the PPM is entered into the quality management system:\n- **Description**: Specific issue and what needs to be done\n- **Owner**: Individual name (not department)\n- **Due date**: Must be before cutting date\n- **Status**: Pending / In progress / Closed\n\n### 5.2 Automated Reminders\n\n- **3 days** before cutting date: System automatically reminds all owners of open action items\n- **1 day** before cutting date: If open action items remain → automatic notification to QA Manager\n- **Cutting date**: If red-flag-level action items remain open → **automatic hold trigger**\n\n---\n\n## 6. QCP Linkage\n\nRisk findings from the PPM are written directly into the Quality Control Plan (QCP) for that PO:\n\n| PPM Finding | QCP Action |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Fabric-trim compatibility risk | Add first-piece fusing confirmation checkpoint |\n| High-risk special process | Change corresponding operation from sampling to 100% inspection or increase inspection frequency |\n| Fabric borderline pass on Four-Point System | Add fabric re-inspection checkpoint at cutting |\n| Lessons-learned related | Add targeted inspection for historical issue points |\n| New supplier / new fabric | Increase in-line inspection frequency |\n\nRefer to [playbooks/control-plan.md](doc:PB-QCP) for QCP development methodology.\n\n---\n\n## 7. PPM Record Template\n\n```\nPO #: ___________    Style #: ___________    Factory: ___________\nPPM Date: ___________    Planned Cutting Date: ___________\n\nAttendees:\n  Brand: ___________\n  Factory: ___________\n\n1. Fabric confirmation:       □ Pass  □ Red Flag: ___________\n2. Trims confirmation:        □ Pass  □ Red Flag: ___________\n3. Fabric-trim compatibility: □ Pass  □ Red Flag: ___________\n4. Dimensions & fit:          □ Pass  □ Red Flag: ___________\n5. Special processes:         □ Pass  □ Red Flag: ___________  □ N/A\n6. Regulatory compliance:     □ Pass  □ Red Flag: ___________\n7. Packaging:                 □ Pass  □ Red Flag: ___________\n8. Lessons learned:           □ No relevant records  □ Records found; corrective actions verified\n\nConclusion:  □ Proceed to cutting  □ Hold cutting (Reason: ___________)\n\nAction Items:\n  1. ___________  Owner: _____  Due: _____\n  2. ___________  Owner: _____  Due: _____\n\nSignatures:\n  Brand QA: ___________\n  Factory Quality Manager: ___________\n```\n"},"SOP-RCL":{"title":"Product Recall and Market Corrective Action Standard Operating Procedure","content":"# Product Recall & Market Corrective Action Standard Operating Procedure\n\n> **Product Recall & Market Corrective Action SOP**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Purpose & Scope\n\n### 1.1 Purpose\n\nStandardize the brand's response process when a product safety risk, regulatory non-compliance, or serious quality defect is identified or reasonably suspected, ensuring that:\n- Consumer safety is the top priority\n- CPSA Sec. 15(b) reporting obligations are fulfilled\n- Traceability, quarantine, and notification are completed within controlled timeframes\n- Drills verify the executability of the process\n\n### 1.2 Scope\n\nAll brand-owned and contract-manufactured products, covering adult apparel, children's apparel, and home textiles.\n\n### 1.3 Referenced Documents\n\n- [SYS-012](case:SYS-012) — Product Recall Simulation Drill Case\n- CPSA Sec. 15(b) — Obligation to Report Substantial Product Hazards\n- CPSA Sec. 20 — Late-Reporting Penalties\n- 16 CFR 1115 — Reporting Procedures for Substantial Product Hazards\n- 16 CFR 1120 — Drawstring Safety (Children's Outerwear)\n- CPSC \"Recall Handbook\"\n- [SOP-IQC](doc:SOP-IQC) — Lot Traceability Requirements in Incoming Inspection\n\n---\n\n## 2. Response Levels\n\n| Level | Definition | Response Timeframe | Decision Authority |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Level 1: Safety Recall** | Confirmed or reasonably suspected substantial product hazard (CPSA Sec. 15(b)) | Activate response team **within 4 hours** | VP-level approval |\n| **Level 2: Voluntary Correction** | Regulatory non-compliance not constituting a safety hazard (label errors, fiber content marking discrepancies, etc.) | Initiate **within 24 hours** | QA Director approval |\n| **Level 3: Quality Recall** | Serious quality issue causing widespread returns or brand reputation risk; no safety concern | Initiate **within 48 hours** | QA Director and Sourcing VP jointly |\n| **Level 4: Monitor** | Signal is unclear; further investigation needed before escalation or closure | Complete preliminary assessment **within 5 business days** | QA Manager |\n\n**Grading Principles**:\n- Safety signals involving children's products default to Level 1 assessment\n- When it is uncertain whether a \"substantial product hazard\" exists, Legal participates in the assessment — under the \"report upon awareness\" principle, reasonable suspicion warrants contacting CPSC within 24 hours\n- The penalty risk for late reporting (CPSA Sec. 20) far exceeds the cost of a false report\n\n---\n\n## 3. Recall Response Team (RCRT)\n\n### 3.1 Composition\n\n| Role | Responsibility | Primary Contact | Alternate Contact |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **QA Director (Convener)** | Initiate assessment, organize response, coordinate internal and external parties | [Name / Mobile] | [Name / Mobile] |\n| **Legal Representative** | CPSC report assessment and drafting, legal risk analysis | [Name / Mobile] | [Name / Mobile] |\n| **Supply Chain Representative** | Lot traceability, supplier communication, inventory quarantine | [Name / Mobile] | [Name / Mobile] |\n| **Retail / E-commerce Operations** | Stop-sale execution, channel inventory verification, consumer return arrangements | [Name / Mobile] | [Name / Mobile] |\n| **PR / Brand** | Consumer notifications, media statements, social media monitoring | [Name / Mobile] | [Name / Mobile] |\n| **Finance** | Cost estimation, insurance claims, supplier chargebacks | [Name / Mobile] | [Name / Mobile] |\n| **IT** | System operations support (WMS lockdown, e-commerce stop-sale, data queries) | [Name / Mobile] | [Name / Mobile] |\n\n### 3.2 Contact List Maintenance\n\n- **Frequency**: Updated quarterly\n- **Verification**: Confirm each mobile number is active upon update (send text message confirmation)\n- **Departure / transfer**: HR notifies QA Director; alternate contact updated within 5 business days\n\n---\n\n## 4. Response Process\n\n### 4.1 Trigger & Preliminary Assessment (0–4 Hours)\n\n| Step | Action | Owner | Timeframe |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| 1 | Safety signal received (test failure / consumer injury report / retailer notification / media coverage / CPSC notice) | Anyone | — |\n| 2 | Notify QA Manager with: product name, SKU, lot (if available), issue description, signal source | Discoverer | Immediately |\n| 3 | QA Manager performs preliminary grading (Level 1–4) | QA Manager | Within 2 hours |\n| 4 | Level 1/2/3: Convene RCRT and send activation email | QA Director | Within 4 hours |\n| 5 | Level 4: Record and initiate investigation; assessment conclusion within 5 business days | QA Manager | 5 days |\n\n### 4.2 Traceability & Scope Determination (4–24 Hours)\n\n1. **Product traceability**: Starting from the affected product's SKU + lot number (Lot#), query WMS for:\n   - PO number for the lot → supplier / factory\n   - Warehouse receipt date and quantity\n   - Shipped channels and quantities (e-commerce / retail / wholesale)\n   - Remaining warehouse inventory and location\n2. **Upstream traceability** (if needed): Contact the factory to trace back to fabric/trim lot, production date, and other POs from the same production lot\n3. **Scope confirmation**: Determine the list of affected lots, total quantity, and distribution across channels\n\n**Target**: Traceability turnaround time ≤ 24 hours (refer to [SYS-012](case:SYS-012); drill benchmark is 8 hours)\n\n### 4.3 Product Quarantine (Concurrent with Traceability)\n\n| Stage | Action | Timeframe |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| Warehouse | Lock affected lots in WMS + physically isolate to designated area | 2 hours after lot identification |\n| In transit | Contact logistics to intercept shipments not yet delivered to warehouse / stores | 4 hours after lot identification |\n| Retail stores | Notify stores to pull affected lots from shelves | 8 hours after lot identification |\n| E-commerce | Stop sale of affected SKUs (if lot-level differentiation is not possible, suspend the entire SKU) | 2 hours after lot identification |\n\n### 4.4 Regulatory Reporting (Level 1)\n\n1. **CPSC report assessment**: Legal evaluates per the CPSC \"Recall Handbook\" whether the issue constitutes a \"substantial product hazard\"\n2. **Initial report**: If reporting is warranted, submit an initial report via the CPSC saferproducts.gov online system or by phone to the CPSC Compliance Officer\n3. **Timeframe**: 16 CFR 1115.13(c) requires \"immediate\" reporting — in practice, contacting CPSC within 24 hours is a reasonable timeframe\n4. **Content**: Product description, known risk, affected quantity, actions taken, proposed corrective action\n5. **Follow-up**: Negotiate the specific recall remedy with CPSC (refund / replacement / repair / notification)\n\n### 4.5 Consumer Notification & Correction\n\n| Channel | Method | Content |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| E-commerce purchasers | Email + order page notification | Product identification, risk description, refund/replacement instructions |\n| Retail purchasers | In-store posting + brand website announcement | Same as above |\n| Social media | Brand official account post | Concise statement + link to website details |\n| CPSC website | CPSC publishes recall notice (if applicable) | CPSC format |\n\n### 4.6 Root Cause Analysis & Corrective Action\n\n- Supplier submits 8D report within the stipulated timeframe (refer to [SYS-011](case:SYS-011) SLA)\n- QA verifies corrective-action effectiveness (pilot-run verification + test confirmation)\n- Update the FMEA (refer to [PB-FMEA](doc:PB-FMEA)) for severity and detection ratings of the relevant risk item\n\n---\n\n## 5. Annual Simulation Drills\n\n### 5.1 Frequency\n\n- ≥ 1 full-process drill per year (tabletop exercise + hands-on execution)\n- ≥ 1 tabletop exercise per year (testing communication chain and decision speed only)\n\n### 5.2 Scenario Design\n\n- The QA Director designs the scenario; participants are told only \"a drill will take place this week\" without specifics\n- Scenarios cover different levels (Level 1 and Level 2/3 alternate)\n- Example scenarios: children's sleepwear flammability failure (16 CFR 1615/1616), bedding formaldehyde exceedance, metal foreign-object injury, drawstring violation (16 CFR 1120)\n\n### 5.3 Evaluation Dimensions\n\n| Dimension | Target |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| Traceability turnaround time | ≤ 24 hours |\n| Communication chain integrity | Zero breakage (alternate contact mechanism effective) |\n| Grading accuracy | Correctly graded; no downgrading |\n| Quarantine execution time | Warehouse ≤ 2 hours; e-commerce ≤ 15 minutes |\n| Regulatory report draft | Legal ≤ 4 hours to complete draft |\n\n### 5.4 Post-Drill Review & Improvement\n\nA post-drill review report must be completed within 5 business days, identifying gaps and entering them into the improvement plan. All gaps must be closed before the next drill.\n"},"REF-CPSIA":{"title":"CPSIA and Flammability Regulation Quick Reference Guide","content":"# CPSIA & Flammability Regulation Quick Reference Guide\n\n> **Quick Reference: CPSIA, Flammability & Key US Federal Textile Regulations**\n\n> This guide is a working reference and does not constitute legal advice. Regulatory entries use `registry/regulations.json` as the single source of truth; in case of conflict, the registry prevails.\n\n---\n\n## 1. CPSIA (Consumer Product Safety Improvement Act of 2008)\n\n### 1.1 Key Provisions Quick Reference\n\n| Provision | Content | Applicable Scope | Limit / Requirement |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Sec. 101** | Lead content in children's products (substrate) | Products intended for children age 12 and under | **≤ 100 ppm** (substrate lead) |\n| **Sec. 101(f)** | Lead in surface coatings | Consumer product coatings / surface paint | **≤ 90 ppm** |\n| **Sec. 108** | Phthalates | **Children's toys and child care articles** (16 CFR 1307); does not apply to general children's apparel | DEHP, DBP, BBP, DINP, DIBP, DPENP, DHEXP, DCHP: all **≤ 1,000 ppm** |\n| **Sec. 102** | Mandatory third-party testing | Children's products subject to CPSC consumer product safety rules or bans | Testing by a CPSC-accepted laboratory |\n| **Sec. 103** | Tracking labels | Children's products | Permanent label with traceable information including manufacturer / private labeler name, production location, production date, lot number, etc. |\n| **Sec. 104(b)** | Durable infant product standards | Cribs, high chairs, play yards, etc. | Must comply with mandatory ASTM / CPSC standards |\n\n### 1.2 Children's Product Certificate (CPC)\n\nAll imported or domestically manufactured children's products (age 12 and under) must be accompanied by a CPC containing:\n1. List of applicable CPSC consumer product safety rules\n2. Test report number from a CPSC-accepted laboratory\n3. Name and contact information of the importer / manufacturer / private labeler\n4. Test date and testing laboratory information\n5. Product identification information (corresponding to the tracking label)\n\n**Note**: The CPC is a **declaration by the manufacturer / importer**, not a certificate issued by the laboratory.\n\n### 1.3 Tracking Labels (Sec. 103)\n\nChildren's products must bear a permanent label (printed, stamped, stitched, or otherwise affixed in a manner not easily removed) containing sufficient information to trace back to the manufacturer, production lot, and production date. Common practices in textile apparel:\n- Print lot number or date code on the main label or care label\n- Lot number encoding rules must be documented on file for decoding during traceability\n\nLinked to WMS lot traceability in [SYS-012](case:SYS-012): the lot number on the finished-product tracking label should match the WMS receiving lot number.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Flammability Regulations\n\n### 2.1 16 CFR 1610 — General Wearing Apparel\n\n| Item | Content |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Applicable scope** | Adult and children's clothing fabrics (excluding hats, gloves, footwear) |\n| **Test method** | 45° angle flame test, 1-second ignition |\n| **Classification** | Class 1 (normal flammability) — pass; Class 2 (intermediate flammability) — raised-surface textiles only, requires warning label; Class 3 (rapid and intense burning) — **prohibited** |\n| **Frequency** | Mandatory testing on first lot of fabric; retest upon fabric composition / construction change |\n\nMost textile fabrics are Class 1, but lightweight raised-surface fabrics (e.g., fleece, flannel, sheer fabrics) may reach Class 3 and require special attention.\n\n### 2.2 16 CFR 1615/1616 — Children's Sleepwear\n\n| Item | Content |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Applicable scope** | Children's sleepwear (1615: sizes 0–6X; 1616: sizes 7–14) |\n| **Exemption** | Tight-fitting sleepwear for ages 9 months–14 years is exempt from flammability testing provided dimensions fall within the prescribed size range |\n| **Test method** | Vertical flame test, 3-second ignition, measure char length |\n| **Acceptance criteria** | Average char length of 5 specimens ≤ 7 inches, and each individual specimen **< 10 inches** (10 inches = full-length char = fail) |\n| **After-laundering test** | Must still pass after 50 wash cycles |\n| **Tight-fitting exemption conditions** | Must not contain flammable fabrics (e.g., polyester raised-surface), dimensions must fall within the CPSC-published tight-fitting size chart |\n\n### 2.3 16 CFR 1632 — Mattresses and Mattress Pads\n\n| Item | Content |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Applicable scope** | Mattresses, mattress pads |\n| **Test method** | Cigarette Ignition Test |\n| **Acceptance criteria** | Char length after cigarette ignition must not exceed 2 inches |\n\n### 2.4 16 CFR 1633 — Mattress Open-Flame Test\n\n| Item | Content |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| **Applicable scope** | Full-size mattresses |\n| **Test method** | Open-flame ignition (simulating bedding fire) |\n| **Acceptance criteria** | Peak heat release rate ≤ 200 kW; total heat release over 30 minutes ≤ 15 MJ |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Other Key Federal Regulations Quick Reference\n\n### 3.1 Fiber Content Labeling (Textile Fiber Products Identification Act / 16 CFR 303)\n\n- All textile products must be labeled with fiber names and percentages (listed in descending order by weight)\n- Fiber content tolerance: ±3% (16 CFR 303.43)\n- Decorative fiber ≤ 5% may be noted as \"exclusive of decoration\"\n- Elastic fiber (e.g., spandex) must be labeled regardless of content percentage\n\n### 3.2 Care Labeling (Care Labeling Rule / 16 CFR 423)\n\n- Apparel must have a care label specifying at least one reasonable care method\n- The labeled care method must have a reasonable basis (may be testing, technical literature, or industry knowledge) — the brand is responsible for the accuracy of care labels\n- Symbols (ASTM D5489) or text may be used; however, symbol-only labels may require supplementary explanation\n\n### 3.3 Country of Origin Marking (Made in USA / FTC Enforcement Policy)\n\n- \"Made in USA\": \"all or virtually all\" of the manufacturing process is completed in the United States\n- Imported products must be marked with the country of origin (19 CFR 134 — Customs regulation, not FTC)\n- Qualified claims such as \"Assembled in USA from imported materials\" have specific FTC compliance requirements\n\n### 3.4 Drawstring Safety (16 CFR 1120 — Drawstrings)\n\n- Hood and neck area drawstrings are **prohibited** on children's upper outerwear in sizes **2T–12**\n- Bottom and hem drawstrings on children's outerwear in sizes **2T–16** must not extend below the garment's bottom edge\n- Refer to ASTM F1816 standard\n- The simulation drill scenario in [SYS-012](case:SYS-012) involves drawstring risk\n\n### 3.5 PFAS Restrictions (California AB 1817 — State Law, Not Federal)\n\n- Effective January 1, 2025, California prohibits the intentional addition of PFAS in textile articles\n- Applies to apparel, accessories, handbags, backpacks, curtains, upholstery fabrics, etc.\n- Refer to the C0 DWR alternative case in [RM-008](case:RM-008)\n\n---\n\n## 4. Recommended Testing Frequency Matrix\n\n| Test Item | First Lot / New Product | Routine per Lot | Annual Retest | Notes |\n| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |\n| Lead content (substrate + coating) | Required | — | Required | Children's products |\n| Phthalates | Required | — | Required | Children's products |\n| Flammability (16 CFR 1610) | Required | — | Upon fabric change | All apparel |\n| Flammability (16 CFR 1615/1616) | Required | — | Upon fabric change | Children's sleepwear |\n| Fiber content | Required | — | Upon supplier change | All textiles |\n| Colorfastness | Required | Every lot | — | Dyed fabrics |\n| Shrinkage | Required | Every lot | — | All fabrics |\n| Formaldehyde | Required | — | Required | Not a federal requirement; executed per customer / retailer specifications |\n| PFAS (California market) | Required | — | Required | AB 1817 applies to all textile articles, not limited to water-repellent products |\n| Physical performance (strength, GSM, etc.) | Required | Every lot | — | Per product type |\n"}};
